{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"ab5a02f3fdfb4bf6e","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_015o3iFZ5e3gSfqc22BuBmPo","status":"completed","output_file":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-agent-jhH7E9/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-kk7Dev/e3091878-9949-4689-ad83-c0c41e6641a2/tasks/ab5a02f3fdfb4bf6e.output","summary":"Below is the complete enumeration. All line numbers are for `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-kk7Dev/c_src/src/pngrtran.c` unless another file is named.\n\n---\n\n# 0. Universal gate: `png_rtran_ok` (pngrtran.c:114-136)\n\nEvery read-transform setter except `png_set_crc_action`, `png_set_read_user_transform_fn`, and the pngtrans.c setters (`bgr/swap/packing/packswap/shift/filler/add_alpha/swap_alpha/invert_alpha/invert_mono`) funnels through this:\n\n- `png_ptr == NULL` -> returns 0, no-op (silent).\n- `flags & PNG_FLAG_ROW_INIT` set (i.e. after `png_start_read_image`/`png_read_update_info`) -> `png_app_error(\"invalid after png_start_read_image or png_read_update_info\")`, returns 0.\n- `need_IHDR != 0 && !(mode & PNG_HAVE_IHDR)` -> `png_app_error(\"invalid before the PNG header has been read\")`, returns 0. **Only `png_set_rgb_to_gray_fixed` passes `need_IHDR=1`** (pngrtran.c:1054). Everything else passes 0.\n- Success -> sets `PNG_FLAG_DETECT_UNINITIALIZED` (0x4000) and returns 1. That flag is what makes `png_do_read_transformations` (pngrtran.c:4900) `png_error(\"Uninitialized row\")` if the app never called start_image/update_info.\n\nSo there are 3 distinct config axes per setter: called-with-NULL, called-too-late, called-before-IHDR (rgb_to_gray only), plus the legal path.\n\n---\n\n# 1. Setters, branch by branch\n\n## 1.1 `png_set_background_fixed` (pngrtran.c:141-168) / `png_set_background` (171-178)\n\nFloat wrapper converts via `png_fixed(...,\"png_set_background\")` — note this uses `png_fixed`, **not** `convert_gamma_value`, so a fixed-point-scaled value passed to the float API is NOT auto-detected here (unlike set_gamma/alpha_mode).\n\nBranches:\n1. `png_rtran_ok(png_ptr,0)==0` **or `background_color == NULL`** -> silent no-op (line 148).\n2. `background_gamma_code == PNG_BACKGROUND_GAMMA_UNKNOWN` (0) -> `png_warning(\"Application must supply a known background gamma\")` and return. This is the warn-and-ignore case.\n3. Otherwise, unconditionally (no colour-type / bit-depth check at all here):\n   - `transformations |= PNG_COMPOSE (0x80) | PNG_STRIP_ALPHA (0x40000)` — **note it always sets STRIP_ALPHA** (line 157).\n   - `transformations &= ~PNG_ENCODE_ALPHA`; `flags &= ~PNG_FLAG_OPTIMIZE_ALPHA` (undoes a prior `png_set_alpha_mode(OPTIMIZED/BROKEN)`).\n   - `background = *background_color` (whole `png_color_16`, including `.index` which is only meaningful for palette + need_expand).\n   - `background_gamma = background_gamma`, `background_gamma_type = code`.\n4. `need_expand != 0` -> `|= PNG_BACKGROUND_EXPAND (0x100)`; else `&= ~PNG_BACKGROUND_EXPAND`.\n\nMatrix rows: 3 gamma codes (SCREEN=1, FILE=2, UNIQUE=3) x need_expand {0,1} = 6, plus UNKNOWN=0 warn row, plus NULL-colour row. Note code 4+ (\"invalid background gamma type\") is *not* rejected here — it becomes `png_error(\"invalid background gamma type\")` later in `png_init_read_transformations` (line 1886) in the non-palette gamma path, but silently defaults to `g=gs=PNG_FP_1` in the *palette* path (line 1742-1745). That asymmetry is a real test row.\n\nOrdering hazard worth a row: calling `png_set_alpha_mode(ASSOCIATED/OPTIMIZED/BROKEN)` *after* `png_set_background` -> `png_error(\"conflicting calls to set alpha mode and background\")` (line 452). The reverse order (background after alpha_mode) does *not* error; it just overwrites the background and clears ENCODE_ALPHA/OPTIMIZE_ALPHA.\n\n## 1.2 `png_set_alpha_mode_fixed` (pngrtran.c:360-457) / `png_set_alpha_mode` (460-465)\n\nFloat wrapper uses `convert_gamma_value` (308-328): if `0 < g < 128` it multiplies by PNG_FP_1; `floor(g+.5)`; out of `[PNG_FP_MIN, PNG_FP_MAX]` -> `png_fixed_error(\"gamma value\")`. Preserves -1/-2 exactly.\n\nPre-switch branches:\n1. `png_rtran_ok(png_ptr,0)==0` -> no-op.\n2. `translate_gamma_flags(output_gamma, is_screen=1)` (278-305): `PNG_DEFAULT_sRGB (-1)` or `PNG_FP_1/-1 (=-100000)` -> `PNG_GAMMA_sRGB (220000)`; `PNG_GAMMA_MAC_18 (-2)` or `PNG_FP_1/-2 (=-50000)` -> `PNG_GAMMA_MAC_OLD (151724)`. (With `is_screen=0` they'd map to 45455 / 65909; that path only used by set_gamma's file_gamma.)\n3. `unsupported_gamma(png_ptr, output_gamma, warn=0)` (331-356): outside `[PNG_LIB_GAMMA_MIN=1000, PNG_LIB_GAMMA_MAX=10000000]` -> **`png_app_error`** (\"gamma out of supported range\") and return. Distinct from set_gamma which only warns.\n4. `file_gamma` defaulting: if `png_ptr->default_gamma == 0`, `default_gamma = file_gamma = png_reciprocal(output_gamma)`; otherwise the pre-existing `default_gamma` (e.g. set by the simplified API) is reused. Two distinct rows.\n\nMode switch (line 404):\n- `PNG_ALPHA_PNG` (0): `compose=0`; clears `PNG_ENCODE_ALPHA`, clears `PNG_FLAG_OPTIMIZE_ALPHA`. `screen_gamma = output_gamma`. No COMPOSE. This is the \"just set a default gamma\" usage that is legal alongside `png_set_background`.\n- `PNG_ALPHA_STANDARD`/`ASSOCIATED`/`PREMULTIPLIED` (all == 1): `compose=1`; clears ENCODE_ALPHA and OPTIMIZE_ALPHA; **overrides `output_gamma = PNG_FP_1`** (linear output) — so the caller's gamma argument is discarded for screen_gamma but was already used to default `default_gamma`.\n- `PNG_ALPHA_OPTIMIZED` (2): `compose=1`; clears ENCODE_ALPHA; **sets `PNG_FLAG_OPTIMIZE_ALPHA`**; keeps output_gamma (encoding of opaque pixels).\n- `PNG_ALPHA_BROKEN` (3): `compose=1`; **sets `PNG_ENCODE_ALPHA`**; clears OPTIMIZE_ALPHA; keeps output_gamma.\n- default: `png_error(\"invalid alpha mode\")`.\n\nThen `screen_gamma = output_gamma` (438) always.\n\nIf `compose != 0` (443-456):\n- `memset(background, 0, ...)` (composite on black),\n- `background_gamma = file_gamma`, `background_gamma_type = PNG_BACKGROUND_GAMMA_FILE`,\n- `&= ~PNG_BACKGROUND_EXPAND`,\n- if `PNG_COMPOSE` already set -> `png_error(\"conflicting calls to set alpha mode and background\")`,\n- `|= PNG_COMPOSE`.\n\nNo colour-type or bit-depth precondition. Note: unlike set_background it does **not** set STRIP_ALPHA, and it does not touch `file_gamma`/`chunk_gamma`.\n\n## 1.3 `png_set_gamma_fixed` (pngrtran.c:892-931) / `png_set_gamma` (934-939)\n\nFloat wrapper uses `convert_gamma_value` for *both* args.\n1. `png_rtran_ok(,0)==0` -> no-op.\n2. `scrn_gamma = translate_gamma_flags(scrn, 1)`; `file_gamma = translate_gamma_flags(file, 0)` — note the *file* mapping gives 45455 (1/2.2) for -1 and 65909 for -2.\n3. `file_gamma <= 0` -> `png_app_error(\"invalid file gamma in png_set_gamma\")` (continues!).\n4. `scrn_gamma <= 0` -> `png_app_error(\"invalid screen gamma in png_set_gamma\")` (continues!).\n5. `unsupported_gamma(file, warn=1) || unsupported_gamma(scrn, warn=1)` -> `png_app_warning` and **return without setting anything**.\n6. Success: sets `png_ptr->file_gamma` and `png_ptr->screen_gamma` only. It does NOT set `PNG_GAMMA` in transformations — that is decided later in `png_init_gamma_values`/`png_init_read_transformations`.\n\nRows: legal, file<=0, scrn<=0, both flags (-1/-2), out-of-range warn-and-ignore, called after row init.\n\n## 1.4 `png_set_strip_16` (pngrtran.c:201-210)\nSets `PNG_16_TO_8 (0x400)`. No colour/bit-depth check. If both scale and strip are set, **scale wins** at row time (do_scale runs first at 5058 and drops bit_depth to 8, so `png_do_chop` at 5067 is a no-op) — but `png_read_transform_info` sets bit_depth=8 for either.\n\n## 1.5 `png_set_scale_16` (pngrtran.c:187-196)\nSets `PNG_SCALE_16_TO_8 (0x4000000)`. No checks.\n\n## 1.6 `png_set_strip_alpha` (pngrtran.c:214-223)\nSets `PNG_STRIP_ALPHA (0x40000)`. No checks. Behaviour splits later on whether COMPOSE is also set (see §2).\n\n## 1.7 `png_set_quantize` (pngrtran.c:488-888)\n\n1. `png_rtran_ok(,0)==0` -> no-op. 2. `palette == NULL` -> silent no-op (line 498).\n3. Sets `PNG_QUANTIZE (0x40)` unconditionally after that.\n4. `full_quantize == 0` (503-522): frees any existing `quantize_index`, mallocs `PNG_MAX_PALETTE_LENGTH` (256, deliberately not `num_palette` — hardening against out-of-range indices), identity-fills it. `full_quantize != 0` -> no quantize_index (so `png_do_quantize`'s PALETTE branch will be skipped since `quantize_lookup==NULL`).\n5. `num_palette > maximum_colors` (524-815) — two sub-paths:\n   - **histogram != NULL** (526-646): bubble-sorts `quantize_sort` by descending histogram count, stopping early if `done`. Then:\n     - `full_quantize != 0` (572-590): moves useful colours below `maximum_colors` in `palette[]` only; no translation table.\n     - `full_quantize == 0` (591-644): swaps colours *and* records `quantize_index[j]=i, [i]=j`; then for every `i < num_palette` whose `quantize_index[i] >= maximum_colors`, finds nearest surviving colour via `PNG_COLOR_DIST` and points it there.\n   - **histogram == NULL** (647-813): the \"closest pair\" elimination. Allocates `index_to_palette` and `palette_to_index` (both `num_palette` bytes), a 769-entry `hash` of `png_dsort`. `max_d` starts 96 and grows by 96 per outer iteration until `num_new_palette <= maximum_colors`. Sub-branches: `png_malloc_warn` returning NULL -> `break` out (t==NULL guards at 711/720/724); parity branch `num_new_palette & 0x01` chooses which of the pair (`p->left` vs `p->right`) survives (740-749); `full_quantize == 0` also rewrites `quantize_index` (754-770). Frees hash + both index arrays, NULLs them.\n   - After either: `num_palette = maximum_colors` (814).\n6. `png_ptr->palette == NULL` (816-825): allocates an owned 256-entry `png_color` array and `memcpy`s `num_palette` entries from the caller's palette. **If `png_ptr->palette` is already non-NULL (the normal indexed-PNG case) the caller's palette is NOT copied in** — a distinct behaviour row.\n7. `num_palette = (png_uint_16)num_palette` (826).\n8. `full_quantize != 0` (828-887): builds `palette_lookup` of `1 << (RED+GREEN+BLUE bits)` = `1<<15` = 32768 entries (pnglibconf.h:216-218 give 5/5/5), using a `distance` array initialised to 0xff and the `d = dmax + dt + db` metric. This is what enables the RGB->palette row conversion.\n\nDistinct rows: {palette NULL}, {full_quantize 0/1} x {num_palette <= max}, {num_palette > max} x {histogram NULL / non-NULL} x {full_quantize 0/1}, {png_ptr->palette pre-existing or not}, {maximum_colors >= num_palette so no reduction but full_quantize builds lookup}, {maximum_colors small enough to force multiple max_d rounds}.\n\n## 1.8 `png_set_rgb_to_gray_fixed` (pngrtran.c:1046-1111) / `png_set_rgb_to_gray` (1118-1125)\n\nFloat wrapper uses `png_fixed(...)` per coefficient.\n1. **`png_rtran_ok(png_ptr, 1)`** — requires IHDR read (because of the colour-type test below). Distinct from every other setter.\n2. `error_action` switch (1057-1073):\n   - `PNG_ERROR_ACTION_NONE (1)` -> `|= PNG_RGB_TO_GRAY` (0x600000, i.e. *both* bits ERR|WARN).\n   - `PNG_ERROR_ACTION_WARN (2)` -> `|= PNG_RGB_TO_GRAY_WARN` (0x400000).\n   - `PNG_ERROR_ACTION_ERROR (3)` -> `|= PNG_RGB_TO_GRAY_ERR` (0x200000).\n   - default -> `png_error(\"invalid error action to rgb_to_gray\")`.\n   Note these OR in, so repeated calls with different actions accumulate to the NONE mask. And at row time (4963-4969) the *exact* mask value is tested: `== PNG_RGB_TO_GRAY_WARN` -> warn; `== PNG_RGB_TO_GRAY_ERR` -> error; the combined mask (action NONE) -> silent, only `rgb_to_gray_status=1`.\n3. `color_type == PNG_COLOR_TYPE_PALETTE` -> `|= PNG_EXPAND` (1077). (Without READ_EXPAND compiled: `png_error(\"Cannot do RGB_TO_GRAY without EXPAND_SUPPORTED\")`.)\n4. Coefficients (1089-1110):\n   - `red >= 0 && green >= 0 && red+green <= PNG_FP_1` -> `red_int = red*32768/100000`, `green_int` likewise (truncating, not rounding); sets `rgb_to_gray_red_coeff`, `rgb_to_gray_green_coeff`, `rgb_to_gray_coefficients_set = 1`. Blue is implicit `32768 - rc - gc` at row time (pngrtran.c:3151).\n   - `red >= 0 && green >= 0` but sum > PNG_FP_1 -> `png_app_warning(\"ignoring out of range rgb_to_gray coefficients\")`, `coefficients_set` stays 0 -> defaults apply.\n   - either negative -> **silently** falls through, no warning, defaults apply. (This is the documented \"-1,-1 means use defaults\" idiom.)\n\nNote there is *no* rejection for GRAY input: setting rgb_to_gray on a grayscale image sets the flag; `png_do_rgb_to_gray` then no-ops because `(color_type & PNG_COLOR_MASK_COLOR)==0` (3146), but `png_read_transform_info` still clears MASK_COLOR (harmlessly).\n\n## 1.9 The EXPAND family (all with no colour-type check; all just OR flags)\n\n- `png_set_expand` (948-957): `|= PNG_EXPAND | PNG_EXPAND_tRNS`.\n- `png_set_palette_to_rgb` (978-987): identical — `PNG_EXPAND | PNG_EXPAND_tRNS`.\n- `png_set_tRNS_to_alpha` (1002-1011): identical — `PNG_EXPAND | PNG_EXPAND_tRNS`.\n- `png_set_expand_gray_1_2_4_to_8` (990-999): `|= PNG_EXPAND` **only** (no EXPAND_tRNS). This is the only member that leaves tRNS alone, so it's the one that distinguishes gray<8 -> gray8 from gray -> gray+alpha.\n- `png_set_expand_16` (1018-1027): `|= PNG_EXPAND_16 | PNG_EXPAND | PNG_EXPAND_tRNS`.\n- `png_set_gray_to_rgb` (1031-1042): calls `png_set_expand_gray_1_2_4_to_8` (so `|= PNG_EXPAND` but not EXPAND_tRNS) then `|= PNG_GRAY_TO_RGB`.\n\nBecause these are indistinguishable at the flag level, the meaningful test rows are (input colour type x bit depth x tRNS present) x (EXPAND-only vs EXPAND+EXPAND_tRNS vs +EXPAND_16).\n\n## 1.10 `png_set_read_user_transform_fn` (pngrtran.c:1132-1142)\nNo NULL check on `png_ptr` (would crash on NULL), no `png_rtran_ok` — it can legally be called *after* row init. Sets `PNG_USER_TRANSFORM (0x100000)` and stores the pointer. Passing `NULL` as the function still sets the flag; at row time (5153) the NULL is checked and only the depth/channels fixup runs. Two extra axes: `png_ptr->user_transform_depth` and `user_transform_channels` (set by `png_set_user_transform_info`, pngset.c) which rewrite both `info_ptr` (2256-2263) and `row_info` (5165-5169).\n\n## 1.11 pngtrans.c setters (no `png_rtran_ok`, so all are legal at any time)\n\n- `png_set_bgr` (pngtrans.c:20-28): NULL check only; `|= PNG_BGR`. No colour-type check (do_bgr no-ops on non-colour).\n- `png_set_swap` (34-43): **only sets `PNG_SWAP_BYTES` if `png_ptr->bit_depth == 16`** — silently ignored for 1/2/4/8. Note this is the *file* bit depth, so `png_set_expand_16` on an 8-bit file followed by `png_set_swap` does nothing.\n- `png_set_packing` (49-63): **only if `bit_depth < 8`**; sets `PNG_PACK` (and `usr_bit_depth=8` when write support compiled). Silently ignored at >=8.\n- `png_set_packswap` (69-78): **only if `bit_depth < 8`**; sets `PNG_PACKSWAP`. Silently ignored otherwise.\n- `png_set_shift` (83-121): NULL `png_ptr` or NULL `true_bits` -> no-op. Validation: if colour -> each of red/green/blue must be `!=0 && <= bit_depth`; else gray must be `!=0 && <= bit_depth`; if alpha channel present, alpha must be `!=0 && <= bit_depth`. Any violation -> `png_app_error(\"png_set_shift: invalid shift values\")` and return. Success -> `|= PNG_SHIFT`, `shift = *true_bits`. **Note the palette case validates `red/green/blue` against `bit_depth`** (so for a 4-bit palette PNG the sBIT-like values must be <= 4 even though the palette entries are 8-bit) — but `png_init_read_transformations` (2019-2061) then applies `8 - sig_bit.X` shifts to the palette. That mismatch is a good test row.\n- `png_set_filler` (147-229):\n  - NULL -> no-op.\n  - Read struct (`mode & PNG_IS_READ_STRUCT`): **always valid regardless of colour type / bit depth**; just records `filler = (png_uint_16)filler`. (The comment at 161-168 says so explicitly; `usr_channels` is not used on read.) The write branch has the colour-type switch (RGB -> usr_channels 4; GRAY with bit_depth>=8 -> 2; GRAY <8 -> `png_app_error(\"png_set_filler is invalid for low bit depth gray output\")`; anything else -> `png_app_error(\"png_set_filler: inappropriate color type\")`).\n  - Then `|= PNG_FILLER (0x8000)`; `filler_loc == PNG_FILLER_AFTER (1)` -> `flags |= PNG_FLAG_FILLER_AFTER (0x0080)`, else (including any value != 1, e.g. PNG_FILLER_BEFORE=0 or garbage) `flags &= ~PNG_FLAG_FILLER_AFTER`.\n  - Effective legality on read is decided by `png_do_read_filler` (see §5.6): it only acts when the *row's* colour type is exactly GRAY or RGB and bit depth is 8 or 16. GRAY/RGB at bit depth 1/2/4 -> silent no-op at row time, **yet `png_read_transform_info` still bumps `channels`** (2243-2251) — an inconsistency worth a dedicated row.\n- `png_set_add_alpha` (232-244): calls `png_set_filler`, and *only if* `PNG_FILLER` actually got set does it add `PNG_ADD_ALPHA (0x1000000)`. So on read it always succeeds; on write it can end up as neither.\n- `png_set_swap_alpha` (250-259): NULL check; `|= PNG_SWAP_ALPHA`. No checks.\n- `png_set_invert_alpha` (264-273): NULL check; `|= PNG_INVERT_ALPHA`. No checks.\n- `png_set_invert_mono` (277-286): NULL check; `|= PNG_INVERT_MONO`. No checks (do_invert restricts to GRAY / GRAY_ALPHA).\n\n---\n\n# 2. `png_init_read_transformations` (pngrtran.c:1424-2063) — the full decision tree\n\nCalled from `png_read_start_row` (pngrutil.c:4416), i.e. before rowbytes are computed and before `png_read_transform_info`.\n\n### Step A — gamma resolution (1437-1459)\n`png_init_gamma_values` (1387-1421) uses `png_resolve_file_gamma` (1351-1385) with strict precedence:\n1. `png_ptr->file_gamma` (set only by `png_set_gamma`),\n2. `png_ptr->chunk_gamma` (gAMA/sRGB per PNGv3 rules, from pngrutil),\n3. `png_ptr->default_gamma` (set by `png_set_alpha_mode` or simplified API),\n4. `png_reciprocal(screen_gamma)` if screen_gamma != 0, else 0.\n\nThen:\n- `file_gamma > 0 && screen_gamma > 0` -> `gamma_correction = png_gamma_threshold(file, screen)` (1151-1164): `!png_muldiv(&gtest, file, screen, PNG_FP_1) || png_gamma_significant(gtest)`. Overflow in muldiv forces correction ON.\n- `file_gamma > 0 && screen_gamma <= 0` -> `screen_gamma = png_reciprocal(file_gamma)`, no correction.\n- `file_gamma <= 0` (both unset) -> `file_gamma = screen_gamma = PNG_FP_1`, no correction.\nBoth fields are written back into png_struct.\n`png_gamma_significant` (png.c:2909): true iff `g < 100000-5000` or `g > 100000+5000` (PNG_GAMMA_THRESHOLD_FIXED = 5000, pnglibconf.h:208).\n\nResult: `transformations |= PNG_GAMMA` if correction needed, else `&= ~PNG_GAMMA` (1454-1458). So PNG_GAMMA is *recomputed* here regardless of prior state.\n\n### Step B — STRIP_ALPHA without COMPOSE (1490-1512)\nIf `STRIP_ALPHA && !COMPOSE`:\n- clears `PNG_BACKGROUND_EXPAND | PNG_ENCODE_ALPHA | PNG_EXPAND_tRNS`,\n- clears `PNG_FLAG_OPTIMIZE_ALPHA`,\n- **`num_trans = 0`** (kills tRNS entirely, so `png_read_transform_info`'s palette EXPAND branch then yields RGB not RGBA).\n\n**strip_alpha + background both set:** because `png_set_background` itself sets STRIP_ALPHA, the combination is COMPOSE|STRIP_ALPHA, so this block is skipped; instead the strip happens *after* compose and gamma at line 5043-5049. Net effect: composite onto background, then drop the (now meaningless) alpha channel.\n\n### Step C — ENCODE/OPTIMIZE cancellation on insignificant screen gamma (1514-1523)\nIf `png_gamma_significant(screen_gamma) == 0` -> clear `PNG_ENCODE_ALPHA` and `PNG_FLAG_OPTIMIZE_ALPHA`. (So `PNG_ALPHA_BROKEN`/`OPTIMIZED` with gamma 1.0 degrade to plain ASSOCIATED.)\n\n### Step D — RGB_TO_GRAY coefficients (1525-1531)\nIf `PNG_RGB_TO_GRAY` -> `png_set_rgb_coefficients` (png.c:1886-1957):\n- If `rgb_to_gray_coefficients_set != 0` (app supplied valid values): do nothing.\n- Else if `have_chromaticities(png_ptr)` (png.c:~1850-1882: mDCV present -> yes; else sRGB present -> **no** (sRGB defaults are the built-ins); else cHRM present -> yes) and `png_XYZ_from_xy` succeeds: derive `r=xyz.red_Y, g=green_Y, b=blue_Y`, scale each to /32768 via `png_muldiv`, require all in `[0,32768]` and `r+g+b <= 32769`; then adjust the **largest** coefficient by ±1 to make the sum exactly 32768; if it still isn't 32768 -> `png_error(\"internal error handling cHRM coefficients\")`. Sets red/green coeff.\n- Else defaults: `red=6968, green=23434` (blue implicitly 2366) — the ITU-R Rec 709/D65 non-overflowing approximation described at 3111-3123.\n\nTest rows: {app coefficients valid} / {app coefficients out of range -> defaults} / {app negative -> defaults} / {no call, sRGB chunk -> defaults} / {no call, cHRM chunk -> derived} / {mDCV present -> derived} / {cHRM that fails the range test -> falls through to... nothing, coefficients stay at whatever png_struct init had} — note in the cHRM-present-but-invalid case the `else` at 1948 is *not* taken, so no defaults are applied; that's a latent zero-coefficient path.\n\n### Step E — PNG_BACKGROUND_IS_GRAY detection (1533-1577)\n- If `PNG_BACKGROUND_EXPAND` set: `(color_type & PNG_COLOR_MASK_COLOR) == 0` -> `mode |= PNG_BACKGROUND_IS_GRAY (0x800)`.\n- Else if `PNG_COMPOSE` set **and** `PNG_GRAY_TO_RGB` set: if `background.red == green == blue` -> `mode |= PNG_BACKGROUND_IS_GRAY` and `background.gray = background.red`.\n- Otherwise not gray.\n\nThis single bit chooses whether `png_do_gray_to_rgb` runs early (step 4 of the row order, before compose) or late (step 13, after quantize/expand_16). Two very different row-order rows.\n\n### Step F — palette vs rgb pre-pass (1590-1594)\n- `color_type == PNG_COLOR_TYPE_PALETTE` -> `png_init_palette_transformations` (1176-1263):\n  - Scans `trans_alpha[0..num_trans)`: `input_has_transparency=1` if any entry is 0 or partial; `input_has_alpha=1` (and break) only if some entry is neither 0 nor 255.\n  - `input_has_alpha == 0` -> clear `PNG_ENCODE_ALPHA` and `PNG_FLAG_OPTIMIZE_ALPHA`; and if `input_has_transparency == 0` also clear `PNG_COMPOSE | PNG_BACKGROUND_EXPAND`. (So a tRNS that is all-255 disables background compositing entirely.)\n  - If `PNG_BACKGROUND_EXPAND && PNG_EXPAND` (1233): **`background.red/green/blue = palette[background.index].{red,green,blue}`** — this is where `need_expand=1` on a palette image means \"background.index is a palette index\".\n  - Inside that, if `PNG_INVERT_ALPHA && !PNG_EXPAND_tRNS` (1245): inverts `trans_alpha[i] = 255 - trans_alpha[i]` in place, *now*, rather than per row. (If EXPAND_tRNS is set the inversion is left to `png_do_read_invert_alpha` later.) Note this in-place mutation persists in png_struct.\n- else -> `png_init_rgb_transformations` (1265-1348):\n  - `input_has_alpha = (color_type & MASK_ALPHA) != 0`; `input_has_transparency = num_trans > 0`.\n  - Same cancellation: no alpha -> clear ENCODE_ALPHA/OPTIMIZE_ALPHA; no alpha and no tRNS -> clear `COMPOSE | BACKGROUND_EXPAND`.\n  - If `BACKGROUND_EXPAND && EXPAND && (color_type & MASK_COLOR) == 0` (GRAY or GRAY_ALPHA) (1300): scale `background.gray` and `trans_color.gray` from the file bit depth to 8 bits: `*0xff` for depth 1, `*0x55` for 2, `*0x11` for 4, unchanged for 8 and 16 (1310-1335). Then `background.red = green = blue = gray`. And if `PNG_EXPAND_tRNS == 0`, also `trans_color.red = green = blue = trans_gray` (1340-1344). Note: **RGB/RGB_ALPHA with BACKGROUND_EXPAND gets no expansion at all** — the supplied background values are used as-is, which is why the comment at 1538-1544 warns about it.\n\n### Step G — background depth fixups (1596-1642)\n- `EXPAND_16 && COMPOSE && !BACKGROUND_EXPAND && bit_depth != 16` (1598): `CHOP` (i.e. `PNG_DIV257`) each of background.red/green/blue/gray from 16-bit to 8-bit, because expand_16 happens *after* compose.\n- `(16_TO_8|SCALE_16_TO_8) && COMPOSE && !BACKGROUND_EXPAND && bit_depth == 16` (1625): multiply each background component by 257 (8-bit -> 16-bit), because the 16->8 reduction happens after compose.\nThese two are mutually exclusive on bit_depth and are distinct rows; both are skipped when BACKGROUND_EXPAND is set.\n\n### Step H — `background_1 = background` (1655), then the big \"do we need gamma tables\" test (1671-1683)\n\nTables are built iff **any** of:\n1. `PNG_GAMMA` set; or\n2. `PNG_RGB_TO_GRAY` set **and** (`png_gamma_significant(file_gamma)` or `png_gamma_significant(screen_gamma)`); or\n3. `PNG_COMPOSE` set **and** (`significant(file_gamma)` or `significant(screen_gamma)` or (`background_gamma_type == PNG_BACKGROUND_GAMMA_UNIQUE` and `significant(background_gamma)`)); or\n4. `PNG_ENCODE_ALPHA` set **and** `significant(screen_gamma)`.\n\nIf yes -> `png_build_gamma_table(png_ptr, png_ptr->bit_depth)` (see §6), then:\n\n#### H1. COMPOSE set (1688-1943)\n- If `PNG_RGB_TO_GRAY` also set -> `png_warning(\"libpng does not support gamma+background+rgb_to_gray\")` (double gamma correction bug).\n- **Palette sub-path** (1700-1857): computes `back` (screen-gamma space) and `back_1` (linear space):\n  - `background_gamma_type == PNG_BACKGROUND_GAMMA_FILE` -> `back.X = gamma_table[background.X]`, `back_1.X = gamma_to_1[background.X]`.\n  - Otherwise compute `g`/`gs` by type: SCREEN -> `g = screen_gamma, gs = PNG_FP_1`; FILE -> `g = 1/file_gamma, gs = 1/(file_gamma*screen_gamma)`; UNIQUE -> `g = 1/background_gamma, gs = 1/(background_gamma*screen_gamma)`; **default -> `g = gs = PNG_FP_1` silently** (1742). Then each of `back`/`back_1` is either `png_gamma_8bit_correct(...)` if the respective gamma is significant, or the raw byte if not — 4 combinations (gs sig x g sig).\n  - Then for each palette entry i (1783-1847):\n    - `i < num_trans && trans_alpha[i] != 0xff`:\n      - `trans_alpha[i] == 0` -> `palette[i] = back`.\n      - else if `PNG_FLAG_OPTIMIZE_ALPHA` -> premultiply only: `gamma_from_1[(gamma_to_1[c]*alpha + 128)/255]` per channel.\n      - else -> composite with `back_1` via `png_composite` in linear space, then `gamma_from_1`.\n    - else -> plain `palette[i].X = gamma_table[palette[i].X]`.\n  - Then **`transformations &= ~(PNG_COMPOSE | PNG_GAMMA)`** and clears `PNG_FLAG_OPTIMIZE_ALPHA` (1855-1856). This is the \"palette-only fast path\": compose+gamma disappear from the per-row pipeline entirely. Consequence: `png_read_transform_info`'s `if (COMPOSE) info_ptr->background = ...` (2126) does **not** fire for this case.\n  - The comment at 1702 asserts this is only reachable when tRNS has non-opaque entries (guaranteed by Step F).\n- **Non-palette sub-path** (1860-1942):\n  - `g`/`gs` by background_gamma_type as above, but here `default: png_error(\"invalid background gamma type\")`.\n  - `g_sig`, `gs_sig` computed. `background_1.gray = png_gamma_correct(background.gray, g)` if g_sig; `background.gray = png_gamma_correct(background.gray, gs)` if gs_sig. (Note: if not significant, the corresponding field is left untouched — `background_1` was copied at 1655 so it holds the raw value.)\n  - Then: if `background.red != green || red != blue || red != gray` (i.e. a genuinely coloured background) -> gamma-correct red/green/blue into `background_1` (if g_sig) and `background` (if gs_sig).\n  - Else (gray background, any colour type) -> `background_1.red=green=blue=background_1.gray` and `background.red=green=blue=background.gray`.\n    **Gotcha:** this test is done *after* `background.gray` was overwritten by the gs correction, so the branch taken depends on the corrected gray value.\n  - Finally `background_gamma_type = PNG_BACKGROUND_GAMMA_SCREEN` (1941) — the background is now in screen space, which is what `png_do_compose` assumes.\n\n#### H2. COMPOSE not set, palette gamma fast path (1948-1972)\n`else if (color_type == PALETTE && (!(EXPAND) || !(RGB_TO_GRAY)))`:\n- Applies `gamma_table` to every palette entry, then **`transformations &= ~PNG_GAMMA`**.\n- The RGB_TO_GRAY guard exists because rgb_to_gray needs un-gamma-corrected values, so palette+EXPAND+RGB_TO_GRAY leaves PNG_GAMMA set and does it per-row via `png_do_rgb_to_gray`'s internal gamma handling.\n\n#### H3. The hanging `else` (1974-2016)\nIf the gamma-table condition was false (and READ_BACKGROUND compiled), the `else` attaches to:\n`if (COMPOSE && color_type == PALETTE)` -> the **no-gamma palette compose fast path**: `back` = raw `background.{red,green,blue}` bytes; for `i < num_trans`: alpha==0 -> `palette[i] = back`; alpha != 0xff -> `png_composite` per channel with no gamma; then `transformations &= ~PNG_COMPOSE`.\nNote the loop bound here is `num_trans` (not `num_palette`), unlike H1's palette loop.\n\n### Step I — palette SHIFT fast path (2018-2062)\n`if (PNG_SHIFT && !PNG_EXPAND && color_type == PALETTE)`:\n- Clears `PNG_SHIFT` immediately (2027) so no per-row unshift happens.\n- For each of red/green/blue independently: `shift = 8 - sig_bit.X`; applies `palette[i].X >>= shift` only if `0 < shift < 8`. `sig_bit.X == 0` (shift 8) or `== 8` (shift 0) -> that channel silently unshifted.\n- Note: palette + SHIFT + **EXPAND** leaves PNG_SHIFT set, and the row then goes through `png_do_unshift` after expansion as RGB/RGBA — a different result.\n\n---\n\n# 3. `png_read_transform_info` (pngrtran.c:2069-2283) — exact info_ptr rewrite order\n\nExecuted in this order; each step sees the previous step's mutations.\n\n1. **Palette sync (2074-2083):** if `info_ptr->color_type == PALETTE && info_ptr->palette != NULL && png_ptr->palette != NULL` -> `memcpy(info_ptr->palette, png_ptr->palette, 256 * sizeof(png_color))`. (This is what makes `png_get_PLTE` reflect the gamma/background-modified palette from §2 H1/H2/H3/I.)\n2. **EXPAND (2086-2118):**\n   - `info_ptr->color_type == PALETTE`:\n     - `png_ptr->num_trans > 0` -> `color_type = PNG_COLOR_TYPE_RGB_ALPHA (6)`; else `PNG_COLOR_TYPE_RGB (2)`.\n     - `bit_depth = 8`; `num_trans = 0`.\n     - `png_ptr->palette == NULL` -> `png_error(\"Palette is NULL in indexed image\")`.\n     - Note this deliberately mirrors `png_do_expand_palette` (which also keys only on `num_trans > 0`, not on whether tRNS is all-opaque).\n   - non-palette:\n     - `png_ptr->num_trans != 0 && (transformations & PNG_EXPAND_tRNS)` -> `color_type |= PNG_COLOR_MASK_ALPHA` (GRAY->GRAY_ALPHA, RGB->RGB_ALPHA).\n     - `bit_depth < 8` -> `bit_depth = 8`.\n     - `num_trans = 0` unconditionally.\n3. **COMPOSE (2126-2127):** `info_ptr->background = png_ptr->background`. Only if PNG_COMPOSE is still set — cleared by the palette fast paths, so palette images typically don't get this.\n4. **Gamma (2140):** `info_ptr->gamma = png_ptr->file_gamma` — unconditional (not gated on PNG_GAMMA), and `file_gamma` was normalised in `png_init_gamma_values` (possibly to PNG_FP_1).\n5. **16-bit reduction (2143-2179):** if `info_ptr->bit_depth == 16` (note: after step 2, so an expanded palette is 8 and skips this): `PNG_SCALE_16_TO_8` -> 8; `PNG_16_TO_8` -> 8. (In a `!PNG_READ_16BIT` build the transformations flag is *forced on* here and bit_depth set to 8.)\n6. **GRAY_TO_RGB (2182-2184):** `color_type |= PNG_COLOR_MASK_COLOR`. Unconditional on colour type — applying it to an already-RGB image is a no-op on the mask, and `png_do_gray_to_rgb` no-ops at row time, so channels stay 3. Consistent.\n7. **RGB_TO_GRAY (2188-2190):** `color_type &= ~PNG_COLOR_MASK_COLOR`. Also unconditional.\n   Order matters: GRAY_TO_RGB then RGB_TO_GRAY means setting both yields **gray**.\n8. **QUANTIZE (2194-2202):** only if `color_type` is RGB or RGB_ALPHA **and** `png_ptr->palette_lookup != 0` **and** `info_ptr->bit_depth == 8` -> `color_type = PNG_COLOR_TYPE_PALETTE`. (Matches `png_do_quantize`'s gating.) Note quantize of a PALETTE input (the quantize_index path) leaves colour type PALETTE and does nothing here.\n9. **EXPAND_16 (2206-2211):** only if `bit_depth == 8 && color_type != PALETTE` -> `bit_depth = 16`. So expand_16 after quantize-to-palette does nothing (matches `png_do_expand_16`'s guard).\n10. **PACK (2215-2217):** if `PNG_PACK && bit_depth < 8` -> `bit_depth = 8`.\n11. **channels (2220-2227):** PALETTE -> 1; else MASK_COLOR -> 3; else 1.\n12. **STRIP_ALPHA (2230-2235):** `color_type &= ~PNG_COLOR_MASK_ALPHA`; `num_trans = 0`.\n13. **alpha channel (2238-2239):** `if (color_type & MASK_ALPHA) channels++`.\n14. **FILLER (2243-2251):** if `PNG_FILLER` **and** resulting `color_type` is exactly `PNG_COLOR_TYPE_RGB` or `PNG_COLOR_TYPE_GRAY`: `channels++`; and if `PNG_ADD_ALPHA` also `color_type |= PNG_COLOR_MASK_ALPHA`. **No bit-depth check here** — this is the mismatch with `png_do_read_filler` for bit depths 1/2/4 noted in §1.11.\n15. **USER_TRANSFORM (2256-2263):** `user_transform_depth != 0` -> `bit_depth = user_transform_depth`; `user_transform_channels != 0` -> `channels = user_transform_channels`.\n16. **Derived (2266-2269):** `pixel_depth = channels * bit_depth`; `rowbytes = PNG_ROWBYTES(pixel_depth, width)`; and `png_ptr->info_rowbytes = info_ptr->rowbytes` (2277) which is later used to bounds-check the app's buffer.\n\nNote that `info_ptr->width/height/interlace_type` are untouched here — interlace expansion is handled elsewhere; also `png_read_start_row` (pngrutil.c:4423-4448+) computes `max_pixel_depth` with a *separate*, parallel set of rules and the comment at 4441 warns that the calculation is duplicated in three places. Any test row where these disagree is a buffer-overflow row.\n\n---\n\n# 4. `png_do_read_transformations` (pngrtran.c:4880-5177) — exact order and gates\n\nPrologue: `row_buf == NULL` -> `png_error(\"NULL row buffer\")` (4891); `PNG_FLAG_DETECT_UNINITIALIZED && !PNG_FLAG_ROW_INIT` -> `png_error(\"Uninitialized row\")` (4907).\n\n| # | Transform | Gate (source line) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `png_do_expand_palette` / `png_do_expand` | `PNG_EXPAND` (4911). If `row_info->color_type == PALETTE` -> expand_palette with `png_ptr->palette, trans_alpha, num_trans` (and NEON riffled-palette init when `num_trans>0 && png_ptr->bit_depth==8`, 4916-4925). Else -> `png_do_expand` with `&trans_color` iff `num_trans != 0 && PNG_EXPAND_tRNS`, otherwise `NULL` (4933-4939). |\n| 2 | `png_do_strip_channel(.., at_start=0)` | `PNG_STRIP_ALPHA && !PNG_COMPOSE && row colour type is RGB_ALPHA or GRAY_ALPHA` (4945). |\n| 3 | `png_do_rgb_to_gray` | `PNG_RGB_TO_GRAY` (4954). On nonzero return: `rgb_to_gray_status=1`; mask `== PNG_RGB_TO_GRAY_WARN` -> warning; `== PNG_RGB_TO_GRAY_ERR` -> `png_error`. |\n| 4 | `png_do_gray_to_rgb` (early) | `PNG_GRAY_TO_RGB && !(mode & PNG_BACKGROUND_IS_GRAY)` (5009). |\n| 5 | `png_do_compose` | `PNG_COMPOSE` (5016). |\n| 6 | `png_do_gamma` | `PNG_GAMMA` **&&** `!PNG_RGB_TO_GRAY` **&&** `!(PNG_COMPOSE && (num_trans != 0 \\|\\| (png_ptr->color_type & MASK_ALPHA)))` **&&** `png_ptr->color_type != PALETTE` (5021-5038). Four independent suppressors. |\n| 7 | `png_do_strip_channel(.., 0)` | `PNG_STRIP_ALPHA && PNG_COMPOSE && row colour type RGB_ALPHA or GRAY_ALPHA` (5043). |\n| 8 | `png_do_encode_alpha` | `PNG_ENCODE_ALPHA && (row_info->color_type & MASK_ALPHA)` (5052). |\n| 9 | `png_do_scale_16_to_8` | `PNG_SCALE_16_TO_8` (5058). |\n| 10 | `png_do_chop` | `PNG_16_TO_8` (5067). Harmless after 9 because bit_depth is already 8. |\n| 11 | `png_do_quantize` | `PNG_QUANTIZE` (5072), passing `palette_lookup` and `quantize_index`. |\n| 12 | `png_do_expand_16` | `PNG_EXPAND_16` (5083). |\n| 13 | `png_do_gray_to_rgb` (late) | `PNG_GRAY_TO_RGB && (mode & PNG_BACKGROUND_IS_GRAY)` (5089). |\n| 14 | `png_do_invert` | `PNG_INVERT_MONO` (5095). |\n| 15 | `png_do_read_invert_alpha` | `PNG_INVERT_ALPHA` (5100). |\n| 16 | `png_do_unshift` | `PNG_SHIFT` (5105), with `&png_ptr->shift`. |\n| 17 | `png_do_unpack` | `PNG_PACK` (5111). |\n| 18 | `png_do_check_palette_indexes` | `row_info->color_type == PALETTE && png_ptr->num_palette_max >= 0` (5117) — not a transform, but a distinct config axis (`png_set_check_for_invalid_index`). |\n| 19 | `png_do_bgr` | `PNG_BGR` (5123). |\n| 20 | `png_do_packswap` | `PNG_PACKSWAP` (5128). |\n| 21 | `png_do_read_filler` | `PNG_FILLER` (5133), passing `png_ptr->filler` and `png_ptr->flags` (only `PNG_FLAG_FILLER_AFTER` is read). |\n| 22 | `png_do_read_swap_alpha` | `PNG_SWAP_ALPHA` (5139). |\n| 23 | `png_do_swap` | `PNG_SWAP_BYTES` (5145), inside `#ifdef PNG_READ_16BIT_SUPPORTED`. |\n| 24 | user transform | `PNG_USER_TRANSFORM` (5151); calls fn only if non-NULL; then applies `user_transform_depth`/`user_transform_channels` and recomputes `pixel_depth` and `rowbytes` (5165-5174). |\n\nNote the header comment at 1466-1488 lists the same order and is accurate. Notable order consequences to test: PACK (17) after SHIFT (16) — unshift operates on packed low-bit-depth data; BGR (19) after FILLER? no — BGR at 19 is *before* FILLER at 21, so a filler channel is never BGR-swapped; INVERT_ALPHA (15) before FILLER (21), so a filler-supplied alpha is *not* inverted; SWAP_ALPHA (22) after FILLER (21), so a filler alpha *is* moved to the front.\n\n`png_do_read_intrapixel` is **not** in pngrtran.c — it lives in pngread.c:226 (MNG features, sequential reader only) and is applied at pngread.c:471 before `png_do_read_transformations` at 481. Its branches: `(color_type & MASK_COLOR) != 0`; bit_depth 8 with `bytes_per_pixel` 3 (RGB) or 4 (RGBA), else return; bit_depth 16 similarly.\n\n---\n\n# 5. Per-row worker sub-branches\n\n## 5.1 `png_do_unpack` (2292-2381)\nOuter guard `bit_depth < 8` (so 8/16 -> total no-op, row_info untouched). Switch on bit depth: **1**, **2**, **4** (each its own reverse-walk loop with its own initial `shift` computation), `default: break`. On any of the three (and also on the default!) it then sets `bit_depth = 8`, `pixel_depth = 8*channels`, `rowbytes = width*channels`.\nNote the depth-4 loop sets `shift = 4` (not `shift += 4`) in the else branch at 2367 — mirrors depth-4 in `png_do_expand` (4612) but differs from `png_do_expand_palette` case 4 which uses `shift += 4` (4433). Worth a targeted odd/even width row.\n\n## 5.2 `png_do_unshift` (2390-2524)\n- Guard: `color_type != PNG_COLOR_TYPE_PALETTE` (palette already handled in init).\n- Channel/shift setup: MASK_COLOR -> shifts from red, green, blue; else from gray; plus alpha if MASK_ALPHA. So `channels` ∈ {1,2,3,4}.\n- Per-channel clamp: `shift[c] <= 0 || shift[c] >= bit_depth` -> 0. If **no** channel has a usable shift -> early return, row untouched (a distinct \"silently ignored\" row).\n- Switch on bit_depth: `default` (i.e. 1 bpp) -> NOTREACHED/break; **2** (2bpp gray, hardcoded `>>1 & 0x55`); **4** (4bpp gray, uses `shift[0]` and mask `0xf>>shift` replicated); **8** (byte-per-channel, cycles `channel` through `channels`); **16** (`#ifdef PNG_READ_16BIT_SUPPORTED`, big-endian pair, cycles channel).\n- Note the 2-bpp case ignores the actual `shift[0]` value and always shifts by 1.\n- `row_info` is *not* updated (no depth change).\n\n## 5.3 `png_do_scale_16_to_8` (2529-2583) vs `png_do_chop` (2591-2611)\nBoth guard on `row_info->bit_depth == 16` (else complete no-op) and both then set `bit_depth = 8`, `pixel_depth = 8*channels`, `rowbytes = width*channels`. Scale uses the exact `tmp += ((*sp - tmp + 128) * 65535) >> 24` rounding; chop discards the low byte. No colour-type dependence (operates on the whole row as components). Only one branch each, but the \"already 8-bit\" no-op is a second row.\n\n## 5.4 `png_do_read_swap_alpha` (2615-2707)\n4 branches: `RGB_ALPHA` x {bit_depth 8 (RGBA->ARGB), else 16 (RRGGBBAA->AARRGGBB, `#ifdef READ_16BIT`)}; `GRAY_ALPHA` x {8 (GA->AG), else 16 (GGAA->AAGG)}. Any other colour type -> no-op. `row_info` unchanged.\n\n## 5.5 `png_do_read_invert_alpha` (2711-2808)\nSame 4 branches: RGB_ALPHA/8, RGB_ALPHA/16, GRAY_ALPHA/8, GRAY_ALPHA/16. Others no-op. In the 16-bit cases *both* alpha bytes are inverted independently (2752-2753, 2796-2797).\n\n## 5.6 `png_do_read_filler` (2813-2995)\nGuard is on the **row's** colour type being exactly `PNG_COLOR_TYPE_GRAY` (2828) or exactly `PNG_COLOR_TYPE_RGB` (2905). Then per colour type:\n- GRAY, bit_depth 8: AFTER -> G->GX (`row_info->channels=2, pixel_depth=16, rowbytes=width*2`); BEFORE -> G->XG (same metrics).\n- GRAY, bit_depth 16 (`#ifdef READ_16BIT`): AFTER -> GG->GGXX; BEFORE -> GG->XXGG (`channels=2, pixel_depth=32, rowbytes=width*4`). Uses both `hi_filler = filler>>8` and `lo_filler`.\n- RGB, bit_depth 8: AFTER -> RGB->RGBX; BEFORE -> RGB->XRGB (`channels=4, pixel_depth=32, rowbytes=width*4`).\n- RGB, bit_depth 16: AFTER -> RRGGBB->RRGGBBXX; BEFORE -> ...->XXRRGGBB (`channels=4, pixel_depth=64, rowbytes=width*8`).\n- **bit depths 1/2/4 -> silent no-op** for both GRAY and RGB (no `else` clause) — and PALETTE, GRAY_ALPHA, RGB_ALPHA -> no-op. That's the mismatch with `png_read_transform_info` step 14.\n- Note the AFTER loops run `i = 1..row_width` then emit one final filler outside the loop (2837/2842 etc.), whereas BEFORE loops run `0..row_width`. Same result, different edge behaviour for width 0/1.\n- The 8-bit paths use only `lo_filler`; the high byte of a 16-bit `filler` value is discarded — worth a row.\n\n## 5.7 `png_do_gray_to_rgb` (3000-3083)\nGuard: `bit_depth >= 8 && (color_type & MASK_COLOR) == 0`. Branches: `GRAY` x {8 (G->RGB), else 16 (GG->RRGGBB)}; `GRAY_ALPHA` x {8 (GA->RGBA), else 16 (GGAA->RRGGBBAA)}. Then unconditionally (inside the guard) `channels += 2`, `color_type |= MASK_COLOR`, recompute `pixel_depth` and `rowbytes` via PNG_ROWBYTES. bit_depth < 8 or already colour -> full no-op (row_info untouched).\n\n## 5.8 `png_do_rgb_to_gray` (3139-3331)\nGuard: `!(color_type & MASK_PALETTE) && (color_type & MASK_COLOR)`.\nCoefficients: `rc`, `gc`, `bc = 32768 - rc - gc`. `have_alpha = (color_type & MASK_ALPHA)`.\n- **bit_depth == 8**:\n  - `gamma_from_1 != NULL && gamma_to_1 != NULL` (3163): per pixel, if `red != green || red != blue` -> linearise via `gamma_to_1`, weighted sum `(rc*r+gc*g+bc*b+16384)>>15` (rounded), back through `gamma_from_1`, and `rgb_error |= 1`. Else (already gray) -> `gamma_table[red]` **if `gamma_table != NULL`** (an inner sub-branch, 3191), otherwise raw red.\n  - else (no gamma tables): unequal -> `(rc*r+gc*g+bc*b)>>15` **truncated, no +16384** (3220) — the historical approach and a numerically different result. Equal -> raw red.\n- **bit_depth == 16** (`else`, 3232):\n  - `gamma_16_to_1 != NULL && gamma_16_from_1 != NULL` (3235): equal-components -> `gamma_16_table[...]` if non-NULL else raw; unequal -> linearise all three through `gamma_16_to_1`, weighted `+16384>>15`, back through `gamma_16_from_1`, `rgb_error |= 1`.\n  - else: always the accurate rounded `+16384>>15` computation (3309, \"from 1.5.5 do the accurate conversion even in the fast case\"), `rgb_error |= 1` only if unequal.\n  - Both use `gamma_shift` for table indexing.\n- `have_alpha` adds a copy of 1 byte (8-bit) or 2 bytes (16-bit) per pixel in every sub-branch.\n- Epilogue (inside the guard): `channels -= 2`, `color_type &= ~MASK_COLOR`, recompute pixel_depth/rowbytes.\n- Returns `rgb_error`, which drives the warn/error decision at 4960-4970.\n\nThat's 8 distinct sub-branches (2 depths x tables-present/absent x equal/unequal), plus the inner `gamma_table == NULL` variants.\n\n## 5.9 `png_do_compose` (3340-4074)\nReads six tables plus `gamma_shift` and `optimize = (flags & PNG_FLAG_OPTIMIZE_ALPHA)`. Switch on `row_info->color_type`:\n- **GRAY** (3363): switch on bit_depth\n  - 1 (3367): single loop, no gamma variant at all — if the 1-bit sample equals `trans_color.gray`, splice `background.gray << shift` into the byte.\n  - 2 (3394): two variants — `gamma_table != NULL` (applies `gamma_table[p replicated to 8 bits] >> 6` to non-transparent pixels) vs not (transparent substitution only).\n  - 4 (3462): same two variants (`gamma_table[p|(p<<4)] >> 4`).\n  - 8 (3530): two variants (`gamma_table[*sp]` for non-transparent).\n  - 16 (3558): two variants keyed on `gamma_16 != NULL`, using `gamma_16[*(sp+1)>>gamma_shift][*sp]`.\n  - default: break.\n- **RGB** (3615): `bit_depth == 8` x {`gamma_table != NULL`, not} ; else 16 x {`gamma_16 != NULL`, not}. Transparency test is the exact 3-component match against `trans_color.red/green/blue`.\n- **GRAY_ALPHA** (3742): `bit_depth == 8` x {`gamma_to_1 && gamma_from_1 && gamma_table` all non-NULL, else}; else 16 x {`gamma_16 && gamma_16_from_1 && gamma_16_to_1`, else}. Inside the gamma variants, three alpha cases: `a == 0xff/0xffff` (just gamma-correct), `a == 0` (substitute `background.gray`, already in screen gamma), otherwise composite in linear space against `background_1.gray` and re-encode via `gamma_from_1`/`gamma_16_from_1` **unless `optimize != 0`** (3770-3771, 3827-3831) in which case the linear value is kept. Non-gamma variants: `a == 0` -> background; `a < 0xff/0xffff` -> `png_composite`/`png_composite_16` against `background.gray`; `a` full -> untouched.\n- **RGB_ALPHA** (3868): identical structure, 8 vs 16 x tables/no-tables, with the same three alpha cases and the same `optimize` sub-branch, per channel against `background_1.{red,green,blue}` / `background.{...}`.\n- default: break (PALETTE reaches here and does nothing — palette compose was done in init).\n\n`row_info` is never modified by compose (the alpha channel is removed later by STRIP_ALPHA, if at all).\n\nCount: GRAY {1, 2x2, 4x2, 8x2, 16x2} = 9; RGB 4; GRAY_ALPHA 4 x 3 alpha cases x (optimize on/off in the partial case); RGB_ALPHA same. Roughly 25 leaf paths.\n\n## 5.10 `png_do_gamma` (4084-4277)\nGuard: `(bit_depth <= 8 && gamma_table != NULL) || (bit_depth == 16 && gamma_16_table != NULL)`. Switch on colour type:\n- RGB: 8 / else-16.\n- RGB_ALPHA: 8 / else-16 (alpha skipped: `sp++` at 4160, `sp += 4` at 4182).\n- GRAY_ALPHA: 8 / else-16.\n- GRAY: `bit_depth == 2` (packs 4 pixels/byte, replicating each 2-bit value to 8 bits for the lookup, then masking back); **then a separate `if (bit_depth == 4)`** (4235 — note it is `if`, not `else if`, so the 2-bit case falls into this test too, but 2 != 4 so it's harmless), `else if 8`, `else if 16`. There is **no bit_depth == 1 case** — 1-bit gray is silently unchanged even though the guard `bit_depth <= 8` admits it.\n- default: break (PALETTE -> nothing; also nothing for a 1-bit gray row).\n`row_info` untouched.\n\n## 5.11 `png_do_encode_alpha` (4285-4342)\nGuard: `(color_type & MASK_ALPHA)`. Then:\n- `bit_depth == 8` and `gamma_from_1 != NULL` -> `step = (color_type & MASK_COLOR) ? 4 : 2`, walks the last component, returns.\n- `bit_depth == 16` and `gamma_16_from_1 != NULL` -> `step = MASK_COLOR ? 8 : 4`, walks the last 2 bytes with `gamma_shift`, returns.\n- **Any fall-through** (no alpha, or bit depth 1/2/4, or the relevant table NULL) -> `png_warning(\"png_do_encode_alpha: unexpected call\")` (4341). That warning is a directly observable test assertion; reachable e.g. with ENCODE_ALPHA set but only the `gamma_table` built.\n\n## 5.12 `png_do_expand_palette` (4349-4518)\nGuard: `row_info->color_type == PNG_COLOR_TYPE_PALETTE`.\n- `bit_depth < 8` (4363): switch 1 / 2 / 4 (each unpacking to one index per byte), `default: break`; then `bit_depth = 8, pixel_depth = 8, rowbytes = row_width`.\n- Then `if (bit_depth == 8)` (4448) — reached both for natively-8-bit and just-unpacked rows:\n  - **`num_trans > 0`** (4451, the tRNS path): produces RGBA. Per pixel: `*sp >= num_trans` -> alpha `0xff`, else `trans_alpha[*sp]`; then blue, green, red from `palette[*sp]`. Sets `bit_depth=8, pixel_depth=32, rowbytes=width*4, color_type=6, channels=4`. NEON fast path `png_do_expand_palette_rgba8_neon` used only when `png_ptr->riffled_palette != NULL` (which init at 4916 only does when `png_ptr->bit_depth == 8`, so 1/2/4-bit palettes take the scalar loop).\n  - **`num_trans <= 0`** (the **\"not tRNS\" fast path**, 4489): produces RGB, 3 stores per pixel, no alpha test at all. Sets `pixel_depth=24, rowbytes=width*3, color_type=2, channels=3`. NEON `png_do_expand_palette_rgb8_neon` used unconditionally when compiled.\n  - The 16-bit-palette case cannot occur (PNG forbids it), so no branch.\n  - Note the bounds behaviour: out-of-range indices are safe for alpha (`>= num_trans` -> 0xff) but `palette[*sp]` relies on the 256-entry allocation.\n\n## 5.13 `png_do_expand` (4523-4746)\nTwo top-level cases:\n- **GRAY** (4534): `gray = trans_color ? trans_color->gray : 0`.\n  - `bit_depth < 8`: switch 1 (`gray = (gray&1)*0xff`; samples become 0/0xff), 2 (`gray = (gray&3)*0x55`; value replicated x4), 4 (`gray = (gray&0xf)*0x11`; value replicated x2), default break. Then `bit_depth=8, pixel_depth=8, rowbytes=row_width`.\n  - Then `if (trans_color != NULL)`: `bit_depth == 8` -> compare `*sp == (gray & 0xff)` and emit alpha 0/0xff, G->GA; `bit_depth == 16` -> compare both bytes against `gray_high`/`gray_low`, GG->GGAA. Then `color_type = GRAY_ALPHA, channels = 2, pixel_depth = bit_depth<<1, rowbytes = PNG_ROWBYTES(...)`.\n  - `trans_color == NULL` -> only the depth expansion happens (this is the `png_set_expand_gray_1_2_4_to_8`-only row); for bit_depth 8/16 with NULL trans_color it's a **total no-op**.\n  - Note the sub-8 case with `trans_color != NULL` combines both: unpack *and* alpha, using the pre-scaled `gray`.\n- **RGB && trans_color != NULL** (4681): `bit_depth == 8` (3-byte compare) or `bit_depth == 16` (6-byte compare) -> adds alpha. Then `color_type = RGB_ALPHA, channels = 4, pixel_depth = bit_depth<<2, rowbytes = ...`. **The epilogue at 4741-4744 is outside the depth `if`s**, so an RGB row at some other bit depth would get its metadata rewritten with no data change — unreachable in practice since RGB is only 8/16.\n- RGB with `trans_color == NULL`, GRAY_ALPHA, RGB_ALPHA, PALETTE -> complete no-op.\n- **The tRNS-in-palette -> alpha expansion is *not* here** — it is in `png_do_expand_palette` (§5.12), which reads `trans_alpha[]` directly. `png_do_expand` only handles the *single-colour* tRNS forms (gray value / RGB triple) in `trans_color`.\n\n## 5.14 `png_do_expand_16` (4753-4779)\nGuard: `bit_depth == 8 && color_type != PALETTE`. Byte replication in place (v -> v*257). Sets `rowbytes *= 2, bit_depth = 16, pixel_depth = channels*16`. Everything else -> no-op (already 16, or sub-8, or a quantized palette row).\n\n## 5.15 `png_do_quantize` (4783-4873)\nGuard: `bit_depth == 8` (all other depths -> no-op). Then three mutually exclusive branches:\n- `color_type == RGB && palette_lookup` (4795): 3-component 5:5:5 index into `palette_lookup`; sets `color_type = PALETTE, channels = 1, pixel_depth = bit_depth (8), rowbytes = PNG_ROWBYTES(...)`.\n- `color_type == RGB_ALPHA && palette_lookup != NULL` (4831): same but skips the alpha byte (`sp++` at 4842) — **alpha is discarded**; same metadata rewrite.\n- `color_type == PALETTE && quantize_lookup` (4862): in-place `*sp = quantize_lookup[*sp]`; **no metadata change**. Requires `full_quantize == 0` at set time.\n- Anything else (GRAY, GRAY_ALPHA, or palette_lookup/quantize_lookup NULL) -> no-op. In particular `full_quantize=1` on a PALETTE row does nothing at row time (only the palette was reordered in the setter), and `full_quantize=0` on an RGB row does nothing.\n\n## 5.16 pngtrans.c workers used by the read path\n- `png_do_strip_channel` (pngtrans.c:522-641): branches on `row_info->channels == 2` x {bit_depth 8, 16, other -> return} and `channels == 4` x {8, 16, other -> return}; `channels` anything else -> return (\"the filler channel has gone already\"). `at_start` selects which end is dropped (read always passes 0). Sets `pixel_depth` to 8/16/24/48, `channels` to 1/3, clears MASK_ALPHA from color_type if it was GRAY_ALPHA/RGB_ALPHA, and `rowbytes = dp - row`.\n- `png_do_invert` (pngtrans.c:290-339): `GRAY` (all bytes inverted, any bit depth including 1/2/4 — the bit-depth test was removed in 1.0.13); `GRAY_ALPHA && bit_depth == 8` (every other byte); `GRAY_ALPHA && bit_depth == 16` (first two of each four). Anything else -> no-op. Note GRAY at 2/4 bpp inverts full bytes, which is correct only if the row is exactly packed.\n- `png_do_swap` (pngtrans.c:347-370): `bit_depth == 16` only, `width*channels` swaps.\n- `png_do_packswap` (pngtrans.c:483-511): `bit_depth < 8`, table selected by depth 1/2/4, else return.\n- `png_do_bgr` (pngtrans.c:648-720): `(color_type & MASK_COLOR)`; then {8, 16} x {RGB, RGB_ALPHA}; other combinations -> no-op.\n- `png_do_check_palette_indexes` (pngtrans.c:728+): only when `num_palette < (1 << bit_depth) && num_palette > 0`; switch on bit_depth 1/2/4/8 with a `padding` computation; updates `num_palette_max`.\n\n---\n\n# 6. Gamma table construction (png.c)\n\n## `png_build_gamma_table` (png.c:3615-3763)\n- If `gamma_table != NULL || gamma_16_table != NULL` -> `png_warning(\"gamma table being rebuilt\")` + `png_destroy_gamma_table`. Reachable by calling `png_read_update_info` twice (legal since 1.5.6) — a distinct assertion row.\n- `file_gamma = png_ptr->file_gamma`, `screen_gamma = png_ptr->screen_gamma` (both already normalised by `png_init_gamma_values`); `file_to_linear = png_reciprocal(file_gamma)`.\n- `screen_gamma > 0` -> `linear_to_screen = png_reciprocal(screen_gamma)`, `correction = png_reciprocal2(screen_gamma, file_gamma)`. Else -> `linear_to_screen = file_gamma`, `correction = PNG_FP_1`.\n- `GAMMA_TRANSFORMS` is 1 when any of READ_BACKGROUND / READ_ALPHA_MODE / READ_RGB_TO_GRAY is compiled (3607-3613).\n\n**`bit_depth <= 8` (3663-3676):**\n- Always `png_build_8bit_table(&gamma_table, correction)`.\n- If `transformations & (PNG_COMPOSE | PNG_RGB_TO_GRAY)` -> also `gamma_to_1` (with `file_to_linear`) and `gamma_from_1` (with `linear_to_screen`). Otherwise those stay NULL — which is exactly what makes `png_do_compose`/`png_do_rgb_to_gray`/`png_do_encode_alpha` take their \"no tables\" branches. **ENCODE_ALPHA alone does not build `gamma_from_1`**, hence the `png_do_encode_alpha` warning path.\n\n**`bit_depth == 16` (3678-3761, `#ifdef PNG_16BIT_SUPPORTED`):**\n- `sig_bit` = max(sig_bit.red, green, blue) if colour, else sig_bit.gray.\n- `shift = (16 - sig_bit) & 0xff` if `0 < sig_bit < 16`, else `0`.\n- If `transformations & (PNG_16_TO_8 | PNG_SCALE_16_TO_8)`: `shift = max(shift, 16 - PNG_MAX_GAMMA_8)` = `max(shift, 5)` since `PNG_MAX_GAMMA_8 == 11` (pnglibconf.h:215).\n- `shift = min(shift, 8)` (\"guarantees at least one table\"). Stored as `png_ptr->gamma_shift`.\n- Main table: if `(PNG_16_TO_8 | PNG_SCALE_16_TO_8)` -> `png_build_16to8_table(&gamma_16_table, shift, png_reciprocal(correction))` (note the *inverse* correction — png.c:3457-3518, boundary-value construction, 255 output steps of 257 plus a final fill with 65535); else `png_build_16bit_table(&gamma_16_table, shift, correction)`.\n- If `GAMMA_TRANSFORMS && (PNG_COMPOSE | PNG_RGB_TO_GRAY)`: `gamma_16_to_1` with `file_to_linear` and `gamma_16_from_1` with `linear_to_screen`, both via `png_build_16bit_table` at the same `shift`.\n\nDistinct axes: bit depth 8 vs 16; sBIT present/absent and its value (drives `shift`, hence the number of sub-tables `1 << (8-shift)`); 16_TO_8/SCALE_16_TO_8 present (switches to the 16to8 table *and* floors shift at 5); COMPOSE|RGB_TO_GRAY present (the to_1/from_1 pair); rebuild-warning.\n\n## `png_build_16bit_table` (png.c:3380-3452)\n`num = 1 << (8 - shift)` sub-tables of 256 entries. Per sub-table, one branch:\n- `png_gamma_significant(gamma_val) != 0` -> compute `pow`. With `PNG_FLOATING_ARITHMETIC_SUPPORTED` it inlines the max-scaling as `floor(65535*pow(ig*fmax, gamma*1e-5)+.5)`; otherwise it rescales `ig = (ig*65535 + max_by_2)/max` when `shift != 0` and calls `png_gamma_16bit_correct`. **These two build modes give slightly different tables** — a build-config axis.\n- Not significant -> identity-ish fast fill (`ig`, rescaled if `shift != 0`).\n\n## `png_build_8bit_table` (png.c:3525-3539)\nSingle 256-byte table; `png_gamma_significant(gamma_val) != 0` -> `png_gamma_8bit_correct(i, gamma_val)`; else identity `i & 0xff`. So a table can exist and be the identity — relevant because all the row code tests only `!= NULL`, never significance.\n\n---\n\n# 7. `png_set_crc_action` (pngrtran.c:40-106)\n\n`png_ptr == NULL` -> return. Two independent switches; note both are outside `PNG_READ_TRANSFORMS_SUPPORTED` and outside `png_rtran_ok`, so this is callable at any time.\n\n**crit_action (49-75)** — masks `PNG_FLAG_CRC_CRITICAL_MASK`:\n| value | branch | resulting flags |\n|---|---|---|\n| `PNG_CRC_NO_CHANGE` (5) | `break` | untouched |\n| `PNG_CRC_WARN_USE` (3) | clear mask, set `PNG_FLAG_CRC_CRITICAL_USE` | warn + use data |\n| `PNG_CRC_QUIET_USE` (4) | clear mask, set `CRITICAL_USE \\| CRITICAL_IGNORE` | quiet + use data |\n| `PNG_CRC_WARN_DISCARD` (2) | `png_warning(\"Can't discard critical data on CRC error\")` then **FALLTHROUGH** | clear mask (= error/quit) |\n| `PNG_CRC_ERROR_QUIT` (1) | falls into default | clear mask |\n| `PNG_CRC_DEFAULT` (0) / anything else | default | clear mask |\n\n**ancil_action (78-105)** — masks `PNG_FLAG_CRC_ANCILLARY_MASK`:\n| value | branch | resulting flags |\n|---|---|---|\n| `PNG_CRC_NO_CHANGE` (5) | `break` | untouched |\n| `PNG_CRC_WARN_USE` (3) | clear mask, set `ANCILLARY_USE` | warn + use |\n| `PNG_CRC_QUIET_USE` (4) | clear mask, set `ANCILLARY_USE \\| ANCILLARY_NOWARN` | quiet + use |\n| `PNG_CRC_ERROR_QUIT` (1) | clear mask, set `ANCILLARY_NOWARN` | error/quit |\n| `PNG_CRC_WARN_DISCARD` (2) | falls into default | clear mask |\n| `PNG_CRC_DEFAULT` (0) / anything else | default | clear mask |\n\nSo: 6 x 6 = 36 combinations but only 5 distinct crit states (NO_CHANGE, WARN_USE, QUIET_USE, and the three that collapse to \"clear\") x 5 distinct ancil states. Note the asymmetry: `ERROR_QUIT` is a distinct state for ancillary (sets NOWARN) but collapses to the default for critical; `WARN_DISCARD` warns for critical but is silent for ancillary.\n\n---\n\n# 8. Additional distinct configuration axes in this file\n\n1. **`png_ptr->default_gamma` pre-set** (pngrtran.c:381-386): if non-zero on entry to `png_set_alpha_mode_fixed`, the file gamma default is *not* recomputed. Set by the simplified read API. Interacts with `png_resolve_file_gamma` precedence rank 3.\n2. **`chunk_gamma`** (from gAMA/sRGB) vs **`file_gamma`** (from `png_set_gamma`) vs **`default_gamma`** vs **`1/screen_gamma`** — four-way precedence in `png_resolve_file_gamma` (1351-1385), each a distinct row, plus the all-unset row where both are forced to PNG_FP_1.\n3. **`png_gamma_threshold` overflow row** (1162): a `png_muldiv` overflow forces PNG_GAMMA on even for nominally reciprocal gammas.\n4. **`png_ptr->sig_bit`** (sBIT chunk) affects two separate things: the 16-bit gamma table `shift` (png.c:3682-3733) and the palette SHIFT fast path (2025-2060) / `png_do_unshift` (2409-2421). sBIT present/absent is an axis for every gamma-16 row.\n5. **The `PNG_INVERT_ALPHA` early palette inversion** (1245-1257): whether tRNS is inverted in `png_init_palette_transformations` or per row depends on `PNG_EXPAND_tRNS`, and requires `BACKGROUND_EXPAND && EXPAND`. Three-way axis.\n6. **`background.index`** is only consulted for palette + `BACKGROUND_EXPAND` + `EXPAND` (1237-1242); out-of-range index reads past valid palette entries within the 256-entry array.\n7. **Repeated `png_read_update_info` calls**: triggers the \"gamma table being rebuilt\" warning (png.c:3634) and the \"duplicate call\" `png_app_error` (pngread.c:190/214).\n8. **`PNG_INTERLACE`** (`png_set_interlace_handling`, pngtrans.c:127-138) returns 7 vs 1 and sets `PNG_INTERLACE`; it isn't in the pngrtran order list but changes `num_rows`/`iwidth` in `png_read_start_row` (pngrutil.c:4419-4430) and therefore every rowbytes assertion.\n9. **`png_ptr->num_palette_max` / `png_set_check_for_invalid_index`** — gate at 5117, an axis orthogonal to the transforms.\n10. **NEON / RISC-V RVV build axes** (pngrtran.c:20-35, 4457-4469, 4494-4499, 4915-4926): `PNG_ARM_NEON_INTRINSICS_AVAILABLE` changes `png_do_expand_palette` into a hybrid (`riffled_palette` allocated 256*4 and only when `png_ptr->bit_depth == 8 && num_trans > 0`). Numerically identical but a distinct path.\n11. **`PNG_FLOATING_ARITHMETIC_SUPPORTED`** changes the 16-bit gamma table values (png.c:3421-3433).\n12. **`PNG_READ_16BIT_SUPPORTED` off** forces `PNG_16_TO_8` (or SCALE) on in `png_read_transform_info` (2161-2176) and removes the 16-bit halves of nearly every worker.\n13. **`PNG_USER_TRANSFORM_PTR_SUPPORTED`** gates the `user_transform_depth`/`channels` overrides in both `png_read_transform_info` (2254-2264) and the row function (5164-5170).\n14. **`png_set_rgb_to_gray` accumulation**: because the error_action bits are ORed, calling it twice with WARN then ERROR yields the combined `PNG_RGB_TO_GRAY` mask, which is action *NONE* at row time — a surprising but testable behaviour.\n15. **`png_set_background` + `png_set_strip_alpha` + `png_set_expand`**: STRIP_ALPHA is auto-set by set_background, so an explicit `png_set_strip_alpha` is redundant, but an explicit `png_set_strip_alpha` *without* background hits the `num_trans = 0` path (1510) which then flips `png_read_transform_info`'s palette-EXPAND result from RGBA to RGB — the single most consequential flag interaction in the file.\n16. **Width parity**: `png_do_unpack`, `png_do_expand`, `png_do_expand_palette` all compute their starting `shift` from `width & mask`, and the depth-4 `else` clauses differ between them (`shift = 4` at 2367/4612 vs `shift += 4` at 4433). Test widths that are and aren't multiples of 8/4/2.\n17. **width 0 / width 1**: the `for (i = 1; i < row_width; i++)` + trailing store pattern in the FILLER_AFTER paths (2837-2842 etc.) behaves differently from the BEFORE paths at width 0.","usage":{"total_tokens":139283,"tool_uses":21,"duration_ms":407946},"uuid":"a526d3d8-1ec3-4acb-aac5-e38ba0138755","session_id":"e3091878-9949-4689-ad83-c0c41e6641a2"}
