{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_ik4gql3p4x5zp5bsizr6d37wbucxf37ymujy3rjsygtxwml5chdq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have everything. Here is the full configuration-surface enumeration.\n\n---\n\n# libpng 1.6.59 (`.git`/BETA build) — configuration surface test matrix\n\n## 0. Build facts that gate everything below\n\nFrom `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-kk7Dev/c_src/include/pnglibconf.h`:\n\n| Setting | Line | Consequence for this matrix |\n|---|---|---|\n| `PNG_DEFAULT_READ_MACROS 1` | 207 | `PNG_USE_READ_MACROS` gets defined for every TU that includes `pngconf.h` (see `pngconf.h:74-78`) |\n| `PNG_READ_INT_FUNCTIONS_SUPPORTED` | 69 | the *function* forms of `png_get_uint_32/16/int_32` **are still compiled and exported** |\n| `PNG_WRITE_INT_FUNCTIONS_SUPPORTED` | 147 | `png_save_uint_32/16` compiled |\n| `PNG_SAVE_INT_32_SUPPORTED` | 110 | `png_save_int_32` compiled |\n| **`PNG_FLOATING_ARITHMETIC_SUPPORTED`** | 35 | **the 64-bit integer `png_muldiv` path, `png_product2`, `png_log8bit`, `png_log16bit`, `png_exp`, `png_exp8bit`, `png_exp16bit` are all `#ifndef`-ed OUT of this build** |\n| `PNG_FLOATING_POINT_SUPPORTED` + `PNG_FIXED_POINT_SUPPORTED` | 36, 34 | both float and `_fixed` API variants exist |\n| `PNG_USER_MEM_SUPPORTED` | 131 | `malloc_fn`/`free_fn` branches live; `png_malloc_default`/`png_free_default` exist |\n| `PNG_ALIGNED_MEMORY_SUPPORTED` | 19 | **no effect on pngmem.c at all.** Only referenced at `pngrutil.c:4618` and as a gate for hardware optimizations in `pngpriv.h:139,175,185`. Zero test rows needed for it in the memory area |\n| `PNG_SETJMP_SUPPORTED` | 113 | `png_set_longjmp_fn`, `png_free_jmpbuf`, `png_longjmp` real bodies |\n| `PNG_BENIGN_ERRORS_SUPPORTED` + `PNG_BENIGN_READ_ERRORS_SUPPORTED` | 22, 23 | `png_benign_error`/`png_app_error`/`png_app_warning` compiled |\n| `PNG_WARNINGS_SUPPORTED`, `PNG_ERROR_TEXT_SUPPORTED` | 134, 33 | `png_error` (not `png_err`) and `png_warning` real bodies |\n| `PNG_16BIT_SUPPORTED` | 18 | 16-bit branches in `png_do_invert`, `png_do_bgr`, `png_gamma_16bit_correct` live |\n| `PNG_GAMMA_THRESHOLD_FIXED 5000` | 208 | `png_gamma_significant` boundary = 95000 / 105000 |\n| `PNG_sCAL_PRECISION 5` | 230 | precision passed to `png_ascii_from_fp` by `png_set_sCAL` |\n| `PNG_MNG_FEATURES_SUPPORTED` | 49 | extra `png_check_IHDR` filter-method branch |\n| `PNG_USER_WIDTH_MAX/HEIGHT_MAX = 1000000` | 223, 224 | `png_check_IHDR` / `png_set_user_limits` defaults |\n| `PNG_USER_CHUNK_CACHE_MAX 1000`, `PNG_USER_CHUNK_MALLOC_MAX 8000000` | 221, 222 | `png_create_png_struct` defaults |\n\n**Additional critical fact:** `png.h:310` sets `PNG_LIBPNG_BUILD_BASE_TYPE = PNG_LIBPNG_BUILD_BETA` (2) and `pngpriv.h:447` defines `PNG_RELEASE_BUILD = (BASE_TYPE >= PNG_LIBPNG_BUILD_RC)` → **`PNG_RELEASE_BUILD == 0`**. Therefore in `png_create_read_struct_2` (`pngread.c:60-68`) only `PNG_FLAG_BENIGN_ERRORS_WARN` is set; `PNG_FLAG_APP_WARNINGS_WARN` is **not**. So on a freshly created *read* struct, `png_app_warning`/`png_app_error` are fatal, `png_benign_error` warns. On a freshly created *write* struct no flag is set, so all three are fatal.\n\n**Big structural surprise for area 3:** `png_colorspace_set_chromaticities`, `png_colorspace_set_endpoints`, `png_colorspace_set_sRGB`, `png_colorspace_set_gamma`, `png_colorspace_sync`, `png_colorspace_endpoints_match` **do not exist in this tree** (verified by grep across `src/` and `include/`; only a stale comment at `pngwrite.c:122` mentions `png_colorspace_sync_info`). The colorspace machinery was refactored: `png_struct::chromaticities` (`pngstruct.h:274`) plus `have_chromaticities()` and `png_set_rgb_coefficients()` replace it. Do **not** write rows for the `png_colorspace_*` names.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Byte access\n\n### Functions vs macros in this build\n- `png.h:2817-2848`: because `PNG_USE_READ_MACROS` is on, callers get the macros:\n  - `PNG_get_uint_32(buf)` — `png.h:2822-2826`\n  - `PNG_get_uint_16(buf)` — `png.h:2831-2834`\n  - `PNG_get_int_32(buf)`  — `png.h:2836-2839`\n  - aliased to the public names at `png.h:2845-2847`.\n- The function bodies survive because `pngrutil.c:56-59` parenthesizes the names: `png_uint_32 (PNGAPI png_get_uint_32)(...)`. Confirmed exported in `build/libpng.so`: `png_get_uint_32`, `png_get_int_32`, `png_get_uint_16`, `png_get_uint_31`, `png_save_uint_32`, `png_save_uint_16`, `png_save_int_32` are all `T` symbols.\n- **Test consequence:** a test that `#include <png.h>` and calls `png_get_uint_32(buf)` exercises the **macro**, not the function. To hit the function you must either `#define PNG_NO_USE_READ_MACROS` before including `png.h`, or take the address / call through `(png_get_uint_32)(buf)`. Worth two rows per function (macro form vs function form) since the macro and function are textually different implementations.\n- `png_get_uint_31` and `png_save_*` are **always functions** (no macro form exists).\n\n### `png_get_uint_32` — `pngrutil.c:62-72`\nStraight-line, no branches. Equivalence classes (by output regime):\n1. all-zero buffer → 0\n2. `00 00 00 01` → 1 (LSB placement / endianness check)\n3. `01 00 00 00` → 0x01000000 (MSB placement)\n4. `7f ff ff ff` → `PNG_UINT_31_MAX`\n5. `80 00 00 00` → 0x80000000 (top bit set, still unsigned)\n6. `ff ff ff ff` → 0xffffffff\n7. `de ad be ef` → arbitrary mixed value\n\n### `png_get_uint_16` — `pngrutil.c:97-110`\n1. `00 00` → 0\n2. `00 01` → 1\n3. `01 00` → 0x0100\n4. `7f ff` → 0x7fff\n5. `80 00` → 0x8000 (top bit; note the internal `unsigned int val` then narrowing cast)\n6. `ff ff` → 0xffff\n\n### `png_get_uint_31` — `pngrutil.c:40-49` (needs `png_ptr`)\nGuard: `if (uval > PNG_UINT_31_MAX) png_error(...)` at line 45-46.\n1. `uval == 0` → returns 0, no error\n2. `0 < uval < 0x7fffffff` → returns value\n3. `uval == 0x7fffffff` (== `PNG_UINT_31_MAX`) → **boundary, returns, no error**\n4. `uval == 0x80000000` → `png_error(\"PNG unsigned integer out of range\")` — first failing value\n5. `uval == 0xffffffff` → same error\nRows 4-5 require a `png_ptr` with a working `jmp_buf`/error_fn. (You said valid inputs only, but this is a *documented* branch of the function and requires the error path to observe; treat 4/5 as one \"error branch\" row plus the boundary row 3.)\n\n### `png_get_int_32` — `pngrutil.c:79-94`\nThree distinct return statements:\n1. **non-negative branch** (`line 83-84`, `(uval & 0x80000000) == 0`): inputs `00 00 00 00` → 0; `00 00 00 01` → 1; `7f ff ff ff` → 2147483647\n2. **negative branch** (`line 86-88`): `ff ff ff ff` → -1; `ff ff ff fe` → -2; `80 00 00 01` → -2147483647 (largest magnitude representable)\n3. **overflow/`0x80000000` branch** (`line 89-93`): input `80 00 00 00`. `uval = (0x80000000 ^ 0xffffffff)+1 = 0x80000000`, top bit still set → **returns 0** (documented \"0 is the most safe value\"). This is the row you specifically asked for.\nNote the *macro* `PNG_get_int_32` (`png.h:2836-2839`) masks with `&0x7fffffffU` and therefore returns **0** for `80 00 00 00` too, but by a different route — worth asserting both agree on this input.\n\n### `png_save_uint_32` — `pngwutil.c:40-47`\n1. `0` → `00 00 00 00`\n2. `1` → `00 00 00 01`\n3. `0x01000000` → `01 00 00 00`\n4. `0x7fffffff`\n5. `0x80000000`\n6. `0xffffffff`\n7. round-trip identity with `png_get_uint_32`\n\n### `png_save_uint_16` — `pngwutil.c:53-58`\nParameter is `unsigned int`, not `png_uint_16`:\n1. `0`, 2. `1`, 3. `0x0100`, 4. `0x7fff`, 5. `0x8000`, 6. `0xffff`\n7. **`0x10000` / `0x12345`** — values > 16 bits: the `& 0xffU` masks silently truncate. Distinct input class worth a row (documents truncation behavior).\n\n### `png_save_int_32` — `png.c:730-734`\nDelegates to `png_save_uint_32((png_uint_32)i)`.\n1. `0`\n2. `+1`\n3. `-1` → `ff ff ff ff`\n4. `INT32_MIN` (`-2147483648`) → `80 00 00 00`\n5. `INT32_MAX` → `7f ff ff ff`\n6. round-trip: `png_save_int_32` then `png_get_int_32`, all of the above except `INT32_MIN` (which round-trips to 0 through the function form — an interesting asymmetry row).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Fixed-point / arithmetic\n\n### `png_muldiv` — `png.c:2769-2882`\nSignature `int png_muldiv(png_fixed_point_p res, png_fixed_point a, png_int_32 times, png_int_32 divisor)`; returns 1 on success, **0 on divide-by-zero or overflow**.\n\nBranches present in *this* build:\n1. **`divisor == 0`** → falls through to `return 0` at line 2881. `*res` untouched. (`a`, `times` arbitrary.)\n2. **`a == 0`** (line 2776) → `*res = 0`, return 1. Distinct short-circuit — does not touch the arithmetic.\n3. **`times == 0`** (same test) → `*res = 0`, return 1.\n4. **`a == 0 && times == 0 && divisor == 0`** → divisor check wins → returns 0. Ordering row.\n5. **floating path success** (lines 2783-2794): `double r = a; r *= times; r /= divisor; r = floor(r+.5);` then range test `r <= 2147483647. && r >= -2147483648.`. Sub-classes:\n   - exact division, positive: `(100000, 100000, 100000)` → 100000\n   - **rounding up**: `(1, 1, 2)` → `floor(0.5+0.5)` = 1 (round-half-up, not banker's)\n   - **rounding of negative**: `(-1, 1, 2)` → `floor(-0.5+0.5)` = 0 — note `floor` gives **round-half-toward-+inf**, so -0.5 → 0 not -1. This asymmetry is a must-test row.\n   - `(-3, 1, 2)` → `floor(-1.0)` = -1 ; `(-1,1,3)` → `floor(0.1666)` = 0\n   - large-but-in-range: `(2147483647, 1, 1)` → 2147483647 (upper boundary, returns 1)\n   - `(-2147483648 …)` — note `png_fixed_point` is signed 32-bit, so `a = INT32_MIN` is representable; `(INT32_MIN, 1, 1)` → `r = -2147483648.` → `>= -2147483648.` is true → returns 1 with `*res = INT32_MIN`. Lower boundary row.\n6. **floating path overflow → return 0** (falls out of the `if` at 2790 to line 2881):\n   - `(2147483647, 2, 1)` → r ≈ 4.29e9 > 2^31-1 → **returns 0**\n   - `(-2147483647, 2, 1)` → r ≈ -4.29e9 → **returns 0**\n   - `(2147483647, 2147483647, 1)` → returns 0\n   - **just-over boundary**: `(2147483647, 1, 1)` succeeds but `(2147483648-equivalent)` cannot be expressed; use `(1073741824, 2, 1)` → r = 2147483648 → `> 2147483647.` → **returns 0**. Cleanest \"one past the top\" row.\n   - negative just-over: `(-1073741824, 2, 1)` → r = -2147483648 → **succeeds** (boundary inclusive). Pair with `(-1073741825, 2, 1)` → r = -2147483650 → returns 0.\n7. **`divisor < 0`** → sign propagates through the double division; `(100000, 100000, -100000)` → -100000.\n8. **`times < 0`** → `(100000, -1, 1)` → -100000.\n9. **both negative** → positive result.\n\n**The 32-bit/64-bit integer path (`png.c:2796-2876`) is NOT compiled** in this build (`#else` of the `#ifdef PNG_FLOATING_ARITHMETIC_SUPPORTED` at 2783). If you want rows for the `s16`/`s32`/`s00` bit-shift-division, the carry (`if (s00 < s16) ++s32`, line 2829), the 32-iteration restoring-division loop (2841-2860), the `if (s00 >= (D >> 1)) ++result` rounding (2863), and the sign-check overflow test (2870-2871), you must rebuild with `PNG_NO_FLOATING_ARITHMETIC`. Otherwise mark those as unreachable-in-config.\n\n### `png_muldiv_warn`\n**Does not exist in this tree.** grep finds no definition and no declaration. Drop this row (it existed in older 1.5.x-era pngpriv.h).\n\n### `png_reciprocal` — `png.c:2885-2901`\nFloat path only (2888-2892): `r = floor(1E10/a + .5)`.\n1. `a == PNG_FP_1` (100000) → 100000\n2. `a == 200000` (2.0) → 50000\n3. `a == 50000` (0.5) → 200000\n4. `a == 1` → 1E10 → **out of range → returns 0** (overflow)\n5. `a == 5` → 2E9 > 2^31-1 → **returns 0**\n6. `a == 5000` → 2000000 → OK\n7. `a == 0` → division by zero in `double` → **`inf`**, `floor(inf)` = inf, range test fails → returns 0. Note: this is *not* a trapping division, it's IEEE inf. Distinct row (in the non-float build it would go through `png_muldiv` and return 0 via divisor!=0 check).\n8. `a < 0`: `a == -100000` → -100000; `a == -1` → -1E10 → returns 0\n9. rounding: `a == 300000` → `floor(33333.33+0.5)` = 33333\n10. `a == INT32_MAX` → `floor(4.65+0.5)` = 5\n\n### `png_reciprocal2` — `png.c:2951-2978`\nFloat path (2955-2964): guarded `if (a != 0 && b != 0)`, then `r = 1E15/a; r /= b; r = floor(r+.5)`.\n1. **`a == 0`** → skips block → returns 0\n2. **`b == 0`** → returns 0\n3. `a == b == PNG_FP_1` → 1E15/1e5/1e5 = 100000\n4. `a = 100000, b = 200000` → 50000\n5. `a = 45455, b = 220000` → typical sRGB file×screen gamma product; asserts no overflow\n6. **overflow**: `a = 1, b = 1` → 1E15 → returns 0\n7. `a = 100000, b = 1` → 1E10 → returns 0\n8. smallest non-overflowing: `a*b >= 465661` in fixed units; e.g. `a = 1000, b = 1000` → 1E15/1e6 = 1E9 → OK (1000000000). `a = 500, b = 1000` → 2E9 → returns 0. Boundary pair.\n9. negative `a` and/or `b` → sign combinations (4 rows: ++, +-, -+, --)\n10. rounding: any `a*b` not dividing 1E15 evenly.\n`png_product2` (`png.c:2929-2948`) is only used by the non-float `png_reciprocal2`; **not compiled here**.\n\n### `png_gamma_significant` — `png.c:2908-2925`\n`return gamma_val < PNG_FP_1 - 5000 || gamma_val > PNG_FP_1 + 5000;` → `< 95000 || > 105000`.\n1. `100000` (1.0) → **0** (not significant)\n2. `95000` → 0 (boundary, `<` is strict)\n3. `94999` → 1 (first significant below)\n4. `105000` → 0 (boundary)\n5. `105001` → 1 (first significant above)\n6. `45455` (1/2.2, sRGB) → 1\n7. `220000` (2.2) → 1\n8. `0` → 1\n9. negative, e.g. `-100000` → 1\n10. `PNG_GAMMA_sRGB_INVERSE` constant from png.h → 1\n\n### `png_gamma_8bit_correct` — `png.c:3272-3317`\nGuard `if (value > 0 && value < 255)` (line 3275), float path at 3302 `floor(255*pow(value/255., gamma*.00001)+.5)`.\n1. **`value == 0`** → skips, returns `(png_byte)(0 & 0xff)` = 0\n2. **`value == 255`** → skips, returns 255\n3. **`value > 255`** (e.g. 256, 511) → skips the `if`, returns `value & 0xff` → 0, 255. Documented masking behavior; distinct row\n4. `value == 128, gamma == 100000` → identity, 128\n5. `value == 128, gamma == 45455` → ≈186 (encode)\n6. `value == 128, gamma == 220000` → ≈56 (decode)\n7. `value == 1, gamma == 45455` → smallest non-trivial\n8. `value == 254, gamma == 220000` → largest non-trivial\n9. **`gamma == 0`** → `pow(x, 0)` = 1 → `floor(255+.5)` = 255 for any value in (0,255). Degenerate-but-valid row\n10. `gamma` very large (e.g. 10000000 = 100.0) → underflows to 0 for small values\n11. `gamma` negative (e.g. -100000) → `pow(x, -1)` > 1 → the `(png_byte)r` cast **truncates modulo 256** (no clamp!). e.g. `value=1, gamma=-100000` → `r = 255*255 = 65025` → `(png_byte)65025` = 0x01. Undefined-ish but reachable; worth a row documenting it.\nThe `png_log8bit`/`png_exp8bit` alternative (`png.c:3305-3312`, including the `png_muldiv` overflow → `value = 0` fallthrough) is **not compiled**.\n\n### `png_gamma_16bit_correct` — `png.c:3320-3348`\nGuard `value > 0 && value < 65535`; `#ifdef PNG_16BIT_SUPPORTED` is satisfied.\n1. `value == 0` → 0\n2. `value == 65535` → 65535\n3. `value == 32768, gamma == 100000` → identity\n4. `value == 32768, gamma == 45455` → ≈47582\n5. `value == 32768, gamma == 220000` → ≈7135\n6. `value == 1`, `value == 65534` (extremes inside the range)\n7. `value > 65535` (e.g. 65536, 70000) → skips guard, `return (png_uint_16)value` → truncation row\n8. `gamma == 0` → 65535\n9. negative gamma → truncating cast row (as above)\nNote the cast at line 3331 is `(png_int_32)value` (not `(int)`), a deliberate difference from the 8-bit version.\n\n### `png_gamma_correct` — `png.c:3355-3369`\nDispatch on `png_ptr->bit_depth`:\n1. `bit_depth == 8` → `png_gamma_8bit_correct`\n2. `bit_depth == 16` → `png_gamma_16bit_correct`\n3. `bit_depth == 1/2/4` → **falls into the `else`** (line 3363), i.e. goes to the **16-bit** routine. Not a typo in your matrix — the function only special-cases 8. Worth a row: `bit_depth=4, value=15` returns `png_gamma_16bit_correct(15, g)`, not the 4-bit answer.\nThis is `PRIVATE` and not exported (`nm` shows no `png_gamma_correct`); reach it only via internal linkage or the gamma-table builders.\n\n### `png_fixed` — `png.c:2725-2738`\n`r = floor(100000*fp + .5)`; `if (r > 2147483647. || r < -2147483648.) png_fixed_error(...)` (which is a `png_error`, `pngerror.c:517-535`).\n1. `fp == 0.0` → 0\n2. `fp == 1.0` → 100000\n3. `fp == 0.45455` → 45455\n4. `fp == -1.0` → -100000\n5. rounding up: `fp == 0.000005` → `floor(0.5+0.5)` = 1\n6. rounding down: `fp == 0.0000049` → 0\n7. negative rounding: `fp == -0.000005` → `floor(-0.5+0.5)` = 0 (asymmetric with 5!)\n8. upper boundary OK: `fp == 21474.83647` → 2147483647\n9. **overflow high**: `fp == 21475.0` → `png_fixed_error(\"...\")` → `png_error`\n10. **overflow low**: `fp == -21475.0` → `png_error`\n11. lower boundary: `fp == -21474.83648` → `r = -2147483648.` → `< -2147483648.` is **false** → succeeds. Boundary-inclusive row.\n12. `fp == NaN` / `fp == inf` — both comparisons false for NaN → passes the guard → UB cast. Note but probably skip.\n\n### `png_fixed_ITU` — `png.c:2744-2757` (**present**, added for cLLI/mDCV)\n`r = floor(10000*fp + .5)`; error if `r > 2147483647. || r < 0` — note **`< 0`, not `< -2^31`**: negatives are always an error.\n1. `fp == 0.0` → 0 (boundary; `r == 0` is not `< 0`)\n2. `fp == 1.0` → 10000\n3. `fp == 100.0` → 1000000 (typical maxCLL)\n4. rounding: `fp == 0.00005` → 1 ; `fp == 0.000049` → 0\n5. **any negative**: `fp == -0.0001` → `r = floor(-1+0.5) = -1` → `png_error`\n6. **`fp == -0.00001`** → `r = floor(-0.1+0.5) = 0` → **succeeds, returns 0**. Sneaky boundary row.\n7. upper boundary: `fp == 214748.3647` → 2147483647\n8. overflow: `fp == 214749.0` → `png_error`\nReturn type is `png_uint_32`, not `png_fixed_point` — distinct from `png_fixed`.\n\n### Not compiled in this configuration (mark unreachable)\n`png_product2` (`png.c:2929-2948`), `png_log8bit` (`3047`), `png_log16bit` (`3104`), `png_exp` (`3196`), `png_exp8bit` (`3244`), `png_exp16bit` (`3259`) — all inside the `#ifndef PNG_FLOATING_ARITHMETIC_SUPPORTED` blocks starting at `png.c:2927` and `png.c:2982` and ending at `png.c:3270`. Confirmed absent from `build/libpng.so`.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Colourspace math\n\n### `png_xy_from_XYZ` — `png.c:1136-1200` (exported: yes)\nReturns **0 on success, 1 on error**. Ten sequential failure points; each is a distinct row.\n1. success: sRGB-ish XYZ, e.g. `red_X=41239, red_Y=21264, red_Z=1933; green=(35758,71517,11919); blue=(18048,7219,95053)` → returns 0, `xy` ≈ sRGB chromaticities\n2. `png_safe_add(&d, red_Y, red_Z)` overflow (line 1146) → 1. Force with `red_X = red_Y = red_Z = 0x7fffffff`\n3. `png_muldiv(&xy->redx, red_X, PNG_FP_1, dred) == 0` (1149) → 1. Force with `dred` such that `red_X*100000/dred > 2^31`, e.g. `red_X = 2000000, red_Y = red_Z = 0` → `dred = 2000000`, quotient = 100000, fine; instead use `red_X = 100000, red_Y = 0, red_Z = 0` → `dred = 100000` → 100000, fine. Simplest forced failure: `red_X = 100000, red_Y = -100000, red_Z = 0` → `dred = 0` → **divide-by-zero in muldiv → returns 0 → outer returns 1**\n4. `xy->redy` muldiv failure (1151)\n5. green block: safe_add overflow (1155), `greenx` muldiv (1158), `greeny` muldiv (1160) — 3 rows\n6. blue block: safe_add (1164), `bluex` (1167), `bluey` (1169) — 3 rows\n7. `dwhite` safe_add of `dblue + dred + dgreen` overflow (1177) → 1. Easy to force with three large components\n8. `whiteX` safe_add (1185) and `whiteY` safe_add (1190) → 2 rows\n9. `xy->whitex` muldiv (1194) and `xy->whitey` muldiv (1196) → 2 rows. Force `dwhite == 0` by making red/green/blue sums cancel\n10. all-zero XYZ: every `d` is 0, `dred == 0` → first `png_muldiv` divisor 0 → returns 1. Clean \"degenerate input\" row\n11. round-trip: `png_XYZ_from_xy(xyz, xy)` then `png_xy_from_XYZ(xy2, xyz)` → `xy2 ≈ xy` within rounding. High-value row.\n\n### `png_XYZ_from_xy` — `png.c:1202-1496`\nReturns 0 on success, 1 on error. `fpLimit = PNG_FP_1 + PNG_FP_1/10 = 110000` (line 1222).\n\n**Range-check branches (lines 1223-1230), 8 pairs = 16 distinct failure rows + boundary rows:**\n| Check | Line | Fail-low | Fail-high | Boundary-OK |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `redx < 0 \\|\\| redx > 110000` | 1223 | `redx = -1` | `redx = 110001` | `redx = 0`, `redx = 110000` |\n| `redy < 0 \\|\\| redy > 110000-redx` | 1224 | `redy = -1` | `redx=64000, redy=46001` | `redx=64000, redy=46000` |\n| `greenx` | 1225 | same shape | | |\n| `greeny < 0 \\|\\| > 110000-greenx` | 1226 | | | |\n| `bluex` | 1227 | | | |\n| `bluey < 0 \\|\\| > 110000-bluex` | 1228 | | | |\n| `whitex` | 1229 | | | |\n| **`whitey < 5`** \\|\\| `> 110000-whitex` | 1230 | `whitey = 4` → **fail**; `whitey = 5` → **pass** (deliberate anti-overflow floor, documented at 1212-1216) | | |\n\n**Arithmetic branches after the range checks (lines 1413-1465):**\n1. `png_muldiv(&left, greenx-bluex, redy-bluey, 8)` fails (1422) → 1\n2. `png_muldiv(&right, greeny-bluey, redx-bluex, 8)` fails (1424) → 1\n3. `denominator = png_fp_sub(left, right, &error)`; `if (error) return 1` (1427-1428) → overflow in the subtract\n4. red-numerator `left` muldiv fails (1431) → 1\n5. red-numerator `right` muldiv fails (1433) → 1\n6. `png_muldiv(&red_inverse, whitey, denominator, png_fp_sub(left,right,&error)) == 0` (1442) → 1. **This includes the degenerate case where `left == right`** (collinear red/green/blue chromaticities) making the divisor 0\n7. same statement, `|| error` term → 1\n8. same statement, **`|| red_inverse <= xy->whitey`** (line 1444) → 1. Sanity check \"r+g+b scales = white scale\". Distinct semantic row\n9. green-inverse `left` muldiv (1448), `right` muldiv (1450) → 2 rows\n10. `png_muldiv(&green_inverse, …) == 0 || error || green_inverse <= whitey` (1452-1454) → 3 sub-rows\n11. `blue_scale = fp_sub(fp_sub(reciprocal(whitey), reciprocal(red_inverse)), reciprocal(green_inverse))`; `if (error || blue_scale <= 0) return 1` (1460-1464) → 2 sub-rows (`error` set vs `blue_scale <= 0`)\n12. final fill-in: 9 `png_muldiv` calls at 1471, 1473, 1475, 1479, 1481, 1483, 1487, 1489, 1491 — each can fail → 9 rows. Note the last three (`blue_*`) use `(bluex, blue_scale, PNG_FP_1)` argument order, opposite to red/green which use `(redx, PNG_FP_1, red_inverse)`\n13. **success rows worth having:**\n   - sRGB: `wx=31270, wy=32900, rx=64000, ry=33000, gx=30000, gy=60000, bx=15000, by=6000` → expected `red_Y ≈ 21264`, `green_Y ≈ 71517`, `blue_Y ≈ 7219` (the exact `Y` triple 0.212639/0.715169/0.072192 is documented at lines 1404-1405)\n   - Kodak ProPhoto (documented Y coefficients at 1406-1407)\n   - Adobe RGB (1408-1409)\n   - Adobe Wide Gamut RGB (1410-1411)\n   - **ACES AP1**, which is the reason the limit was relaxed to 110000 and produces `red_Z = -600` — negative-Z success row (comment at 1218-1220)\n   - **ACES AP0** must FAIL (`bluey = -7700 < 0`, comment at 1214-1216) — explicit negative row\n\n### `png_fp_add` — `png.c:1069-1093` (static)\nNot directly callable; reach via `png_safe_add` inside the two above. Branch classes:\n1. `addend0 > 0` and `0x7fffffff - addend0 >= addend1` → sum\n2. `addend0 > 0` and overflow → `*error = 1`, returns `PNG_FP_1/2` = 50000\n3. `addend0 < 0` and `-0x7fffffff - addend0 <= addend1` → sum\n4. `addend0 < 0` and underflow → error, 50000\n5. `addend0 == 0` → returns `addend1` unchanged (no overflow check at all)\n\n### `png_fp_sub` — `png.c:1095-1114` (static)\nSymmetric: 5 classes on the sign of `addend1` (note it branches on **addend1**, not addend0).\n\n### `png_safe_add` — `png.c:1116-1129` (static)\n`png_fp_add(*a0, png_fp_add(a1, a2, &err), &err)`; returns the error flag, and **does not write the result on overflow** (line 1127). Two rows: all-fit vs inner-overflow vs outer-overflow (3).\n\n### `have_chromaticities` — `png.c:1845-1882` (static, READ_RGB_TO_GRAY only)\nPrecedence ladder, each an equivalence class:\n1. `png_has_chunk(png_ptr, mDCV)` true → **return 1** (highest priority, line 1865)\n2. else `png_has_chunk(png_ptr, sRGB)` true → **return 0** (line 1871-1872) — sRGB defaults win over cHRM\n3. else `png_has_chunk(png_ptr, cHRM)` true → **return 1** (line 1876)\n4. else → **return 0** (line 1881)\n5. mDCV **and** sRGB both present → 1 (mDCV wins)\n6. sRGB **and** cHRM both present → 0 (sRGB wins) — the counter-intuitive row\nNote `cICP` is documented as first-in-priority but **not yet implemented** (comment 1855-1856) — no branch exists.\n\n### `png_set_rgb_coefficients` — `png.c:1885-1957` (exported)\n1. `png_ptr->rgb_to_gray_coefficients_set != 0` → **whole function is a no-op** (guard at 1892)\n2. `have_chromaticities()` returns 0 → the `else` at 1948 → hardcoded REC 709 values `red=6968, green=23434` (1952-1953)\n3. `have_chromaticities()` 1 but `png_XYZ_from_xy(...) != 0` → same `else` → REC 709\n4. `have_chromaticities()` 1, XYZ OK, but the big validation chain at 1908-1912 fails (`total <= 0`, any component `< 0`, any `png_muldiv` returns 0, any scaled coefficient `< 0` or `> 32768`, or `r+g+b > 32769`) → **nothing is assigned**, coefficients stay at whatever they were (note this falls out of the `if` without reaching the `else`, so REC 709 is *not* applied — subtle third outcome)\n5. success, `r+g+b == 32768` exactly → `add == 0`, direct assignment\n6. success, `r+g+b == 32769` → `add = -1` (1922)\n7. success, `r+g+b == 32767` → `add = +1` (1924)\n8. the largest-coefficient tie-break: `g >= r && g >= b` → adjust g (1928); `r >= g && r >= b` → adjust r (1930); else → adjust b (1932). **3 distinct rows.** Note b is adjusted but `rgb_to_gray_blue_coeff` is not stored (commented out at 1954) — b's adjustment is only observable through the internal-error check\n9. internal-error path: `r+g+b != 32768` after adjustment → `png_error(\"internal error handling cHRM coefficients\")` (1938). Should be unreachable; a row asserting it never fires is valuable\n\n### `png_check_IHDR` — `png.c:1960-2122` (exported)\nAccumulates `error = 1` across independent checks then `png_error(\"Invalid IHDR data\")` at 2120-2121. Every check also emits its own `png_warning` first, so a single call can produce **multiple warnings then one error**. Distinct rows:\n\n**Success rows (no warning, no error):**\n1. `1×1, bd=8, ct=0 (GRAY), il=0, comp=0, filt=0`\n2. `1×1, bd=1, ct=0`, `bd=2`, `bd=4`, `bd=16` — 4 more bit-depth rows for GRAY\n3. `ct=2 (RGB)` with `bd=8` and `bd=16`\n4. `ct=3 (PALETTE)` with `bd=1,2,4,8` — 4 rows\n5. `ct=4 (GRAY_ALPHA)` with `bd=8,16`\n6. `ct=6 (RGBA)` with `bd=8,16`\n7. `il=1 (ADAM7)`\n8. `width == height == 1000000` (exactly the user limit) → OK\n9. `width == PNG_UINT_31_MAX` — fails the user-limit check but not the `> PNG_UINT_31_MAX` check; distinct\n\n**Failure branches, each a row:**\n| Branch | Line | Warning text |\n|---|---|---|\n| `width == 0` | 1969 | \"Image width is zero in IHDR\" |\n| `width > PNG_UINT_31_MAX` | 1975 | \"Invalid image width in IHDR\" |\n| `((width+7)&~7) > (PNG_SIZE_MAX-48-1)/8-1` | 1989 | \"Image width is too large for this architecture\" — on 64-bit LP64 this is effectively unreachable via a `png_uint_32` width; mark so |\n| `width > png_ptr->user_width_max` | 2012 | \"Image width exceeds user limit in IHDR\" (uses `user_width_max`, so `png_set_user_limits` interacts) |\n| `height == 0` | 2021 | \"Image height is zero in IHDR\" |\n| `height > PNG_UINT_31_MAX` | 2027 | \"Invalid image height in IHDR\" |\n| `height > user_height_max` | 2034 | \"Image height exceeds user limit in IHDR\" |\n| `bit_depth ∉ {1,2,4,8,16}` | 2044 | \"Invalid bit depth in IHDR\" — rows: 0, 3, 5, 6, 7, 9, 12, 32 |\n| `color_type < 0 \\|\\| == 1 \\|\\| == 5 \\|\\| > 6` | 2051 | \"Invalid color type in IHDR\" — rows: -1, 1, 5, 7 |\n| PALETTE with `bd > 8` | 2058 | \"Invalid color type/bit depth combination in IHDR\" — row: `ct=3, bd=16` |\n| RGB/GA/RGBA with `bd < 8` | 2059-2061 | same message — rows: `ct=2,bd=4`; `ct=4,bd=1`; `ct=6,bd=2` |\n| `interlace_type >= PNG_INTERLACE_LAST` (2) | 2067 | \"Unknown interlace method in IHDR\" — row: `il=2`, `il=7` |\n| `compression_type != 0` | 2073 | \"Unknown compression method in IHDR\" — row: `comp=1` |\n| **MNG block** (`PNG_MNG_FEATURES_SUPPORTED` is on) | 2079-2110 | |\n| ↳ `(mode & PNG_HAVE_PNG_SIGNATURE) && mng_features_permitted != 0` | 2089 | \"MNG features are not allowed in a PNG datastream\" — **warning only, `error` NOT set** |\n| ↳ `filter_type != 0` and NOT the permitted-64 combination | 2093-2103 | \"Unknown filter method in IHDR\" |\n| ↳ `filter_type == 64` + `PNG_FLAG_MNG_FILTER_64` permitted + no signature seen + `ct ∈ {2,6}` | 2095-2099 | **succeeds** (5-condition conjunction — needs `png_permit_mng_features(png_ptr, PNG_FLAG_MNG_FILTER_64)` and `sig_bytes` untouched). One row per condition dropped = 5 near-miss rows |\n| ↳ `filter_type != 0` and signature seen | 2105 | \"Invalid filter method in IHDR\" (can fire *in addition to* the previous one → 2 warnings) |\n| multi-error accumulation | 2120 | e.g. `width=0, height=0, bd=3, ct=1` → 4 warnings then 1 error |\n\nAlso note `png_get_IHDR` (`pngget.c:974-976`) and `png_set_IHDR` (`pngset.c:453-455`) both call `png_check_IHDR` — so any invalid stored state makes the *getter* raise `png_error`. That is a distinct high-value test row.\n\n---\n\n## 4. ASCII / number formatting\n\n### `png_check_fp_number` — `png.c:2133-2256`\n`int png_check_fp_number(png_const_charp string, size_t size, int *statep, size_t *whereami)`. Returns `(state & PNG_FP_SAW_DIGIT) != 0`. State constants at `pngpriv.h:2114-2154`: `PNG_FP_INTEGER 0`, `PNG_FP_FRACTION 1`, `PNG_FP_EXPONENT 2`, `PNG_FP_STATE 3` (mask), `PNG_FP_SAW_SIGN 4`, `PNG_FP_SAW_DIGIT 8`, `PNG_FP_SAW_DOT 16`, `PNG_FP_SAW_E 32`, `PNG_FP_SAW_ANY 60`, `PNG_FP_WAS_VALID 64`, `PNG_FP_NEGATIVE 128`, `PNG_FP_NONZERO 256`, `PNG_FP_STICKY 448`.\n\n**Character-classification arm (`switch (string[i])`, lines 2144-2156):**\n1. `'+'` (43) → `PNG_FP_SAW_SIGN`\n2. `'-'` (45) → `PNG_FP_SAW_SIGN | PNG_FP_NEGATIVE`\n3. `'.'` (46) → `PNG_FP_SAW_DOT`\n4. `'0'` (48) → `PNG_FP_SAW_DIGIT` (no NONZERO)\n5. `'1'`–`'9'` → `PNG_FP_SAW_DIGIT | PNG_FP_NONZERO`\n6. `'E'` (69) or `'e'` (101) → `PNG_FP_SAW_E`\n7. anything else (space, `'a'`, `','`, `'\\0'`, `0xff`) → `goto PNG_FP_End` — terminates scan at that index\n\n**State-machine arm (`switch ((state & PNG_FP_STATE) + (type & PNG_FP_SAW_ANY))`, lines 2162-2242). Each `case` is one row:**\n| # | Case | Line | Sub-branch |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 8 | `INTEGER + SAW_SIGN` | 2164 | `(state & SAW_ANY) != 0` → **End**. Input `\"+-1\"`: second sign terminates |\n| 9 | `INTEGER + SAW_SIGN` | 2168 | fresh → add SAW_SIGN. Input `\"+1\"`, `\"-1\"` |\n| 10 | `INTEGER + SAW_DOT` | 2173 | `SAW_DOT` already set → **End**. Input `\"1..2\"` |\n| 11 | `INTEGER + SAW_DOT` | 2176 | digit already seen → trailing dot, stays INTEGER + SAW_DOT. Input `\"1.\"` |\n| 12 | `INTEGER + SAW_DOT` | 2180 | no digit yet → `png_fp_set(state, FRACTION\\|SAW_DOT)`. Input `\".5\"` |\n| 13 | `INTEGER + SAW_DIGIT` | 2185 | `SAW_DOT` set (delayed fraction) → set state FRACTION. Input `\"1.5\"` — the dot was consumed at 2176, the digit flips state here |\n| 14 | `INTEGER + SAW_DIGIT` | 2188 | plain → add `SAW_DIGIT \\| WAS_VALID`. Input `\"1\"`, `\"123\"` |\n| 15 | `INTEGER + SAW_E` | 2193 | no digit yet → **End**. Input `\"E5\"`, `\"+E5\"` |\n| 16 | `INTEGER + SAW_E` | 2196 | digit seen → state EXPONENT. Input `\"1E5\"`, `\"1e5\"` |\n| 17 | `FRACTION + SAW_SIGN` | 2200 (commented) | falls to `default` → **End**. Input `\".5-\"`, `\"1.2+3\"` |\n| 18 | `FRACTION + SAW_DOT` | 2203 (commented) | falls to `default` → **End**. Input `\".5.\"` |\n| 19 | `FRACTION + SAW_DIGIT` | 2206 | add `SAW_DIGIT \\| WAS_VALID`. Input `\".5\"`, `\"1.25\"` |\n| 20 | `FRACTION + SAW_E` | 2215 | no digit in fraction → **End**. Input `\".E5\"` (the only way to reach it, per the comment at 2211-2214) |\n| 21 | `FRACTION + SAW_E` | 2218 | digit present → EXPONENT. Input `\".5E3\"`, `\"1.5e-3\"` |\n| 22 | `EXPONENT + SAW_SIGN` | 2223 | `SAW_ANY` set → **End**. Input `\"1E+-5\"`, `\"1E5+\"` |\n| 23 | `EXPONENT + SAW_SIGN` | 2226 | fresh → add SAW_SIGN. Input `\"1E+5\"`, `\"1E-5\"` |\n| 24 | `EXPONENT + SAW_DOT` | 2230 (commented) | `default` → **End**. Input `\"1E5.2\"` |\n| 25 | `EXPONENT + SAW_DIGIT` | 2233 | add `SAW_DIGIT \\| WAS_VALID`. Input `\"1E5\"`, `\"1E123\"` |\n| 26 | `EXPONENT + SAW_E` | 2238 (commented) | `default` → **End**. Input `\"1E5E5\"` |\n| 27 | loop exhausts `size` | 2140/2246 | `i == size` on exit, e.g. `(\"1.5\", 3)` |\n\n**Sticky-flag rows** (`PNG_FP_STICKY = WAS_VALID|NEGATIVE|NONZERO`, preserved across `png_fp_set`):\n28. `\"-0\"` → `NEGATIVE` set, `NONZERO` clear → `PNG_FP_IS_NEGATIVE` true but value is zero. Test the `PNG_FP_IS_ZERO`/`IS_POSITIVE`/`IS_NEGATIVE` macros (`pngpriv.h:2152-2154`) against this\n29. `\"0\"` → `PNG_FP_IS_ZERO` true\n30. `\"0.0\"` → still zero (NONZERO never set)\n31. `\"0.1\"` → NONZERO set via the fraction digit\n32. `\"1\"` → `PNG_FP_IS_POSITIVE` true\n\n**Resumable-scan rows** (the whole point of the `statep`/`whereami` out-params):\n33. call with `(\"1.5\", 1, &state=0, &i=0)` → returns 1, `i == 1`, state = INTEGER+DIGIT; then call again with `(\"1.5\", 3, &state, &i)` → continues from `i==1`, ends with FRACTION state. Two-call streaming row\n34. empty scan: `(\"\", 0, …)` → loop body never runs, returns `(0 & SAW_DIGIT) != 0` = **0**\n35. `size == 0` with non-empty string → same, returns 0, `i` unchanged\n36. leading `i` past a valid prefix, e.g. `(\"xx1.5\", 5, &state, &i=2)`\n\n### `png_check_fp_string` — `png.c:2260-2271`\n`return state` (non-zero) on success, `0` on failure. Condition: `png_check_fp_number(...) != 0 && (char_index == size || string[char_index] == 0)`.\n1. `(\"1.5\", 3)` → consumed exactly, `char_index == size` → returns state\n2. `(\"1.5\", 10)` where buffer is `\"1.5\\0......\"` → `string[3] == 0` → returns state (the NUL-terminator escape hatch)\n3. `(\"1.5x\", 4)` → `char_index == 3`, `string[3] == 'x'` → **returns 0**\n4. `(\"\", 0)` → inner returns 0 → **0**\n5. `(\"+\", 1)` → no digit → **0**\n6. `(\".\", 1)` → **0**\n7. `(\"E5\", 2)` → **0**\n8. `(\"-1.5e-3\", 7)` → success, state has NEGATIVE|NONZERO|WAS_VALID|SAW_DIGIT\n9. `(\"1.\", 2)` → trailing dot is accepted → success\n10. `(\".5\", 2)` → success\n11. `(\"1e\", 2)` → the `'e'` moves to EXPONENT, `char_index == 2 == size`, `SAW_DIGIT` was cleared by `png_fp_set(state, PNG_FP_EXPONENT)`… careful: `png_fp_set` keeps only `PNG_FP_STICKY` bits, and `SAW_DIGIT` (8) is not sticky, so `SAW_DIGIT` is **cleared** → inner returns 0 → **returns 0**. High-value row\n12. `(\"1e+\", 3)` → same, returns 0\n13. `(\"1e+5\", 4)` → success\nCallers: `png_set_sCAL_s` (`pngset.c:626, 630`) and `png_set_pCAL` (`pngset.c:531`).\n\n### `png_ascii_from_fp` — `png.c:2316-2636` (float only)\n`void png_ascii_from_fp(png_ptr, ascii, size, double fp, unsigned precision)`. Errors via `png_error(\"ASCII conversion buffer too small\")` at 2635. `DBL_DIG` is 15 on x86-64.\n\n**Precision normalization (2325-2330):**\n1. `precision == 0` → `precision = DBL_DIG` (15)\n2. `precision > DBL_DIG+1` (i.e. `> 16`) → clamped to 16. Rows: 17, 100, `UINT_MAX`\n3. `precision == 1` … `16` pass through — worth rows at 1, 5 (the `PNG_sCAL_PRECISION` value), 15, 16\n\n**Buffer-size gate (2333):** `if (size >= precision+5)`\n4. `size == precision+5` exactly → **succeeds** (boundary). e.g. `precision=5, size=10`\n5. `size == precision+4` → **`png_error`**. First failing size\n6. `size == 0` → `png_error`\n7. the *second* size check, for exponent digits (line 2608 `if (size > cdigits)`): if it fails, control falls out of the inner block to 2634 → `png_error`. Force with a tiny buffer and a huge exponent, e.g. `fp = 1e300, precision = 1, size = 6`\n\n**Sign (2335-2340):**\n8. `fp < 0` → writes `'-'`, decrements `size`\n9. `fp == -0.0` → `fp < 0` is **false** for negative zero → no minus sign. Row\n10. `fp > 0` → no sign\n\n**Magnitude classification (2342, 2618, 2624):**\n11. `fp >= DBL_MIN && fp <= DBL_MAX` → main path\n12. `!(fp >= DBL_MIN)` → line 2620: emits `\"0\"`. Rows: `fp = 0.0`, `fp = -0.0` (after the sign test), `fp = DBL_MIN/2` (subnormal), `fp = 1e-320`, `fp = NaN` (all comparisons false, so `!(fp >= DBL_MIN)` is **true** → emits `\"0\"` — surprising, worth a row)\n13. `fp > DBL_MAX` → line 2626: emits `\"inf\"`. Rows: `fp = HUGE_VAL`, `fp = -HUGE_VAL` (sign written first → `\"-inf\"`)\n\n**Exponent-extraction regime (2356-2375):**\n14. `frexp` + `(exp_b10*77)>>8` for positive base-2 exponent: `fp = 1.0` → exp_b10 from frexp is 1 → `(1*77)>>8 = 0`\n15. negative base-2 exponent (arithmetic shift floors): `fp = 0.5` → frexp gives 0 → 0; `fp = 0.001` → frexp gives -9 → `(-9*77)>>8 = -3`\n16. the `while (base < DBL_MIN || base < fp)` loop (2363-2375) iterating: needs `10^exp_b10 < fp`\n17. the `else break` at 2373-2374: `png_pow10(exp_b10+1) > DBL_MAX` → `fp` near `DBL_MAX`, e.g. `1e308`\n18. `png_pow10` underflow path (`power < DBL_MIN_10_EXP` → returns 0, line 2290) → `base == 0 < DBL_MIN` → forces loop iterations. `fp = 1e-310`\n19. normalization loop `while (fp >= 1) { fp /= 10; ++exp_b10; }` (2385-2388) iterating ≥ once\n\n**Leading-zeros regime (2403-2409):**\n20. `exp_b10 < 0 && exp_b10 > -3` → `czero = -exp_b10` (1 or 2 leading zeros), `exp_b10 = 0`. Rows: `fp = 0.05` (1 zero), `fp = 0.005` (2 zeros)\n21. `exp_b10 == -3` → **not** in `(-3, 0)` → `czero = 0`, will take the exponent route. `fp = 0.0005` → `\"5E-4\"`. Boundary row\n22. `exp_b10 >= 0` → `czero = 0`\n\n**Digit-generation loop (2417-2540):**\n23. `modf` path (`cdigits+czero+1 < precision+clead`, 2427-2428) — normal digit extraction\n24. `floor(fp+.5)` final-rounding path (2432)\n25. `d > 9` rounding-to-10 with `czero > 0` (2437-2441): `--czero; d = 1;` and the nested `if (cdigits == 0) --clead`. Force with `fp = 0.0999999, precision = 3`\n26. `d > 9` with `czero == 0`, the carry-propagation `while (cdigits > 0 && d > 9)` loop (2444-2463):\n   - `exp_b10 != -1` → `++exp_b10` (2448)\n   - `exp_b10 == -1 && ch == 46` (the `'.'`) → back up over the dot, `++size`, `exp_b10 = 1` (2451-2459)\n   - `d = ch - 47` continuation\n   Force with `fp = 0.999, precision = 2` → `\"1.0\"`, and `fp = 9.99, precision = 2` → `\"10\"`\n27. reached-the-beginning `if (d > 9)` at 2469-2493:\n   - `exp_b10 == -1` and the recovered `ch == 46` → `++size; exp_b10 = 1` (2479-2482)\n   - `exp_b10 == -1` and `ch != 46` (lost a leading zero) → exp_b10 stays -1 (comment 2484-2486)\n   - `exp_b10 != -1` → `++exp_b10` (2489)\n   - always outputs `'1'` (2492)\n   Force with `fp = 0.9999999, precision = 1` → `\"1\"`\n28. `d == 0` accumulation (2499-2503): `++czero`, and `if (cdigits == 0) ++clead`. Embedded zeros: `fp = 1.005, precision = 4`\n29. `d != 0` flush-the-zeros block (2504-2537):\n   - `cdigits += czero - clead; clead = 0`\n   - the `while (czero > 0)` loop emitting `'0'`s, with `exp_b10 == 0` inserting the `'.'` (2518-2521)\n   - the post-loop `exp_b10 == 0` `'.'` insertion (2528-2536)\n30. loop termination via `cdigits+czero >= precision+clead` (2540)\n31. loop termination via `fp <= DBL_MIN` — exact value, e.g. `fp = 0.5, precision = 15` → `\"0.5\"` not `\"0.500000000000000\"` (trailing-zero stripping)\n\n**Exponent-vs-plain decision (2551):** `if (exp_b10 >= -1 && exp_b10 <= 2)`\n32. `exp_b10 == -1` → plain, the `while (exp_b10-- > 0)` loop runs 0 times → `\"0.5\"`\n33. `exp_b10 == 0` → `\"5\"` style\n34. `exp_b10 == 1` → one trailing `'0'` appended\n35. `exp_b10 == 2` → two trailing `'0'`s. Boundary; e.g. `fp = 500.0, precision = 1` → `\"500\"`\n36. `exp_b10 == 3` → **exponent route**: `fp = 5000.0, precision = 1` → `\"5E3\"`. First value past the boundary\n37. `exp_b10 == -2` → exponent route: `fp = 0.0005, precision = 1` → `\"5E-4\"` (the `exp_b10 = -2 → E-4`? verify against actual — the point is it is the first value below the boundary)\n\n**Exponent-emission regime (2576-2615):**\n38. `exp_b10 < 0` → emits `'E'`, `'-'`, then digits (2587-2591)\n39. `exp_b10 > 0` → emits `'E'` then digits, no sign (2593-2594)\n40. 1-digit exponent (`exp_b10 = 3`)\n41. 2-digit exponent (`exp_b10 = 20`)\n42. 3-digit exponent (`exp_b10 = 300`) — `fp = 1e300`; also stresses the `exponent[10]` local buffer\n43. `size > cdigits` fails (2608) → falls to 2635 `png_error`\n\n**Recommended concrete success matrix (precision × value):** cross `precision ∈ {1, 5, 15, 16}` with `fp ∈ {0.0, 1.0, -1.0, 0.5, 0.05, 0.005, 0.0005, 1.5, 123.456, 1e-10, 1e10, 1e300, 1e-300, DBL_MIN, DBL_MAX, 0.999999, 9.999999}` — that plus the 13 structural rows above covers every arm.\n\n### `png_ascii_from_fixed` — `png.c:2642-2714`\n1. **`size <= 12`** → skips everything → `png_error(\"ASCII conversion buffer too small\")`. `size == 12` fails, `size == 13` succeeds (guard is `size > 12` at 2649). Boundary pair\n2. `fp < 0` → writes `'-'`, `num = (png_uint_32)(-fp)` (2656)\n3. `fp >= 0` → `num = (png_uint_32)fp`\n4. `fp == PNG_INT_MIN` (`-2147483648`): `-fp` overflows signed but the cast gives `0x80000000` = 2147483648, and the guard `if (num <= 0x80000000)` (2661) **accepts** it. Boundary row producing `\"-21474.83648\"`\n5. `num > 0x80000000` → **impossible** from a `png_fixed_point` (max positive is 0x7fffffff); the check is dead for valid inputs. Note it\n6. `fp == 0` → `while (num)` never runs → `ndigits == 0` → `else *ascii++ = 48` (2704) → `\"0\"`\n7. `fp == 1` → `ndigits = 1`, `first = 1`, `ndigits <= 5` so the `while (ndigits > 5)` loop is skipped, `first <= 5` → `'.'` then 4 leading zeros then `'1'` → `\".00001\"`\n8. `fp == 100000` → `ndigits = 6`, one integer digit `'1'`, then `first == 6 > 5` → **no fraction emitted** → `\"1\"`\n9. `fp == 150000` → `\"1.5\"` (trailing zeros suppressed because `first == 5`)\n10. `fp == 100001` → `\"1.00001\"` (`first == 1`, all 5 fraction digits)\n11. `fp == 10` → `\".0001\"`\n12. `fp == 99999` → `\".99999\"`\n13. `fp == 2147483647` → `\"21474.83647\"` (10 digits, max width)\n14. `fp == -1` → `\"-.00001\"` — note **no leading `'0'`** before the dot. Distinct formatting row\n15. `fp == -100000` → `\"-1\"`\n16. `first == 16` sentinel never updated (all digits zero) — only reachable if `num != 0` but every extracted digit is 0, which is impossible; note as dead\n17. the `while (ndigits > 5)` integer-digit loop iterating multiple times: `fp = 2100000` → `\"21\"`\n18. `first == 5` exactly: `fp = 110000` → `\"1.1\"` (boundary of the `first <= 5` test at 2687)\n19. `first == 6`: `fp = 200000` → `\"2\"`\nCallers: `png_set_sCAL_fixed` (`pngset.c:723-724`) with `size = PNG_sCAL_MAX_DIGITS+1`.\n\n### `png_safecat` — `pngerror.c:72-86`\n`size_t png_safecat(png_charp buffer, size_t bufsize, size_t pos, png_const_charp string)`. Never errors.\n1. `buffer == NULL` → returns `pos` unchanged, no write\n2. `pos >= bufsize` → returns `pos` unchanged, **no NUL written**\n3. `pos == bufsize - 1` → enters, inner `while` immediately fails (`pos < bufsize-1` false) → writes `buffer[pos] = '\\0'` only, returns `pos`\n4. `string == NULL` → skips the copy loop, writes the NUL, returns `pos`\n5. `string == \"\"` → same effect\n6. normal append, fits entirely → returns `pos + strlen(string)`\n7. **truncation**: `strlen(string) > bufsize-1-pos` → copies as much as fits, NUL-terminates, returns the truncated `pos`. e.g. `bufsize=5, pos=0, string=\"abcdefg\"` → `\"abcd\"`, returns 4\n8. exact fit: `bufsize=5, pos=0, string=\"abcd\"` → returns 4\n9. one-over: `bufsize=5, pos=0, string=\"abcde\"` → returns 4, `'e'` dropped\n10. chained calls (the `APPEND_STRING` idiom in `png_convert_to_rfc1123_buffer`, `png.c:762`): three successive calls where the third truncates\n11. `pos == 0, bufsize == 1` → writes only the NUL\n12. `bufsize == 0` → `pos < 0` is false for `pos=0` → returns 0, no write. Boundary row\n\n### `png_format_number` — `pngerror.c:93-167`\n`png_charp png_format_number(png_const_charp start, png_charp end, int format, png_alloc_size_t number)` — fills **backwards** from `end`, returns a pointer to the start of the digits. `*--end = '\\0'` unconditionally at 101. `PNG_NUMBER_BUFFER_SIZE = 24` (`pngpriv.h:1957`).\n\nFormat constants (`pngpriv.h:1962-1968`): `PNG_NUMBER_FORMAT_u = 1`, `_02u = 2`, `_d = 1` (alias of `_u`), `_02d = 2` (alias of `_02u`), `_x = 3`, `_02x = 4`, `_fixed = 5`.\n\nPer format:\n1. **`PNG_NUMBER_FORMAT_u` (1)** — `mincount = 1`: `number = 0` → `\"0\"`; `1` → `\"1\"`; `9`; `10` → `\"10\"`; `12345`; `UINT32_MAX` → `\"4294967295\"`; `SIZE_MAX`\n2. **`PNG_NUMBER_FORMAT_02u` (2)** — sets `mincount = 2` then falls through: `0` → `\"00\"`; `5` → `\"05\"`; `9` → `\"09\"`; `10` → `\"10\"`; `100` → `\"100\"` (mincount doesn't cap). Used by `png_convert_to_rfc1123_buffer` for hour/minute/second (`png.c:773,775,777`)\n3. **`PNG_NUMBER_FORMAT_x` (3)** — `digits[number & 0xf]`, `number >>= 4`: `0` → `\"0\"`; `15` → `\"F\"` (uppercase); `16` → `\"10\"`; `255` → `\"FF\"`; `0xdeadbeef` → `\"DEADBEEF\"`\n4. **`PNG_NUMBER_FORMAT_02x` (4)** — `mincount = 2` + fallthrough: `0` → `\"00\"`; `5` → `\"05\"`; `15` → `\"0F\"`; `255` → `\"FF\"`; `256` → `\"100\"`. Used by `png_check_keyword`'s bad-character warning (`pngset.c:2046`)\n5. **`PNG_NUMBER_FORMAT_fixed` (5)** — `mincount = 5`, plus the trailing-zero suppression (`if (output != 0 || number % 10 != 0)`, 2116) and the decimal-point insertion at `count == 5` (2153-2163). Sub-rows:\n   - `number = 0` → the loop runs once (`count < mincount`), `number%10 == 0` and `output == 0` → nothing emitted, `count = 1`; loop continues while `count < 5`… then at `count == 5`, `output == 0 && number == 0` → `*--end = '0'` (2162) → **`\"0\"`**\n   - `number = 100000` → `\"1\"` (five trailing zeros all suppressed, then `output == 0 && number != 0`?? at `count==5` `number` is 1, so neither branch fires → no `'.'`, no `'0'` → then loop continues with `number = 1` → emits `'1'`) → `\"1\"`\n   - `number = 150000` → `\"1.5\"`\n   - `number = 100001` → `\"1.00001\"`\n   - `number = 1` → `\".00001\"`\n   - `number = 10` → `\".0001\"`\n   - `number = 99999` → `\".99999\"`\n   - `number = 2147483647` → `\"21474.83647\"`\n   - `number = 10000` → `\".1\"`\n6. **`default` (any other format, e.g. 0, 6, -1)** → line 2144-2146 `number = 0`, `++count`, loop exits after mincount iterations → produces `\"0\"`-ish. One row\n7. **`end` reached `start`** (buffer exhaustion, loop condition `end > start` at 106): pass a 2-byte window with `number = 123456789` → truncated output, returns `start`. One row\n8. Interaction with the `PNG_FORMAT_NUMBER` wrapper macro (`pngpriv.h:1953-1955`) which passes `buffer, buffer+PNG_NUMBER_BUFFER_SIZE` — normal-use row\n\n### `png_convert_to_rfc1123_buffer` — `png.c:741-787`\n`int png_convert_to_rfc1123_buffer(char out[29], png_const_timep ptime)`. Returns 1 on success, **0** on any validation failure.\n1. **`out == NULL`** → 0 (line 748)\n2. `ptime->year > 9999` → 0 (751). Rows: `9999` OK, `10000` fail, `65535` fail\n3. `ptime->month == 0` → 0 ; `month == 1` OK ; `month == 12` OK ; `month == 13` → 0\n4. `ptime->day == 0` → 0 ; `day == 1` OK ; `day == 31` OK ; `day == 32` → 0\n5. `ptime->hour > 23` → 0 ; `hour == 0` OK ; `hour == 23` OK ; `hour == 24` → 0\n6. `ptime->minute > 59` → 0 ; `59` OK ; `60` → 0\n7. `ptime->second > 60` → 0 ; **`second == 60` is OK** (leap second) ; `61` → 0\n8. success formatting: `{2024, 7, 4, 13, 5, 9}` → `\"4 Jul 2024 13:05:09 +0000\"` — note day uses `PNG_NUMBER_FORMAT_u` (no zero pad) but h/m/s use `_02u`\n9. `day == 31, month == 12` → `\"31 Dec ...\"` (widest day + last month)\n10. `year == 0` → `\"...  0 ...\"` (year is `_u`, so single `'0'`)\n11. `year == 9999` → 4-digit year, exercises the `number_buf[5]` comment at 760\n12. all 12 months → 12 rows for the `short_months[ptime->month - 1]` table (744-746)\n13. the `APPEND(ch)` macro's `if (pos < 28)` guard (765) — reached only with the widest possible input; assert no truncation for `{9999, 12, 31, 23, 59, 60}` → `\"31 Dec 9999 23:59:60 +0000\"` = 26 chars + NUL, fits in 29\n14. the trailing `APPEND_STRING(\" +0000\")` reliably terminating (778)\n\n### `png_convert_to_rfc1123` — `png.c:795-809` (deprecated, still exported)\n1. `png_ptr == NULL` → returns `NULL` (falls to 808)\n2. valid `ptime` → returns `png_ptr->time_buffer` (the private buffer in `png_struct`)\n3. invalid `ptime` (any of the 6 failures above) → `png_warning(\"Ignoring invalid time value\")` then returns `NULL`\n4. two successive calls → second overwrites the first's buffer (aliasing row)\nGuarded by `#if PNG_LIBPNG_VER < 10700` at 789 — present here.\n\n### `png_convert_from_struct_tm` — `pngwrite.c:507-517`\nNo branches, no validation at all.\n1. `tm_year = 124` → `year = 2024`\n2. `tm_year = 0` → `year = 1900`\n3. `tm_year = -1900` → `year = 0`\n4. `tm_mon = 0` → `month = 1` ; `tm_mon = 11` → `month = 12`\n5. **`tm_mon = 12`** → `month = 13` — **no validation**; the resulting `png_time` will then be rejected by `png_set_tIME`/`png_convert_to_rfc1123_buffer`. Distinct row\n6. `tm_sec = 60` (leap second) → `second = 60`\n7. `tm_sec = 61` → `second = 61` (accepted here, rejected downstream)\n8. `tm_year = 8100` → `year = 10000` → downstream rejection\n9. `(png_byte)` truncation: `tm_mday = 300` → `day = 44`. Row\n10. `(png_uint_16)` truncation: `tm_year = 63636` → `year = 0`. Row\n\n### `png_convert_from_time_t` — `pngwrite.c:520-538`\n1. `ttime == 0` → `gmtime` gives 1970-01-01T00:00:00 → `{1970,1,1,0,0,0}`\n2. a normal timestamp\n3. **`gmtime` returns NULL** (line 522-530) → `memset(ptime, 0, sizeof(*ptime))` and return. Force with `ttime = (time_t)INT64_MIN` or a year past `INT_MAX`. This zero-fill is the documented failure return; the resulting `png_time` has `month == 0` so it will be rejected downstream\n4. negative `ttime` (pre-epoch), e.g. `-1` → 1969-12-31T23:59:59\n5. `ttime = 2147483647` → 2038-01-19 (32-bit boundary)\n6. large positive `ttime` on 64-bit → year > 9999 → downstream rejection\nBoth are guarded by `PNG_CONVERT_tIME_SUPPORTED` (`pnglibconf.h:29`) — on.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Signature, palette, version, io-state\n\n### `png_sig_cmp` — `png.c:79-97`\n`int png_sig_cmp(png_const_bytep sig, size_t start, size_t num_to_check)`. Reference signature `{137,80,78,71,13,10,26,10}` (line 82).\nClamping/rejection ladder:\n1. **`num_to_check > 8`** → clamped to 8 (84-85). Rows: `num_to_check = 9`, `100`, `SIZE_MAX`\n2. **`num_to_check < 1`** (i.e. `== 0`) → **returns -1** (87-88), *before* the `start` check\n3. **`start > 7`** → **returns -1** (90-91). Rows: `start = 8`, `start = 100`, `start = SIZE_MAX`\n4. **`start + num_to_check > 8`** → `num_to_check = 8 - start` (93-94). Rows: `(start=4, num=8)` → checks 4; `(start=7, num=8)` → checks 1\n5. `memcmp` returns 0 (match): `(sig=good, 0, 8)` → 0\n6. `memcmp` < 0: `(sig[0] = 0, 0, 8)` → negative\n7. `memcmp` > 0: `(sig[0] = 255, 0, 8)` → positive\n8. Every `start ∈ {0..7}` with `num_to_check = 1` — 8 rows exercising each signature byte in isolation\n9. Every `start ∈ {0..7}` with `num_to_check = 8-start` — 8 rows exercising each suffix\n10. `(start=0, num=1)` → only checks the 0x89 byte\n11. `(start=0, num=4)` → the \"\\x89PNG\" prefix, the classic partial check\n12. `(start=4, num=4)` → the \"\\r\\n\\x1a\\n\" suffix — this is the case `png_read_sig` uses to distinguish \"Not a PNG file\" from \"corrupted by ASCII conversion\" (`pngrutil.c:135-141`)\n13. **ASCII-mangled signature**: `{137,80,78,71,13,10,26,10}` with the CRLF converted, i.e. `{137,80,78,71,10,26,10}` — mismatch at index 4\n14. mismatch in the middle: differ only at index 3 → positive/negative depending on direction\n15. `(start=7, num=1)` → last byte only\n16. combination `(start=0, num=0)` → -1 (rule 2 wins)\n17. combination `(start=8, num=0)` → -1 (rule 2 fires first, before rule 3 — ordering row)\n18. combination `(start=8, num=8)` → -1 (rule 3)\nGuarded by `#ifdef PNG_READ_SUPPORTED` (`png.c:51`/`99`) — on.\n\n### `png_check_sig` — `png.h:940`\n`#define png_check_sig(sig, n) (png_sig_cmp((sig), 0, (n)) == 0)` — **a macro, marked DEPRECATED**, not a function. Rows: `n = 0` → `png_sig_cmp` returns -1 → macro yields **0 (false)**; `n = 8` good sig → 1; `n = 4` good prefix → 1; `n = 9` → clamped, 1.\n\n### `png_build_grayscale_palette` — `png.c:879-926`\n`void png_build_grayscale_palette(int bit_depth, png_colorp palette)`.\n1. **`palette == NULL`** → early return, no crash (889-890)\n2. **`bit_depth == 1`** → `num_palette = 2`, `color_inc = 0xff` → entries `{0,0,0}`, `{255,255,255}`\n3. **`bit_depth == 2`** → `4`, `0x55` → `0, 0x55, 0xAA, 0xFF`\n4. **`bit_depth == 4`** → `16`, `0x11` → `0x00, 0x11, ..., 0xFF`\n5. **`bit_depth == 8`** → `256`, `1` → `0..255`\n6. **`default`** (any other bit depth) → `num_palette = 0`, `color_inc = 0` → **loop body never executes, palette untouched**. Rows: `bit_depth = 0`, `3`, `5`, `6`, `7`, `16`, `-1`, `32`. Note **16 falls in the default arm** — a grayscale 16-bit palette is not built. High-value row\n7. For each successful depth, assert `red == green == blue` for every entry, and the `v & 0xff` masking at 922-924\n8. `bit_depth = 8` last entry: `v = 255` → `{255,255,255}` (no wraparound)\nGuarded by `PNG_BUILD_GRAYSCALE_PALETTE_SUPPORTED` (`pnglibconf.h:25`) — on. Comment at 873 notes it is **not used internally**.\n\n### Version / copyright accessors\n- `png_get_copyright` — `png.c:815-831`. `PNG_STRING_COPYRIGHT` is not defined in this build, so the `#else` at 821 applies: a multi-line string beginning with `PNG_STRING_NEWLINE` and containing `\"libpng version 1.6.59.git\"`. Rows: `png_ptr == NULL` (ignored, `PNG_UNUSED`) and `png_ptr != NULL` → identical pointer. Assert the returned pointer is the same for both calls (static storage).\n- `png_get_libpng_ver` — `png.c:841-846` → delegates to `png_get_header_ver`. One row + assert `strcmp(png_get_libpng_ver(p), png_get_header_ver(p)) == 0`.\n- `png_get_header_ver` — `png.c:848-854` → `PNG_LIBPNG_VER_STRING`. Rows: NULL and non-NULL `png_ptr`.\n- `png_get_header_version` — `png.c:856-870`. `__STDC__` is defined by any conforming compiler → the `#ifdef` branch at 861 → `PNG_HEADER_VERSION_STRING` + `PNG_STRING_NEWLINE`. `PNG_READ_SUPPORTED` is on so `\" (NO READ SUPPORT)\"` is **not** appended. Two config rows if you can toggle READ; one otherwise. Assert it *contains* `png_get_header_ver()`'s string.\n- `png_access_version_number` — `png.c:990-995` → `(png_uint_32)PNG_LIBPNG_VER`. Takes **no arguments**. One row: assert `== 10659` and `== PNG_LIBPNG_VER` from the header (compile-vs-runtime consistency).\n\n### IO state getters\n- `png_get_io_state` — `pngget.c:1343-1347`. **No NULL check** — dereferences `png_ptr->io_state` directly. Rows: freshly created struct → `PNG_IO_NONE` (0); during a read the values are `PNG_IO_READING|PNG_IO_SIGNATURE` (0x0011), `|PNG_IO_CHUNK_HDR` (0x0021), `|PNG_IO_CHUNK_DATA` (0x0041), `|PNG_IO_CHUNK_CRC` (0x0081); during a write `PNG_IO_WRITING|...` (0x0022, 0x0042, 0x0082). Also test the masks `PNG_IO_MASK_OP` (0x000f) and `PNG_IO_MASK_LOC` (0x00f0) from `png.h:2663-2671`. Set at `pngrutil.c:128` (signature) and `pngrutil.c:285` (CRC).\n- `png_get_io_chunk_type` — `pngget.c:1349-1353`. Also **no NULL check**. Rows: fresh struct → 0; after IHDR → `png_IHDR` (0x49484452); after IDAT, IEND, and an ancillary chunk. Pair with `PNG_CHUNK_ANCILLARY()` to test the ancillary bit.\n- `png_get_io_chunk_name` — **removed**: `PNG_REMOVED(200, ...)` at `png.h:2655`. No row.\nBoth gated by `PNG_IO_STATE_SUPPORTED` (`pnglibconf.h:44`) — on.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Shared transforms — `pngtrans.c`\n\nAll five take `png_row_infop row_info` + `png_bytep row`; the *only* fields consulted are `color_type`, `bit_depth`, `channels`, `width`, `rowbytes`, `pixel_depth`. These are `PRIVATE` but all five are exported symbols (`png_do_invert`, `png_do_swap`, `png_do_packswap`, `png_do_strip_channel`, `png_do_bgr` all appear in `nm`), so they are directly unit-testable.\n\n### `png_do_invert` — `pngtrans.c:289-340`\nThree top-level arms plus a silent no-op:\n1. **`color_type == PNG_COLOR_TYPE_GRAY` (0)** (line 297) — inverts **every byte of `rowbytes`**, regardless of `bit_depth`. Sub-rows: `bit_depth = 1` (packed, so partial last byte is also inverted including padding bits — worth asserting), `2`, `4`, `8`, `16`. `rowbytes = 0` (empty loop). `rowbytes = 1`.\n2. **`color_type == GRAY_ALPHA (4) && bit_depth == 8`** (310-311) — strides 2, inverts only byte 0 of each pair. Sub-rows: `rowbytes = 2` (1 pixel), `rowbytes = 8` (4 pixels), `rowbytes` odd (e.g. 3 — the `i += 2` loop reads `rp` at index 2 which is in bounds, but the alpha at 3 doesn't exist; boundary row)\n3. **`color_type == GRAY_ALPHA && bit_depth == 16`** (325-326, inside `#ifdef PNG_16BIT_SUPPORTED` — on) — strides 4, inverts bytes 0 and 1. Sub-rows: `rowbytes = 4`, `rowbytes = 16`\n4. **no-op arms**: `color_type = 2 (RGB)`, `3 (PALETTE)`, `6 (RGBA)` → function returns having done nothing. 3 rows\n5. **`GRAY_ALPHA` with `bit_depth ∈ {1,2,4}`** → matches neither arm 2 nor arm 3 → **no-op**. Distinct row (an alpha channel at sub-byte depth is impossible in PNG, but the function silently does nothing)\n6. double-invert identity: apply twice → original row\n7. the historical note at 294-296 (the `bit_depth == 1` restriction was removed in 1.0.13) means GRAY at bit_depth 16 inverts *both* bytes of each sample — assert that.\n\n### `png_do_swap` — `pngtrans.c:346-371` (inside `#ifdef PNG_16BIT_SUPPORTED` at 343)\nSingle gate: `if (row_info->bit_depth == 16)` (351). Loop count is **`width * channels`**, not `rowbytes`.\n1. `bit_depth != 16` → **no-op**. Rows: `1, 2, 4, 8`\n2. `bit_depth == 16, channels == 1` (GRAY) — `width = 1`, `width = 4`\n3. `bit_depth == 16, channels == 2` (GRAY_ALPHA)\n4. `bit_depth == 16, channels == 3` (RGB)\n5. `bit_depth == 16, channels == 4` (RGBA)\n6. `width == 0` → empty loop, no-op\n7. **`rowbytes` inconsistent with `width*channels*2`** — the function ignores `rowbytes` entirely, so an under-sized buffer with an over-large `width` overruns. Worth a row asserting the function reads exactly `2*width*channels` bytes\n8. double-swap identity\n9. `PNG_BUILTIN_BSWAP16_SUPPORTED` is **not defined** in this build (`pnglibconf.h` has no such entry) → the `#else` byte-swap at 365-367 is what compiles. Note the `#ifdef` at 359 for completeness.\n\n### `png_do_packswap` — `pngtrans.c:482-508`\nGate `if (row_info->bit_depth < 8)` (487), then a three-way table selection.\n1. `bit_depth >= 8` → **no-op**. Rows: `8`, `16`\n2. **`bit_depth == 1`** → `onebppswaptable` (`pngtrans.c:376-409`) — reverses 8 pixels per byte\n3. **`bit_depth == 2`** → `twobppswaptable` (411-444) — reverses 4 pixels per byte\n4. **`bit_depth == 4`** → `fourbppswaptable` (446-479) — swaps the two nibbles\n5. **`bit_depth ∈ {0, 3, 5, 6, 7}`** → the `else return` at 502-503 → **no-op**. Rows: `3`, `5`, `0` (all `< 8` so they pass the outer gate but hit the inner `else`). Distinct from arm 1\n6. `rowbytes == 0` → the `for (rp = row; rp < row_end; rp++)` loop never runs\n7. `rowbytes == 1` → single byte\n8. table correctness spot-checks: `1bpp: 0x01 → 0x80`, `0x80 → 0x01`, `0xF0 → 0x0F`, `0x00 → 0x00`, `0xFF → 0xFF`; `2bpp: 0x01 → 0x40`, `0x1B → 0xB1`?? (verify against the table); `4bpp: 0x12 → 0x21`, `0xAB → 0xBA`\n9. involution: applying the same table twice is the identity for all three tables — assert for all 256 byte values × 3 depths (768 assertions, 3 rows)\n10. `color_type` is **not consulted** — assert the same result for PALETTE and GRAY at the same bit depth\n\n### `png_do_strip_channel` — `pngtrans.c:521-642`\nSignature `(row_info, row, int at_start)`. Modifies `row_info->pixel_depth`, `channels`, `color_type`, `rowbytes`. Compiled because `PNG_WRITE_FILLER_SUPPORTED` and `PNG_READ_STRIP_ALPHA_SUPPORTED` are both on (`pnglibconf.h:142, 80`).\n\nTop-level dispatch is on **`channels`** (539, 587, 637):\n| # | channels | bit_depth | at_start | Line | Effect |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 2 | 8 | ≠0 | 543-544 | `++sp` — drops the *first* byte of each pixel pair (GA→G? no: `at_start != 0` means \"skip initial filler\", so this handles `AG`/`XG` layout per the comment at 534-535). `pixel_depth = 8`, `channels = 1`, `rowbytes = dp-row` |\n| 2 | 2 | 8 | 0 | 546-548 | `sp += 2; ++dp` — keeps the first channel, drops the second (`GA`/`GX`) |\n| 3 | 2 | 16 | ≠0 | 561-562 | `sp += 2` |\n| 4 | 2 | 16 | 0 | 563-566 | `sp += 4; dp += 2`. `pixel_depth = 16` |\n| 5 | 2 | **∉{8,16}** | any | 576-577 | **`return` with no change at all** — `rowbytes`/`channels` untouched. Rows: `bit_depth = 1, 2, 4` |\n| 6 | 2 | 8 or 16 | any | 582-583 | if `color_type == GRAY_ALPHA` → becomes `GRAY`; otherwise `color_type` left alone (e.g. a `GRAY` with a filler stays `GRAY`). 2 sub-rows |\n| 7 | 4 | 8 | ≠0 | 591-592 | `++sp` (`ARGB`/`XRGB`) → `pixel_depth = 24` |\n| 8 | 4 | 8 | 0 | 593-596 | `sp += 4; dp += 3` (`RGBA`/`RGBX`) |\n| 9 | 4 | 16 | ≠0 | 609-610 | `sp += 2` → `pixel_depth = 48` |\n| 10 | 4 | 16 | 0 | 611-614 | `sp += 8; dp += 6` |\n| 11 | 4 | ∉{8,16} | any | 627-628 | **`return` unchanged**. Rows: `1, 2, 4` |\n| 12 | 4 | 8 or 16 | any | 633-634 | `RGB_ALPHA` → `RGB`; else unchanged. 2 sub-rows |\n| 13 | **∉{2,4}** | any | any | 637-638 | **`return` unchanged** (\"filler channel has gone already\"). Rows: `channels = 1`, `3`, `0`, `5` |\n\nAdditional rows:\n14. **1-pixel-wide image**: the comment at 550 says \"For a 1 pixel wide image there is nothing to do\". `channels=2, bd=8, width=1, rowbytes=2, at_start=1` → `sp = row+1`, `ep = row+2`, so the `while (sp < ep)` runs **once** copying `row[1]` to `row[0]`. With `at_start=0`: `sp = row+2 == ep` → loop runs 0 times, `dp = row+1`, `rowbytes` becomes 1. Both are distinct rows worth pinning\n15. `rowbytes == 0` → `ep == row`, `sp > ep` immediately for the `at_start=0` cases → `rowbytes` becomes a *negative-then-huge* `size_t`? No: `dp` was advanced past `row`, so `dp-row > 0` while nothing was copied. `channels=4, bd=8, rowbytes=0, at_start=0` → `dp = row+3`, `rowbytes = 3` from a 0-byte row. Genuine edge-case row\n16. the final `row_info->rowbytes = (size_t)(dp-row)` (641) — assert the exact expected value for each of the 8 live combinations: `2ch/8bit` → `width`; `2ch/16bit` → `2*width`; `4ch/8bit` → `3*width`; `4ch/16bit` → `6*width`\n17. verify `pixel_depth` is set to `8 / 16 / 24 / 48` respectively and **not** derived from `channels*bit_depth` after the change\n18. multi-pixel data-correctness rows for each of the 8 combinations with a distinctive byte pattern\n\n### `png_do_bgr` — `pngtrans.c:647-721`\nOuter gate `(color_type & PNG_COLOR_MASK_COLOR) != 0` (652), then `bit_depth`, then exact `color_type`.\n1. **`(color_type & 0x02) == 0`** → **no-op**. Rows: `color_type = 0 (GRAY)`, `4 (GRAY_ALPHA)`\n2. **`color_type == 3 (PALETTE)`** — `PNG_COLOR_MASK_COLOR` is 2 and `3 & 2 == 2`, so it **enters** the outer `if`, but then matches neither `== PNG_COLOR_TYPE_RGB (2)` nor `== RGB_ALPHA (6)` → **no-op**. Sneaky row: PALETTE passes the mask test but does nothing\n3. **`bit_depth == 8, color_type == RGB (2)`** (655-657) — stride 3, swap bytes 0↔2. Rows: `width = 1`, `width = 3`\n4. **`bit_depth == 8, color_type == RGB_ALPHA (6)`** (670) — stride 4, swap 0↔2, alpha untouched\n5. **`bit_depth == 16, color_type == RGB`** (685-687, inside `#ifdef PNG_16BIT_SUPPORTED` at 684) — stride 6, swap `0↔4` **and** `1↔5`\n6. **`bit_depth == 16, color_type == RGB_ALPHA`** (703) — stride 8, swap `0↔4` and `1↔5`\n7. **`bit_depth ∈ {1,2,4}` with a COLOR color_type** — enters the outer `if`, matches neither `== 8` nor `== 16` → **no-op**. Rows: `color_type = 3, bit_depth = 4` (the only real-world case), `color_type = 2, bit_depth = 4` (invalid PNG but reachable)\n8. `width == 0` → empty loops for all four live arms\n9. **`row_info->rowbytes` is never consulted** — the loop is driven purely by `width`. A row asserting exactly `width*stride` bytes are touched\n10. involution: `png_do_bgr` twice → identity, for all four live arms\n11. assert green is untouched in the 8-bit arms and that both green bytes are untouched in the 16-bit arms\n\n---\n\n## 7. CRC\n\n### `png_reset_crc` — `png.c:139-144`\nNo branches. `png_ptr->crc = (png_uint_32)crc32(0, Z_NULL, 0)` — i.e. **0** with zlib.\n1. fresh struct → after the call, `crc == 0`\n2. call after a `png_calculate_crc` → resets back to 0 (idempotence row)\n3. call twice in a row\n\n### `png_calculate_crc` — `png.c:151-200`\n`need_crc` decision (154-167) crossed with the length loop (174-195).\n**`need_crc` classes:**\n1. **ancillary chunk** (`PNG_CHUNK_ANCILLARY(chunk_name) != 0`, i.e. bit 5 of byte 0 set — lowercase first letter, e.g. `tEXt`, `gAMA`) **and** `(flags & PNG_FLAG_CRC_ANCILLARY_MASK) == (USE|NOWARN)` → **`need_crc = 0`, CRC left untouched**. Reach via `png_set_crc_action(png_ptr, PNG_CRC_NO_CHANGE, PNG_CRC_QUIET_USE)` (`pngrtran.c` `PNG_CRC_QUIET_USE` arm sets exactly `USE|NOWARN`)\n2. ancillary with only `USE` (`PNG_CRC_WARN_USE`) → `need_crc = 1`\n3. ancillary with only `NOWARN` (`PNG_CRC_ERROR_QUIET`… actually `PNG_CRC_ERROR_QUIT` sets `NOWARN` alone) → `need_crc = 1`\n4. ancillary with neither (default) → `need_crc = 1`\n5. **critical chunk** (uppercase first letter, e.g. `IHDR`, `IDAT`) **and** `(flags & PNG_FLAG_CRC_CRITICAL_IGNORE) != 0` → **`need_crc = 0`**. Reach via `png_set_crc_action(png_ptr, PNG_CRC_QUIET_USE, ...)` which sets `CRITICAL_USE|CRITICAL_IGNORE`\n6. critical with `CRITICAL_USE` only (`PNG_CRC_WARN_USE`) → `need_crc = 1`\n7. critical, default flags → `need_crc = 1`\n**Length classes:**\n8. `length == 0` → the `if (need_crc != 0 && length > 0)` guard fails → **no-op** even when `need_crc == 1`\n9. `length == 1`\n10. `length` typical (e.g. 13 for IHDR)\n11. `length` such that `(uInt)length == 0` — the `if (safe_length == 0) safe_length = (uInt)-1` hack at 182-183. On LP64 `uInt` is 32-bit, so `length = 0x100000000` triggers it. Unreachable in practice; note (also gated by `#ifndef __COVERITY__`)\n12. multi-iteration `do/while` — only when `length > UINT_MAX`; unreachable on this platform with realistic buffers. Note\n13. known-answer test: `png_reset_crc` then `png_calculate_crc(ptr, \"IHDR-payload\", 13)` → compare against a reference `crc32`\n14. incremental equivalence: `calculate_crc(a, n)` then `calculate_crc(b, m)` == `calculate_crc(ab, n+m)`\n\n### `png_crc_read` — `pngrutil.c:225-233`\n1. `png_ptr == NULL` → early return (228-229)\n2. normal → `png_read_data` then `png_calculate_crc`\n3. `length == 0` → read of 0 bytes then a no-op CRC\n\n### `png_crc_error` — `pngrutil.c:238-299` — **`static`**, not exported\nReachable only through `png_crc_finish` / `png_crc_finish_critical`. Note the extra `handle_as_ancillary` parameter (new in this version; the old signature had none).\n1. `handle_as_ancillary != 0` → takes the ancillary branch regardless of `chunk_name` (271)\n2. `handle_as_ancillary == 0`, ancillary chunk name → ancillary branch\n3. `handle_as_ancillary == 0`, critical chunk name → critical branch (278)\n4. ancillary + `USE|NOWARN` → `need_crc = 0` → **always returns 0** (298), CRC never compared\n5. critical + `CRITICAL_IGNORE` → `need_crc = 0` → returns 0\n6. `need_crc = 1`, stored CRC matches → returns 0\n7. `need_crc = 1`, stored CRC differs → returns **non-zero**\n8. `io_state` side effect: always set to `PNG_IO_READING | PNG_IO_CHUNK_CRC` at 285 **even when `need_crc == 0`**, and the 4 CRC bytes are **always read** (289). Row asserting the stream position advances by 4 in every case\n\n### `png_crc_finish_critical` — `pngrutil.c:311-357` — `static`\n1. `skip == 0` → the `while (skip > 0)` loop is skipped\n2. `skip < PNG_INFLATE_BUF_SIZE` (1024) → one iteration\n3. `skip == 1024` → one iteration\n4. `skip == 1025` → **two** iterations (the `len = min(sizeof tmpbuf, skip)` split at 323-325)\n5. `skip` large (e.g. 5000) → 5 iterations\n6. **`handle_as_ancillary && (flags & PNG_FLAG_CRC_CRITICAL_IGNORE)`** → `handle_as_ancillary` is **reset to 0** at 336-338 (because `png_crc_read` never actually computed the CRC). Subtle row\n7. `png_crc_error(...) == 0` → returns 0\n8. `png_crc_error(...) != 0` and the ternary at 345-347 selects **warning**: (`handle_as_ancillary || ancillary-name`) with `ANCILLARY_NOWARN` clear → `png_chunk_warning(\"CRC error\")`, returns 1\n9. same, critical name with `CRITICAL_USE` set → `png_chunk_warning`, returns 1\n10. `png_crc_error != 0` and the ternary selects **error**: ancillary with `ANCILLARY_NOWARN` set → `png_chunk_error(\"CRC error\")` → **does not return** (longjmp)\n11. critical with `CRITICAL_USE` clear (the default) → `png_chunk_error` → longjmp\n\n### `png_crc_finish` — `pngrutil.c:359-363`\nThin wrapper: `png_crc_finish_critical(png_ptr, skip, 0)`. One row per `skip` class; the `handle_as_ancillary = 1` path is reached only by the PLTE/IEND handlers.\n\n### `png_set_crc_action` — `pngrtran.c:41-…` (the configuration knob for all of the above)\nTwo independent `switch`es. **`crit_action` arms:**\n1. `PNG_CRC_NO_CHANGE` → flags untouched\n2. `PNG_CRC_WARN_USE` → `CRITICAL_MASK` cleared, `CRITICAL_USE` set\n3. `PNG_CRC_QUIET_USE` → `CRITICAL_USE | CRITICAL_IGNORE`\n4. `PNG_CRC_WARN_DISCARD` → `png_warning(\"Can't discard critical data on CRC error\")` then **falls through** to the default → mask cleared\n5. `PNG_CRC_ERROR_QUIT` / `PNG_CRC_DEFAULT` / any other value → mask cleared\n**`ancil_action` arms:**\n6. `PNG_CRC_NO_CHANGE` → untouched\n7. `PNG_CRC_WARN_USE` → `ANCILLARY_USE`\n8. `PNG_CRC_QUIET_USE` → `ANCILLARY_USE | ANCILLARY_NOWARN`\n9. `PNG_CRC_ERROR_QUIT` → `ANCILLARY_NOWARN` alone\n10. `PNG_CRC_WARN_DISCARD` / `PNG_CRC_DEFAULT` / other → mask cleared\n11. `png_ptr == NULL` → early return\nCross-product: 6 × 6 = 36 flag states, of which the 4 distinct behavioral classes per chunk-criticality matter. Recommend 6 + 6 rows plus the 4 `png_calculate_crc`/`png_crc_error` behavior rows.\n\n---\n\n## 8. `png_check_keyword` — `pngset.c:1980-2055`\n\n`png_uint_32 png_check_keyword(png_structrp png_ptr, png_const_charp key, png_bytep new_key)`. `new_key` must be ≥ 80 bytes. Returns the resulting length, **0 meaning \"no valid keyword could be generated\"**. State vars: `key_len`, `bad_character`, `space` (initialized to **1**, so a leading space is treated as \"already in a space run\").\n\n1. **`key == NULL`** → `*new_key = 0`, return 0 (1992-1996)\n2. **`key == \"\"`** → loop never runs, `key_len == 0`, the trailing-space adjust is skipped (`key_len > 0` false), `*new_key = 0`, **return 0** (2033-2034)\n3. **all-space key** `\"   \"` → every char is a space with `space == 1` already, so the `else if (bad_character == 0)` arm at 2019-2020 records `bad_character = 32` and nothing is emitted → `key_len == 0` → **return 0**\n4. **valid printable run** `\"Title\"` → `key_len = 5`, exact copy, no warning\n5. **char class boundaries** (test at 2002: `(ch > 32 && ch <= 126) || (ch >= 161)`):\n   - `ch == 33` (`'!'`) → accepted (first accepted low char)\n   - `ch == 32` (space) → *not* in the accepted class\n   - `ch == 126` (`'~'`) → accepted (last accepted low char)\n   - `ch == 127` (DEL) → **rejected**\n   - `ch == 128`…`160` → **rejected** (includes the non-breaking space 0xA0 at 160, per the doc comment at 1973-1974)\n   - `ch == 161` → **accepted** (first accepted high char)\n   - `ch == 255` → accepted\n   - `ch == 1` (control) → rejected\n6. **leading space** `\" Title\"` → `space` starts at 1, so the leading space hits arm 2019 → `bad_character = 32`, not emitted; result `\"Title\"`, `key_len = 5`, warning fired\n7. **trailing space** `\"Title \"` → the space is emitted by arm 2007-2012 (`space == 0`), then the post-loop fixup at 2023-2028 does `--key_len; --new_key;` and sets `bad_character = 32` → result `\"Title\"`, warning fired\n8. **multiple internal spaces** `\"A  B\"` → first space emitted (`space` 0→1), second space hits arm 2019 (`bad_character` already 32 so nothing changes) → result `\"A B\"`, `key_len = 3`\n9. **single internal space** `\"A B\"` → `\"A B\"`, `key_len = 3`, `bad_character == 0` → **no warning**\n10. **invalid char mid-run** `\"A\\tB\"` → tab with `space == 0` → arm 2007 emits a **space** and sets `bad_character = 9` → result `\"A B\"`, warning `\"keyword \\\"A\\tB\\\": bad character '0x09'\"` (uses `PNG_NUMBER_FORMAT_02x` at 2046)\n11. **invalid char after a space** `\"A \\tB\"` → tab with `space == 1` → arm 2019, silently skipped (`bad_character` becomes 32 from the earlier space, so the *first* error wins — the comment at 2020 says \"record the first error\"). Result `\"A B\"`\n12. **exactly 79 chars** → loop condition `key_len < 79` is satisfied for all → `key_len = 79`, `*key == 0` after the loop → **no truncation warning**\n13. **exactly 80 chars** → loop stops with `key_len == 79`, `*key != 0` → `png_warning(\"keyword truncated\")` (2038-2039). First truncating length\n14. **200 chars** → truncated to 79, warning\n15. **79 chars where the 79th is a space** → trailing-space fixup makes `key_len = 78`, `bad_character = 32`, and `*key != 0`?? — after the loop `key` points at char 80 which is NUL for an 80-char input; the truncation warning takes precedence over the bad-character warning (the `if`/`else if` at 2038/2041). Warning-precedence row\n16. **truncation *and* bad character** → only the truncation warning fires (else-if precedence). Distinct row\n17. **only one warning per keyword** — the comment at 2037 promises this; assert with a key containing 5 bad characters that exactly 1 warning fires\n18. return-value contract: assert `strlen((char*)new_key) == return value` for every success row\n19. `new_key` NUL-termination at 2031 in every path including the `key_len == 0` returns\n20. `PNG_WARNINGS_SUPPORTED` is on, so the `orig_key` save at 1984 and the whole warning block compile. If you can build with warnings off, the `PNG_UNUSED(png_ptr)` `#else` at 2051 is a second config row.\nCallers (each a separate integration row): `png_write_iCCP` (`pngwutil.c:1151`), `png_write_sPLT` (1191), `png_write_tEXt` (1577), `png_write_zTXt` (1630), `png_write_iTXt` (1673), `png_write_pCAL` (1799).\n\n---\n\n## 9. MEMORY — `pngmem.c` (287 lines total)\n\n### `png_malloc_base` — `pngmem.c:67-99` (exported)\nThe single choke point. `PNG_MAX_MALLOC_64K` is **not** defined here, so the block at 76-84 is out.\n1. **`size > PNG_SIZE_MAX`** → return NULL (88). On LP64 `PNG_SIZE_MAX == SIZE_MAX` and `png_alloc_size_t == size_t`, so this is unreachable. Mark so; it becomes live on 32-bit\n2. **`png_ptr != NULL && png_ptr->malloc_fn != NULL`** → calls `png_ptr->malloc_fn(png_ptr, size)` (91-92). **Row: user allocator is used, and `png_ptr` is passed non-const-cast**\n3. **`png_ptr == NULL`** → falls through to system `malloc` (98). Row (this is exactly what `png_malloc_default` does at 200)\n4. **`png_ptr != NULL && malloc_fn == NULL`** → system `malloc`. Row\n5. `size == 0` → `malloc(0)`: implementation-defined non-NULL (glibc) → returned as success. Row worth pinning\n6. large-but-satisfiable size\n7. allocation failure → NULL propagated to the caller **without** any error/warning (the doc comment at 64-65 says checking must happen outside)\n\n### `png_malloc` — `pngmem.c:172-187` (exported)\n1. **`png_ptr == NULL`** → return NULL, **no error** (178-179)\n2. success → returns the pointer\n3. `png_malloc_base` returns NULL → **`png_error(png_ptr, \"Out of memory\")`** (184) — never returns. The distinguishing message is lowercase-`m` \"Out of memory\" (comment: \"'m' means png_malloc\")\n4. `size == 0` with `png_ptr != NULL` → whatever `malloc(0)` gives\n5. with a user `malloc_fn` installed → user path + error-on-NULL\n6. with a user `malloc_fn` that returns NULL → `png_error`\n\n### `png_malloc_default` — `pngmem.c:190-206` (exported, `PNG_DEPRECATED`, only with `PNG_USER_MEM_SUPPORTED` — on here)\n1. `png_ptr == NULL` → NULL\n2. success — **note it passes `NULL` as `png_ptr` to `png_malloc_base` (line 200), deliberately bypassing the user allocator**. Key row: install a `malloc_fn` that counts calls, call `png_malloc_default`, assert the counter did **not** increment\n3. failure → `png_error(png_ptr, \"Out of Memory\")` — **capital `M`**, distinguishable from `png_malloc`'s message (comment at 203). Row asserting the exact message\n\n### `png_malloc_warn` — `pngmem.c:213-228` (exported)\n1. **`png_ptr == NULL`** → returns NULL with **no warning** (the whole body is inside `if (png_ptr != NULL)`)\n2. success → pointer\n3. failure → **`png_warning(png_ptr, \"Out of memory\")`** then returns NULL — the caller must handle NULL. Row asserting execution continues\n4. with a user `malloc_fn` → goes through it\n5. `size == 0`\nUsed by `png_zalloc` (`png.c:126`), `png_create_png_struct` (`png.c:332`), `png_set_longjmp_fn` (`pngerror.c:570`), and most of `pngset.c`.\n\n### `png_calloc` — `pngmem.c:48-60` (exported)\n1. `png_ptr == NULL` → `png_malloc` returns NULL → the `if (ret != NULL)` guard skips the memset → returns NULL\n2. success → **assert all `size` bytes are zero**\n3. `size == 0` → `malloc(0)` non-NULL → `memset(ret, 0, 0)` is a no-op → returns the pointer\n4. failure → `png_malloc` raises `png_error` first, so `png_calloc` never sees NULL from a non-NULL `png_ptr`. Note: the `ret != NULL` check at 56 is therefore only meaningful for the `png_ptr == NULL` case\n5. large size → verify the whole region is zeroed (used by `png_set_PLTE` at `pngset.c:815, 817` for 256 `png_color` entries)\n\n### `png_malloc_array` — `pngmem.c:120-129` (exported) + `png_malloc_array_checked` — `pngmem.c:107-118` (static)\nCompiled because `PNG_TEXT_SUPPORTED || PNG_sPLT_SUPPORTED || PNG_STORE_UNKNOWN_CHUNKS_SUPPORTED` (guard at 101-102) — all on.\n1. **`nelements <= 0`** → `png_error(png_ptr, \"internal error: array alloc\")` (125-126). Rows: `0`, `-1`, `INT_MIN`\n2. **`element_size == 0`** → same `png_error`\n3. success: `nelements = 1`, `element_size = 1`\n4. success: `nelements = 8, element_size = sizeof(png_text)`\n5. **`req > PNG_SIZE_MAX/element_size`** → `png_malloc_array_checked` returns NULL (117), and `png_malloc_array` **returns NULL without any error/warning**. Rows: `nelements = INT_MAX, element_size = SIZE_MAX/2`; `nelements = INT_MAX, element_size = 1024` on a 32-bit build\n6. exact boundary: `nelements * element_size == PNG_SIZE_MAX` → the `<=` at 113 accepts → the underlying `malloc` fails → NULL\n7. `nelements = INT_MAX, element_size = 1` → arithmetic fine, `malloc` fails → NULL\n8. contents are **not** zeroed by this function (unlike `png_realloc_array`)\n\n### `png_realloc_array` — `pngmem.c:131-165` (exported)\n1. **`add_elements <= 0`** → `png_error(\"internal error: array realloc\")` (137-139). Rows: `0`, `-1`\n2. **`element_size == 0`** → same error\n3. **`old_elements < 0`** → same error\n4. **`old_array == NULL && old_elements > 0`** → same error. Distinct 4th condition\n5. **`old_array == NULL && old_elements == 0`** → **valid** (the fresh-allocation case). Row\n6. **`add_elements > INT_MAX - old_elements`** → skips the whole block, **returns NULL** (164). Rows: `old_elements = INT_MAX, add_elements = 1`; `old_elements = INT_MAX/2+1, add_elements = INT_MAX/2+1`\n7. exact boundary: `add_elements == INT_MAX - old_elements` → **accepted** (the `<=` at 144)\n8. `png_malloc_array_checked` fails → returns NULL, and **the old array is not freed** (the caller owns it; see `png_set_text_2` at `pngset.c:998-1004` reporting via `png_chunk_report` and *keeping* the old array)\n9. **`old_elements == 0`** → the `memcpy` at 154-155 is skipped, the whole new array is memset to 0\n10. **`old_elements > 0`** → `memcpy` of `element_size*old_elements` bytes, then **memset of the `add_elements` tail to 0** (157-158). Row asserting: old contents preserved AND new tail zeroed\n11. non-overlapping guarantee: `new_array` is a fresh block, so `old_array` remains valid and readable after the call — this is exactly what the deferred-free pattern in `png_set_text_2` (`pngset.c:1009, 1148`), `png_set_sPLT` (`pngset.c:1312, 1376`) and `png_set_unknown_chunks` (`pngset.c:1476, 1523`) relies on. High-value aliasing row\n12. `element_size` = 1, 2, `sizeof(png_sPLT_t)`, `sizeof(png_unknown_chunk)`\n\n### `png_free` — `pngmem.c:233-256` (exported)\nNote the unusual `#ifdef` structure: with `PNG_USER_MEM_SUPPORTED` on, `png_free`'s body *falls into* `png_free_default`'s definition.\n1. **`png_ptr == NULL`** → return, no-op (236-237)\n2. **`ptr == NULL`** → return, no-op. Row: `png_free(valid_ptr, NULL)` is explicitly documented as safe (comment 230-231)\n3. **both NULL** → no-op\n4. **`png_ptr->free_fn != NULL`** → calls the user free (240-241). Row: counting free_fn\n5. **`free_fn == NULL`** → `png_free_default` → system `free` (244, 255)\n6. double-free protection: **none**. Note only\n7. freeing a pointer obtained from `png_malloc_default` while a `free_fn` is installed → **mismatch**: the user `free_fn` is called on a system-`malloc`'d pointer. Genuine hazard row worth documenting in the matrix\n\n### `png_free_default` — `pngmem.c:247-256` (exported, `PNG_DEPRECATED`)\n1. `png_ptr == NULL` → no-op (251-252)\n2. `ptr == NULL` → no-op\n3. valid → system `free`, **bypassing any installed `free_fn`**. Pair with the `png_malloc_default` row\n\n### `png_destroy_png_struct` — `pngmem.c:23-40` (`PRIVATE`)\n1. `png_ptr == NULL` → no-op\n2. valid: makes a stack copy (`dummy_struct`, line 31), **zeroes the real struct** (32), then `png_free(&dummy_struct, png_ptr)` — so the user `free_fn` and `mem_ptr` survive the zeroing. Row: install a `free_fn` that reads `png_get_mem_ptr(png_ptr)` and assert it still sees the original `mem_ptr`\n3. `PNG_SETJMP_SUPPORTED` on → also calls `png_free_jmpbuf(&dummy_struct)` (37). Rows: jmpbuf was local (`jmp_buf_size == 0`) vs heap-allocated (`jmp_buf_size > 0`)\n\n### `png_set_mem_fn` — `pngmem.c:262-272` (exported, `PNG_USER_MEM_SUPPORTED` only)\n1. **`png_ptr == NULL`** → no-op\n2. all three params non-NULL → stored\n3. `mem_ptr == NULL`, fns non-NULL\n4. `malloc_fn == NULL`, `free_fn` non-NULL → `png_malloc_base` uses system malloc but `png_free` uses the user free. **Asymmetric configuration row — a real hazard**\n5. `malloc_fn` non-NULL, `free_fn == NULL` → the mirror hazard\n6. both NULL → reverts to system allocator\n7. called twice → second wins; any pointers allocated under the first allocator are now freed by the second. Row\n8. called on the `&create_struct` stack struct from inside `png_create_png_struct` (`png.c:298`) — this is *before* error handling is set up (comment at 296-297). Row: a `malloc_fn` that is invoked during `png_create_read_struct_2` before the real `png_ptr` exists\n\n### `png_get_mem_ptr` — `pngmem.c:278-285` (exported)\n1. `png_ptr == NULL` → NULL\n2. never set → NULL (zeroed at struct creation)\n3. set via `png_set_mem_fn` → that pointer\n4. set via `png_create_read_struct_2`'s `mem_ptr` arg → that pointer\n5. read from *inside* the `malloc_fn`/`free_fn` callback — the documented use. Row\n\n### `png_zalloc` — `png.c:103-127` (`PRIVATE` but exported)\nzlib's `alloc_func`, signature `(voidpf png_ptr, uInt items, uInt size)`.\n1. **`png_ptr == NULL`** → NULL (109-110)\n2. **`size != 0 && items >= (~(png_alloc_size_t)0)/size`** → `png_warning(\"Potential overflow in png_zalloc()\")` and NULL (118-123). Note the comment at 112-117 calls this vestigial. On LP64 with `uInt` = 32 bits this needs `items*size >= 2^64`, impossible → **unreachable**; mark so\n3. **`size == 0`** → the overflow check is skipped, `num_bytes = 0 * items = 0` → `png_malloc_warn(png_ptr, 0)`. Row\n4. **`items == 0`** → `num_bytes = 0`. Row\n5. normal: `items = 1, size = 8192` → 8192 bytes via `png_malloc_warn`\n6. allocation failure → `png_malloc_warn` warns and returns NULL, which zlib will turn into `Z_MEM_ERROR`. Row\n7. with a user `malloc_fn` installed → zlib allocations go through it. Row (this is the reason `png_zalloc` exists)\n8. `png_ptr` is passed as `voidpf` and cast with `png_voidcast(png_structrp, png_ptr)` — the `zstream.opaque` back-pointer is wired up at `png.c:1088-1090` (`create_struct.zstream.opaque = png_ptr`). Row: assert `zstream.zalloc == png_zalloc` on a fresh struct\n\n### `png_zfree` — `png.c:130-134`\n1. `png_ptr == NULL` → `png_free` no-ops\n2. `ptr == NULL` → no-op\n3. normal → `png_free`, honoring any user `free_fn`\n4. assert `zstream.zfree == png_zfree` on a fresh struct\n\n### `png_free_data` — `png.c:482-683` (exported)\n`void png_free_data(png_ptr, info_ptr, png_uint_32 mask, int num)`. **Every** block is gated on `(mask & PNG_FREE_xxx) & info_ptr->free_me` — i.e. the bit must be in **both** the caller's mask and `free_me`.\n\n1. **`png_ptr == NULL`** → no-op (488-489)\n2. **`info_ptr == NULL`** → no-op\n\nPer-bit rows (each needs: allocate via the matching `png_set_*`, verify `free_me` bit set, call with the bit, verify pointer NULLed and `valid` flag cleared):\n\n| Mask bit | Value | png.c lines | `#ifdef` gate | Fields cleared | `valid` bit cleared | `num`-sensitive? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `PNG_FREE_TEXT` | 0x4000 | 491-515 | `PNG_TEXT_SUPPORTED` ✓ | `text[i].key` (all), `text`, `num_text`, `max_text` | — (none) | **YES** |\n| `PNG_FREE_TRNS` | 0x2000 | 517-526 | `PNG_tRNS_SUPPORTED` ✓ | `trans_alpha`, `num_trans` | `PNG_INFO_tRNS` | no |\n| `PNG_FREE_SCAL` | 0x0100 | 528-538 | `PNG_sCAL_SUPPORTED` ✓ | `scal_s_width`, `scal_s_height` | `PNG_INFO_sCAL` | no |\n| `PNG_FREE_PCAL` | 0x0080 | 540-561 | `PNG_pCAL_SUPPORTED` ✓ | `pcal_purpose`, `pcal_units`, `pcal_params[0..nparams-1]`, `pcal_params` | `PNG_INFO_pCAL` | no |\n| `PNG_FREE_ICCP` | 0x0010 | 563-573 | `PNG_iCCP_SUPPORTED` ✓ | `iccp_name`, `iccp_profile` | `PNG_INFO_iCCP` | no |\n| `PNG_FREE_SPLT` | 0x0020 | 575-604 | `PNG_sPLT_SUPPORTED` ✓ | `splt_palettes[i].name/.entries`, `splt_palettes`, `splt_palettes_num` | `PNG_INFO_sPLT` (**only in the `num == -1` arm**, line 601) | **YES** |\n| `PNG_FREE_UNKN` | 0x0200 | 606-628 | `PNG_STORE_UNKNOWN_CHUNKS_SUPPORTED` ✓ | `unknown_chunks[i].data`, `unknown_chunks`, `unknown_chunks_num` | — | **YES** |\n| `PNG_FREE_EXIF` | 0x8000 | 630-641 | `PNG_eXIf_SUPPORTED` ✓ | `exif` | `PNG_INFO_eXIf` | no |\n| `PNG_FREE_HIST` | 0x0008 | 643-651 | `PNG_hIST_SUPPORTED` ✓ | `hist` | `PNG_INFO_hIST` | no |\n| `PNG_FREE_PLTE` | 0x1000 | 653-660 | **always compiled** | `palette`, `num_palette` | `PNG_INFO_PLTE` | no |\n| `PNG_FREE_ROWS` | 0x0040 | 662-677 | `PNG_INFO_IMAGE_SUPPORTED` ✓ | `row_pointers[0..height-1]`, `row_pointers` | `PNG_INFO_IDAT` | no |\n\nFull `PNG_FREE_*` constant list from `png.h:1838-1853`:\n```\nPNG_FREE_HIST 0x0008U   PNG_FREE_ICCP 0x0010U   PNG_FREE_SPLT 0x0020U\nPNG_FREE_ROWS 0x0040U   PNG_FREE_PCAL 0x0080U   PNG_FREE_SCAL 0x0100U\nPNG_FREE_UNKN 0x0200U   (only if PNG_STORE_UNKNOWN_CHUNKS_SUPPORTED, png.h:1844-1846)\nPNG_FREE_PLTE 0x1000U   PNG_FREE_TRNS 0x2000U   PNG_FREE_TEXT 0x4000U\nPNG_FREE_EXIF 0x8000U   PNG_FREE_ALL  0xffffU   PNG_FREE_MUL  0x4220U\n```\nNote: **0x0001, 0x0002, 0x0004, 0x0800, and 0x0400 are unused.** 0x0400 was `PNG_FREE_LIST`, \"removed in 1.6.0 because it is ignored\" (comment at `png.h:1847`). `PNG_FREE_MUL = 0x4220 = SPLT|TEXT|UNKN` — the three array-valued items.\n\n**The `num` argument — the key axis (lines 496, 580, 610, 679-682):**\n3. **`num == -1`** → \"free everything\": the array itself is freed, the count zeroed, and (for SPLT) `PNG_INFO_sPLT` cleared. Then at 679 the `mask &= ~PNG_FREE_MUL` is **skipped**, so `free_me` loses the TEXT/SPLT/UNKN bits too\n4. **`num >= 0`** for `PNG_FREE_TEXT` → frees only `text[num].key` and NULLs it; **`num_text` is NOT decremented**, leaving a hole in the array. Then `mask &= ~PNG_FREE_MUL` at 680 means `free_me` **keeps** its TEXT bit so a later `num == -1` call still works. This deferred-ownership interaction is the single most important row in this section\n5. **`num >= 0`** for `PNG_FREE_SPLT` → frees `splt_palettes[num].name` and `.entries`; `PNG_INFO_sPLT` stays set (contrast with the `-1` arm)\n6. **`num >= 0`** for `PNG_FREE_UNKN` → frees `unknown_chunks[num].data` only\n7. **`num >= 0`** for a non-MUL bit (e.g. `PNG_FREE_TRNS`) → `num` is ignored entirely, the full free happens, **but** then `mask &= ~PNG_FREE_MUL` at 680 doesn't affect TRNS so `free_me`'s TRNS bit *is* cleared at 682. Row: `png_free_data(p, i, PNG_FREE_TRNS, 0)` behaves identically to `num == -1`\n8. **`num` out of range** (e.g. `num == 5` with `num_text == 2`) → out-of-bounds access. Documented hazard; note but don't test\n9. **`num >= 0` with `mask == PNG_FREE_ALL`** → the array-valued items get their single-element treatment while everything else is fully freed, and `free_me` keeps TEXT|SPLT|UNKN. Complex composite row\n10. **guard: `info_ptr->text != NULL`** (493) — mask bit set + `free_me` bit set but `text == NULL` → block skipped. Same shape for `splt_palettes` (577) and `unknown_chunks` (607). Note `TRNS`, `SCAL`, `PCAL`, `ICCP`, `EXIF`, `HIST`, `PLTE`, `ROWS` have **no such NULL guard on the container** and rely on `png_free(NULL)` being safe\n11. **`PNG_FREE_ROWS` with `row_pointers != NULL`** → loops `row < info_ptr->height` (669) — so **`info_ptr->height` must be correct**. Row: set `row_pointers` with `png_set_rows` but leave `height == 0` → nothing is freed per-row (leak), only the outer array. Genuine hazard row\n12. **`mask == 0`** → no-op except `info_ptr->free_me &= ~0` (682) which is also a no-op\n13. **`mask == PNG_FREE_ALL, num == -1`** on a fully-populated info → everything freed, `free_me == 0`\n14. **`free_me` bit clear** (application-owned data, after `png_data_freer(..., PNG_USER_WILL_FREE_DATA, ...)`) → the block is skipped, the pointer is **left dangling but not freed**. Row per bit — 11 rows, or one composite\n15. **idempotence**: calling `png_free_data(p, i, PNG_FREE_ALL, -1)` twice → second call is a no-op because `free_me == 0`\n16. `PNG_INFO_*` flags cleared vs. not: assert `png_get_valid` afterwards for each bit (note TEXT and UNKN clear **no** `valid` flag, since text/unknown chunks aren't tracked in `valid`)\n\n### `png_data_freer` — `png.c:463-480` (exported)\n`void png_data_freer(png_ptr, info_ptr, int freer, png_uint_32 mask)`.\nCrucially **`PNG_DESTROY_WILL_FREE_DATA == PNG_SET_WILL_FREE_DATA == 1`** (`png.h:1834-1835`) — they are the *same value*, so there are only **two** live `freer` branches, not three.\n1. **`png_ptr == NULL`** → no-op (469-470)\n2. **`info_ptr == NULL`** → no-op\n3. **`freer == 1`** (`PNG_DESTROY_WILL_FREE_DATA` / `PNG_SET_WILL_FREE_DATA`) → **`free_me |= mask`** (472-473). Libpng takes ownership\n4. **`freer == 2`** (`PNG_USER_WILL_FREE_DATA`) → **`free_me &= ~mask`** (475-476). Application takes ownership\n5. **any other `freer`** → **`png_error(\"Unknown freer parameter in png_data_freer\")`** (478-479). Rows: `0`, `3`, `-1`, `100`\n6. **`freer == PNG_SET_WILL_FREE_DATA`** → identical observable effect to `PNG_DESTROY_WILL_FREE_DATA`. Row asserting they are interchangeable\n\n**Cross-product matrix you asked for — `freer` × mask bit.** Because only 2 `freer` values are distinct and there are 11 live mask bits, the meaningful matrix is **2 × 11 = 22 rows**, each of the form: (a) populate the item via its `png_set_*`, (b) assert `free_me` bit is set, (c) call `png_data_freer(p, i, freer, BIT)`, (d) assert `free_me` bit is now set/clear as expected, (e) call `png_free_data(p, i, BIT, -1)` and assert the memory was/was not freed.\n\nRecommended row list:\n- `(1, PNG_FREE_HIST)`, `(2, PNG_FREE_HIST)`\n- `(1, PNG_FREE_ICCP)`, `(2, PNG_FREE_ICCP)`\n- `(1, PNG_FREE_SPLT)`, `(2, PNG_FREE_SPLT)`\n- `(1, PNG_FREE_ROWS)`, `(2, PNG_FREE_ROWS)`\n- `(1, PNG_FREE_PCAL)`, `(2, PNG_FREE_PCAL)`\n- `(1, PNG_FREE_SCAL)`, `(2, PNG_FREE_SCAL)`\n- `(1, PNG_FREE_UNKN)`, `(2, PNG_FREE_UNKN)`\n- `(1, PNG_FREE_PLTE)`, `(2, PNG_FREE_PLTE)`\n- `(1, PNG_FREE_TRNS)`, `(2, PNG_FREE_TRNS)`\n- `(1, PNG_FREE_TEXT)`, `(2, PNG_FREE_TEXT)`\n- `(1, PNG_FREE_EXIF)`, `(2, PNG_FREE_EXIF)`\nPlus composite rows: `(1, PNG_FREE_ALL)`, `(2, PNG_FREE_ALL)`, `(1, PNG_FREE_MUL)`, `(2, PNG_FREE_MUL)`, `(1, 0)`, `(2, 0)`, `(1, 0x0400)` (the removed `PNG_FREE_LIST` bit — sets a `free_me` bit that nothing reads), `(1, 0xFFFFFFFF)` (bits above 0xffff — same, silently stored). And the 4 `png_error` rows for invalid `freer`.\n\n### `png_create_read_struct_2` / `png_create_write_struct_2` with user allocators\n`pngread.c:41-79` and `pngwrite.c:558-…`, both funneling into `png_create_png_struct` (`png.c:258-359`).\n1. **all three of `mem_ptr`/`malloc_fn`/`free_fn` NULL** → equivalent to `png_create_read_struct` (which itself calls the `_2` form because `PNG_USER_MEM_SUPPORTED` is on, `pngread.c:32-33`). Row asserting equivalence\n2. **`malloc_fn` non-NULL** → the very first allocation (the `png_struct` itself, via `png_malloc_warn(&create_struct, sizeof *png_ptr)` at `png.c:332`) goes through the user allocator. Row: counting allocator sees exactly 1 call for a minimal create\n3. **`malloc_fn` returns NULL** → `png_malloc_warn` warns, `png_ptr == NULL`, `png_create_png_struct` returns **NULL** (line 358) and the `_2` function returns NULL. Row asserting a clean NULL return, not a crash\n4. **`free_fn` non-NULL** → exercised by `png_destroy_read_struct` → `png_destroy_png_struct` → `png_free`\n5. **`mem_ptr` non-NULL, both fns NULL** → `mem_ptr` is stored and retrievable via `png_get_mem_ptr` but never used. Row\n6. **`malloc_fn` set, `free_fn` NULL** → asymmetric; allocations from the user pool are released with system `free`. Hazard row\n7. **`free_fn` set, `malloc_fn` NULL** → mirror hazard\n8. **user allocator called before error handling exists**: `png_set_mem_fn(&create_struct, ...)` at `png.c:298` precedes `png_set_error_fn` at `png.c:310` and the `setjmp` at 314. A `malloc_fn` that calls `png_error` at this point longjmps to `create_jmp_buf` (set at 322-324) and yields a NULL return. Row\n9. **`user_png_ver` interaction**: `png_user_version_check` (`png.c:205-…`) runs *before* the `png_struct` allocation (line 328), so a bad version string means the user allocator is **never called**. Row: pass `\"1.5.0\"` and assert zero allocator invocations and a NULL return\n10. read-vs-write struct differences: `png_create_read_struct_2` sets `mode = PNG_IS_READ_STRUCT` (`pngread.c:51`), `IDAT_read_size = PNG_IDAT_READ_SIZE` (57), `flags |= PNG_FLAG_BENIGN_ERRORS_WARN` (60), and calls `png_set_read_fn(png_ptr, NULL, NULL)` (74) which installs `png_default_read_data`. **`PNG_FLAG_APP_WARNINGS_WARN` is NOT set** because `PNG_RELEASE_BUILD == 0` in this BETA build (63-65). Two rows: read struct vs write struct default `flags`\n11. default limits from `png_create_png_struct` (`png.c:275-292`): `user_width_max == user_height_max == 1000000`, `user_chunk_cache_max == 1000`, `user_chunk_malloc_max == 8000000`. Row asserting each via the corresponding getter\n12. `zstream.zalloc/zfree/opaque` wiring at `png.c:1086-1090`. Row\n\n### `PNG_ALIGNED_MEMORY_SUPPORTED` — explicit finding\nDefined at `pnglibconf.h:19` but **never referenced anywhere in `pngmem.c`**. The only uses in the whole tree are `pngrutil.c:4618` and `pngpriv.h:139/175/185` (gating hardware-accelerated filter/palette code). **Zero test rows needed in the memory area**; it does not change alignment of any `png_malloc` result. Worth one negative row documenting that `png_malloc` results have only `malloc`'s natural alignment.\n\n---\n\n## 10. `pngerror.c` — the on/off configuration axis\n\n### `png_set_error_fn` — `pngerror.c:717-731`\n1. `png_ptr == NULL` → no-op\n2. all set → `error_ptr`, `error_fn`, `warning_fn` stored\n3. `error_fn == NULL`, `warning_fn` set → errors go to `png_default_error`, warnings to the app\n4. `error_fn` set, `warning_fn == NULL` → warnings go to `png_default_warning` (stderr)\n5. both NULL → full default behavior\n6. `error_ptr == NULL` with fns set\n7. `PNG_WARNINGS_SUPPORTED` on → `warning_fn` is actually stored (726-727). If off, the `#else PNG_UNUSED(warning_fn)` at 729 discards it silently — a second config row if you can rebuild\n8. called twice → second wins\n9. **the error_fn must not return** — the contract at 712-716. Row: an `error_fn` that *does* return → falls through to `png_default_error` at `png_error`'s line 48. This is a documented, deliberately-supported fallthrough and is a must-test row\n\n### `png_get_error_ptr` — `pngerror.c:738-745`\n1. `png_ptr == NULL` → NULL\n2. never set → NULL\n3. set → that pointer\n4. read from inside the `error_fn`/`warning_fn` callback — the documented use\n5. note the simplified API reuses `error_ptr` to hold a `png_imagep` (`pngerror.c:768, 803`)\n\n### `png_set_longjmp_fn` — `pngerror.c:543-610`\n`jmp_buf* png_set_longjmp_fn(png_ptr, png_longjmp_ptr longjmp_fn, size_t jmp_buf_size)`. This is what the `png_jmpbuf(png_ptr)` macro expands to.\n1. **`png_ptr == NULL`** → NULL (557-558)\n2. **first call, `jmp_buf_size <= sizeof(png_ptr->jmp_buf_local)`** → returns `&jmp_buf_local`, `jmp_buf_size` stays **0** meaning \"not allocated\" (562-565). This is the normal `png_jmpbuf()` path\n3. **first call, `jmp_buf_size > sizeof(jmp_buf_local)`** → `png_malloc_warn` (569-570); on success `jmp_buf_size` records the size\n4. **first call, larger size, allocation fails** → **returns NULL** (572-573). \"new NULL return on OOM\". Row\n5. **second call, same size, `jmp_buf_size == 0`** (local) and `jmp_buf_ptr == &jmp_buf_local` → `size` recomputed as `sizeof(jmp_buf_local)`; if it equals `jmp_buf_size` argument → falls through to 608, `longjmp_fn` updated, returns the same pointer\n6. **second call, `jmp_buf_size == 0` but `jmp_buf_ptr != &jmp_buf_local`** → **`png_error(\"Libpng jmp_buf still allocated\")`** (593). Internal-error branch; comment 588-592 says it's a png_error to make it easy to detect. Hard to reach legitimately; note\n7. **second call with a *different* `jmp_buf_size`** → `png_warning(\"Application jmp_buf size changed\")` and **returns NULL** (600-601). Comment: \"caller will probably crash: no choice here\". Must-test row\n8. **`longjmp_fn` can be changed on every call** (comment 605-607) even when the buffer is reused. Row: call with `longjmp`, then with a custom `longjmp_fn`, and assert the custom one is used by `png_longjmp`\n9. `longjmp_fn == NULL` → stored; `png_longjmp` then skips the call (its guard at 676) and goes straight to `PNG_ABORT()`. Row\n10. `jmp_buf_size == 0` → `0 <= sizeof(jmp_buf_local)` → local buffer. Row\n11. `jmp_buf_size == SIZE_MAX` → `png_malloc_warn` fails → NULL\n12. exact boundary `jmp_buf_size == sizeof(jmp_buf)` → local; `sizeof(jmp_buf)+1` → heap. Boundary pair\nGated by `PNG_SETJMP_SUPPORTED` (`pnglibconf.h:113`) — on. Without it the whole 539-650 block vanishes and `png_jmpbuf` is undefined.\n\n### `png_free_jmpbuf` — `pngerror.c:612-649` (`PRIVATE`, exported)\n1. `png_ptr == NULL` → no-op\n2. `jmp_buf_ptr == NULL` → skips the free, still zeroes the three fields (644-647)\n3. **`jmp_buf_size == 0`** (local allocation) → skips the free block entirely, zeroes the fields\n4. **`jmp_buf_size > 0` and `jb != &jmp_buf_local`** → the elaborate self-protecting free at 629-641: sets up a *local* `free_jmp_buf`, points `jmp_buf_ptr` at it, sets `jmp_buf_size = 0` and `longjmp_fn = longjmp`, then `png_free(png_ptr, jb)`. Rows: normal free; and a user `free_fn` that calls `png_error` → longjmps back to `free_jmp_buf` and the error is swallowed (comment 625-628). Excellent row\n5. **`jmp_buf_size > 0` but `jb == &jmp_buf_local`** → inconsistent state; the inner `if` at 629 is false so nothing is freed, fields zeroed. Note\n6. always: `jmp_buf_size == 0`, `jmp_buf_ptr == NULL`, `longjmp_fn == 0` afterwards (comment 644). Assert for every arm\n\n### `png_longjmp` — `pngerror.c:671-691` (exported, `PNG_NORETURN`)\n1. `png_ptr != NULL && longjmp_fn != NULL && jmp_buf_ptr != NULL` → `longjmp_fn(*jmp_buf_ptr, val)` (676-678). Rows: `val = 1`, `val = 42`\n2. **`png_ptr == NULL`** → falls to `PNG_ABORT()` (690)\n3. **`longjmp_fn == NULL`** → `PNG_ABORT()`\n4. **`jmp_buf_ptr == NULL`** → `PNG_ABORT()`\n5. custom `longjmp_fn` installed → it is called instead of `longjmp`\nRows 2-4 abort the process; test with a forked child or a custom `PNG_ABORT`.\n\n### `png_error` — `pngerror.c:38-49` (`PNG_ERROR_TEXT_SUPPORTED` is on, so this is the compiled variant; `png_err` at 51-66 is **not** compiled)\n1. `png_ptr != NULL && error_fn != NULL` → the app handler runs first (42-44)\n2. app handler longjmps → `png_default_error` never reached\n3. **app handler returns** → falls through to `png_default_error` (48) which prints and `png_longjmp`s. Documented fallthrough (comment 46-47)\n4. `png_ptr == NULL` → straight to `png_default_error(NULL, msg)` → `fprintf` then `png_longjmp(NULL, 1)` → `PNG_ABORT()`\n5. `error_fn == NULL` → same\n6. `error_message == NULL` → `png_default_error`'s ternary at 662 substitutes `\"undefined\"`. Row\n7. `PNG_CONSOLE_IO_SUPPORTED` is on (`pnglibconf.h:28`) so `png_default_error` writes to stderr (661-664). A build with it off is a second config row\n\n### `png_warning` — `pngerror.c:176-185`\n1. `png_ptr != NULL && warning_fn != NULL` → app handler, then **returns normally**\n2. `warning_fn == NULL` → `png_default_warning` → stderr\n3. `png_ptr == NULL` → `png_default_warning(NULL, msg)` — `png_ptr` is `PNG_UNUSED` there, so this is safe\n4. note the vestigial `int offset = 0` at 179 (`warning_message + offset`), a leftover from `PNG_ERROR_NUMBERS_SUPPORTED`\n5. `warning_message == NULL` → `png_default_warning`'s `fprintf(\"%s\", NULL)` — UB. Hazard note\n\n### `png_chunk_error` — `pngerror.c:425-438` (`PNG_READ_SUPPORTED && PNG_ERROR_TEXT_SUPPORTED` — both on)\n1. `png_ptr == NULL` → `png_error(NULL, msg)` directly (430-431)\n2. valid → `png_format_buffer` then `png_error(msg_with_chunk_prefix)`. Buffer is `char msg[18+196]`\n3. via `png_format_buffer` (`pngerror.c:379-421`), the chunk-name rendering: **alphabetic bytes** → literal; **non-alpha bytes** (`isnonalpha(c)`: `c < 65 || c > 122 || (c > 90 && c < 97)`) → `[XX]` hex form. Rows: `chunk_name = png_IHDR` → `\"IHDR: msg\"`; `chunk_name = 0` → `\"[00][00][00][00]: msg\"`; `chunk_name` with one byte = `'_'` (95, in the 91-96 gap) → `\"[5F]\"`; a byte = `'z'` (122) → literal; a byte = `'{'` (123) → `[7B]`\n4. `error_message == NULL` → `png_format_buffer` writes only the chunk name and a NUL (405-406), no `\": \"` prefix\n5. `error_message` longer than `PNG_MAX_ERROR_TEXT-1` (195) → truncated at 415. Row\n\n### `png_chunk_warning` — `pngerror.c:442-454` (`PNG_WARNINGS_SUPPORTED`)\nSame shape as `png_chunk_error`: `png_ptr == NULL` → plain `png_warning`; otherwise formatted. Same 5 `png_format_buffer` rows.\n\n### `png_benign_error` — `pngerror.c:307-335` (`PNG_BENIGN_ERRORS_SUPPORTED`)\nFour-way dispatch. **No NULL check on `png_ptr`** — dereferences `flags` immediately.\n1. **`PNG_FLAG_BENIGN_ERRORS_WARN` set + read struct + `chunk_name != 0`** → `png_chunk_warning` (315)\n2. **`PNG_FLAG_BENIGN_ERRORS_WARN` set + write struct (or `chunk_name == 0`)** → `png_warning` (318)\n3. **flag clear + read struct + `chunk_name != 0`** → `png_chunk_error` → **fatal**, longjmp (326)\n4. **flag clear + otherwise** → `png_error` → **fatal** (329)\nReachability with valid config:\n- On a **fresh read struct** the flag **is** set (`pngread.c:60`) → arms 1/2 (warning). Rows: before any chunk (`chunk_name == 0`) → arm 2; during chunk processing → arm 1\n- On a **fresh write struct** the flag is **clear** → arms 3/4 (fatal). `mode` has no `PNG_IS_READ_STRUCT` so it's always arm 4\n- After `png_set_benign_errors(png_ptr, 1)` → arms 1/2 on either struct type\n- After `png_set_benign_errors(png_ptr, 0)` → arms 3/4 on either\nSo: **4 arms × 2 struct types × 2 `png_set_benign_errors` values = the full grid; 6 distinct reachable rows.**\n\n### `png_app_warning` — `pngerror.c:337-348` (`PRIVATE`, exported)\n1. `PNG_FLAG_APP_WARNINGS_WARN` set → `png_warning`\n2. flag clear → `png_error` — **fatal**\n**Default reachability in this BETA build:** the flag is **NOT set** on a fresh read struct (because `PNG_RELEASE_BUILD == 0`, `pngread.c:63-65`) and never set on a write struct. So **by default `png_app_warning` is fatal on both.** Only `png_set_benign_errors(png_ptr, 1)` (`pngset.c:1937-1938`, which sets all three flags together) makes it a warning. This is a high-value row — behavior differs between a BETA build and a stable release build.\nRows: default read struct (fatal), default write struct (fatal), after `png_set_benign_errors(p,1)` (warns), after `png_set_benign_errors(p,0)` (fatal).\nCallers: `png_set_shift` (`pngtrans.c:114`), `png_set_filler` (171, 202, 209, 214), `png_set_user_transform_info` (845), `check_location` (`pngset.c:1396`), `png_set_sPLT` (1327), `png_set_unknown_chunk_location` (1540), `png_set_keep_unknown_chunks` (1611, 1665, 1681), `png_chunk_report` (507).\n\n### `png_app_error` — `pngerror.c:350-361`\n1. `PNG_FLAG_APP_ERRORS_WARN` set → `png_warning`\n2. flag clear → `png_error` — **fatal**\nSame default story as `png_app_warning`: the flag is only set by `png_set_benign_errors(p, 1)`. 4 rows.\n\n### `png_chunk_benign_error` — `pngerror.c:459-472` (`PNG_READ_SUPPORTED && PNG_BENIGN_ERRORS_SUPPORTED`)\n1. `PNG_FLAG_BENIGN_ERRORS_WARN` set → `png_chunk_warning`\n2. clear → `png_chunk_error` — fatal\n2 rows × the `png_format_buffer` chunk-name variations.\n\n### `png_chunk_report` — `pngerror.c:476-513` (`PRIVATE`, the write-side dispatcher)\nBoth READ and WRITE are supported, so both `#if` arms compile.\n1. **read struct** (`mode & PNG_IS_READ_STRUCT`) + `error < PNG_CHUNK_ERROR` → `png_chunk_warning`\n2. read struct + `error >= PNG_CHUNK_ERROR` → `png_chunk_benign_error`\n3. **write struct** + `error < PNG_CHUNK_WRITE_ERROR` → `png_app_warning`\n4. write struct + `error >= PNG_CHUNK_WRITE_ERROR` → `png_app_error`\n4 rows × the benign/app flag states. Callers pass `PNG_CHUNK_WRITE_ERROR` throughout `pngset.c` (515, 522, 533, 544, 1000, 1031, 1063, 1092, 1305, 1379, 1468, 1505) — so on a **write** struct these are `png_app_error` → **fatal by default in this build**. High-value row: `png_set_pCAL` with an invalid equation type is fatal, not a warning, unless `png_set_benign_errors(p,1)` was called.\n\n### `png_set_benign_errors` — `pngset.c:1925-1943`\n**No NULL check on `png_ptr`.**\n1. **`allowed != 0`** → sets **all three** flags: `PNG_FLAG_BENIGN_ERRORS_WARN | PNG_FLAG_APP_WARNINGS_WARN | PNG_FLAG_APP_ERRORS_WARN` (1937-1938). Rows: `allowed = 1`, `allowed = 2`, `allowed = -1` (all take this arm)\n2. **`allowed == 0`** → clears all three (1941-1942)\n3. Round-trip: `set(1)` then `set(0)` then `set(1)`\n4. Interaction with the creation defaults: on a fresh read struct only `BENIGN_ERRORS_WARN` is set, so `set(0)` then observing `png_benign_error` become fatal is a real state change; `set(1)` additionally enables the two APP flags that were never on\n5. Observable through: `png_benign_error`, `png_app_warning`, `png_app_error`, `png_chunk_benign_error`, `png_chunk_report`. Recommend one row per observer × `allowed ∈ {0,1}` = 10 rows\n\n### `png_warning_parameter` / `_unsigned` / `_signed` / `png_formatted_warning` — `pngerror.c:192-303`\nNot in your list but reached by `png_check_keyword`; enumerate briefly:\n- `png_warning_parameter` (192-198): `number < 1` → no-op; `number > PNG_WARNING_PARAMETER_COUNT` → no-op; `1..COUNT` → `png_safecat` into `p[number-1]`; over-long string → truncated\n- `png_warning_parameter_signed` (208-227): `value >= 0` → plain; `value < 0` → `u = ~u+1` then `*--str = '-'`; `value == INT32_MIN` → the unsigned negate avoids overflow; `str == buffer` (no room for the minus) → sign dropped\n- `png_formatted_warning` (229-303): `p == NULL` → `@` chars printed literally; `@` at end of string → printed literally (comment 249-251); `@1`..`@9` → substituted; `@0` and `@:` → the digit search runs off the end, `parameter == 9 >= PNG_WARNING_PARAMETER_COUNT` → the `@` and the char are printed literally; message longer than 191 → truncated at `msg[191]`; uninitialized `p[n]` → the `parm < pend` bound at 276 prevents overrun\n\n### `png_fixed_error` — `pngerror.c:517-535` (`PNG_ERROR_TEXT_SUPPORTED && PNG_FLOATING_POINT_SUPPORTED` — both on)\n1. `name == NULL` → message is just `\"fixed point overflow in \"` (the `while` at 527-532 is skipped)\n2. `name` short → concatenated\n3. `name` ≥ 195 chars → truncated at `PNG_MAX_ERROR_TEXT-1`\nAlways ends in `png_error` → fatal. Reached from `png_fixed` (`png.c:2731`) and `png_fixed_ITU` (`png.c:2750`).\n\n### `png_set_strip_error_numbers` — `pngerror.c:749-754`\n`PNG_ERROR_NUMBERS_SUPPORTED` is **not** defined in `pnglibconf.h`, so this function **is not compiled**. No row. (Even when compiled it's a pure no-op body.)\n\n### Simplified-API error plumbing — `pngerror.c:763-848`\nCompiled (`PNG_SIMPLIFIED_READ_SUPPORTED`, `PNG_SIMPLIFIED_WRITE_SUPPORTED` at `pnglibconf.h:119, 123`).\n- `png_safe_error` (763-796): `image == NULL` → `abort()`; `image->opaque == NULL` or `error_buf == NULL` → writes `\"bad longjmp: \"` + message then `abort()`; normal → logs and longjmps\n- `png_safe_warning` (799-811): `warning_or_error == 0` → logs; already set → **discards** the warning (first-wins). Also **no NULL check on `image`**\n- `png_safe_execute` (814-848): `setjmp` returns 0 and `function` returns true → 1; returns false → 0 with `error_buf` restored; longjmp'd into → 0; `saved_error_buf == NULL` (outermost frame) → also calls `png_image_free` (844-845). 4 rows\n\n---\n\n## 11. `pngget.c` / `pngset.c` — full export list\n\n### `pngget.c` — every exported getter, with branch-worthiness\n\n**Trivial \"NULL-check + field read\", return 0/NULL on failure — 2 rows each (both-NULL vs valid):**\n| Function | Line | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `png_get_rowbytes` | 40-46 | returns `info_ptr->rowbytes`, which `png_set_IHDR` computes as `PNG_ROWBYTES(pixel_depth, width)` (`pngset.c:471`). **Worth extra rows** for the packed-depth rounding: `(bd=1,w=1)→1`, `(1,7)→1`, `(1,8)→1`, `(1,9)→2`, `(2,3)→1`, `(4,3)→2`, `(8,3)→3`, `(16,3)→6`, `(ct=6,bd=16,w=3)→24` |\n| `png_get_rows` | 50-56 | `PNG_INFO_IMAGE_SUPPORTED`; returns `row_pointers` (may be NULL) |\n| `png_get_image_width` | 62-68 | `PNG_EASY_ACCESS` |\n| `png_get_image_height` | 71-77 | |\n| `png_get_bit_depth` | 80-86 | |\n| `png_get_color_type` | 89-95 | |\n| `png_get_filter_type` | 98-104 | |\n| `png_get_interlace_type` | 107-113 | |\n| `png_get_compression_type` | 116-122 | |\n| `png_get_channels` | 481-487 | **Worth extra rows**: `png_set_IHDR` computes it at `pngset.c:457-467` — `ct=3→1`, `ct=0→1`, `ct=2→3`, `ct=4→2`, `ct=6→4`. 5 rows |\n| `png_get_signature` | 491-497 | `PNG_READ_SUPPORTED`; returns `info_ptr->signature` |\n| `png_get_rgb_to_gray_status` | 1274-1277 | ternary NULL check, returns 0 |\n| `png_get_user_chunk_ptr` | 1282-1285 | ternary |\n| `png_get_user_width_max` | 1315-1318 | ternary; default 1000000 |\n| `png_get_user_height_max` | 1321-1324 | ternary; default 1000000 |\n| `png_get_chunk_cache_max` | 1328-1331 | ternary; default 1000 |\n| `png_get_chunk_malloc_max` | 1335-1338 | ternary; default 8000000 |\n\n**Non-trivial — distinct rows required:**\n\n1. **`png_get_valid`** — `pngget.c:18-37`. Special case at 29-30: **`flag == PNG_INFO_tRNS && png_ptr->num_trans == 0` → returns 0 even if the `valid` bit is set** (comment 25-28: `png_handle_PLTE` may have canceled a tRNS). Rows: all 22 `PNG_INFO_*` bits from `png.h:730-761` individually; `flag == 0` → 0; `flag == PNG_INFO_tRNS` with `num_trans > 0` → non-zero; `flag == PNG_INFO_tRNS` with `num_trans == 0` and `valid` set → **0**; multi-bit `flag` (e.g. `gAMA|cHRM`) → returns the AND, so partial matches return non-zero. Note `PNG_INFO_acTL/fcTL/fdAT` (0x100000/0x200000/0x400000) are declared but marked \"unknown\".\n\n2. **`png_get_x_pixels_per_meter`** — 124-143. Three-way: NULL/no-pHYs → 0; `phys_unit_type != PNG_RESOLUTION_METER (1)` → **0**; `== METER` → value. Rows: unit=0 (UNKNOWN) → 0; unit=1 → value; unit=2 → 0; `valid` bit clear → 0; value 0 with METER → 0 (indistinguishable from failure — worth noting)\n3. **`png_get_y_pixels_per_meter`** — 145-164. Same 3 branches\n4. **`png_get_pixels_per_meter`** — 166-185. **Four** branches: adds `x_pixels_per_unit == y_pixels_per_unit` (176). Rows: equal+METER → value; **unequal+METER → 0**; equal+non-METER → 0\n5. **`png_get_pixel_aspect_ratio`** (float) — 188-208. Guard `x_pixels_per_unit != 0` (198). Rows: `x=0` → `0.0f`; `x=300, y=300` → 1.0f; `x=300, y=600` → 2.0f; `y=0, x=300` → 0.0f (indistinguishable from failure). Gated on `PNG_READ_pHYs_SUPPORTED` — on\n6. **`png_get_pixel_aspect_ratio_fixed`** — 212-240. Five-part guard at 219-223: `x > 0 && y > 0 && x <= PNG_UINT_31_MAX && y <= PNG_UINT_31_MAX`, then `png_muldiv` which can return 0. Rows: `x=0` → 0; `y=0` → 0; `x = 0x80000000` → 0; `y = 0x80000000` → 0; `x=1, y=100000` → muldiv `100000*100000/1` = 1e10 → **overflow → returns 0**; `x=300, y=300` → 100000; `x=300, y=600` → 200000; `x = PNG_UINT_31_MAX, y = PNG_UINT_31_MAX` → 100000\n7. **`png_get_x_offset_microns`** — 243-261. `offset_unit_type == PNG_OFFSET_MICROMETER (1)` required. Rows: unit=0 → 0; unit=1 → value; negative value with unit=1 → negative returned (it's `png_int_32`); unit=1 with value 0 → 0\n8. **`png_get_y_offset_microns`** — 263-281. Same\n9. **`png_get_x_offset_pixels`** — 283-301. `offset_unit_type == PNG_OFFSET_PIXEL (0)` required — **the opposite condition**. Rows: unit=0 → value; unit=1 → 0. Cross-row with #7: for a given info only one of `_microns`/`_pixels` can return non-zero\n10. **`png_get_y_offset_pixels`** — 303-321. Same\n11. **`ppi_from_ppm`** (static) — 324-354. The `#if 0` bit-twiddle version at 327-341 is dead; the live path is `ppm <= PNG_UINT_31_MAX && png_muldiv(&result, ppm, 127, 5000)`. Rows: `ppm = 0` → 0; `ppm = 5000` → 127; `ppm = 2835` → 72 (72 dpi); `ppm = 3937` → 100; `ppm = PNG_UINT_31_MAX` → `2147483647*127/5000` ≈ 5.45e7, in range → OK; `ppm = 0x80000000` → **fails the `<=` → returns 0**; rounding: `ppm = 1` → `floor(127/5000+0.5)` = 0\n12. **`png_get_pixels_per_inch`** — 356-360 = `ppi_from_ppm(png_get_pixels_per_meter(...))`. Inherits all of #4 + #11. Rows: unequal x/y → 0; equal → converted\n13. **`png_get_x_pixels_per_inch`** — 362-366 = `ppi_from_ppm(png_get_x_pixels_per_meter(...))`\n14. **`png_get_y_pixels_per_inch`** — 368-372\n15. **`png_fixed_inches_from_microns`** (static) — 375-390. `png_muldiv(&result, microns, 500, 127)`; on failure emits **`png_warning(\"fixed point overflow ignored\")`** and returns 0. Rows: `microns = 0` → 0; `microns = 127` → 500; `microns = 25400` → 100000 (1 inch); `microns = INT32_MAX` → `2147483647*500/127` ≈ 8.45e9 → **overflow → warning + 0**; `microns = -25400` → -100000; `microns = INT32_MIN` → overflow → warning + 0. The warning is the observable side effect — distinct row\n16. **`png_get_x_offset_inches_fixed`** — 392-398. Composition of #7 and #15. Row: oFFs in PIXEL units → inner returns 0 → outer returns 0 with **no warning**\n17. **`png_get_y_offset_inches_fixed`** — 402-408\n18. **`png_get_x_offset_inches`** (float) — 412-419. `(float)(microns * .00003937)` — **no overflow check**, direct float multiply (comment 415-417). Rows: 0 → 0.0f; 25400 → ≈1.0f; `INT32_MAX` → ≈84557.6f (no warning, unlike #15). Contrast row with `_fixed`\n19. **`png_get_y_offset_inches`** — 422-430\n20. **`png_get_pHYs_dpi`** — 434-471 (**inside `PNG_INCH_CONVERSIONS_SUPPORTED`**, note this is a *different* function from `png_get_pHYs`). The `retval` accumulates `PNG_INFO_pHYs` per non-NULL out-param, and the `unit_type == 1` branch at 462-466 **rewrites `*res_x`/`*res_y` by `*.0254 + .50`**. Rows: all three NULL → returns **0** even with valid pHYs; only `res_x` → returns `PNG_INFO_pHYs`, `res_y` untouched; only `unit_type` → returns flag; all three with `unit_type == 0` → raw values; all three with `unit_type == 1` → **values converted to per-inch**; `unit_type` non-NULL but `res_x`/`res_y` NULL with `unit_type == 1` → the inner NULL guards prevent the rewrite; `valid` bit clear → 0 and nothing written. Note the naming trap: this returns *converted* values while `png_get_pHYs` does not\n21. **`png_get_bKGD`** — 501-514. Requires `background != NULL` **and** the valid bit → `*background = &info_ptr->background` (an **interior pointer**, aliasing hazard) and returns `PNG_INFO_bKGD`. Rows: valid+non-NULL → flag; `background == NULL` → **0**; valid bit clear → 0\n22. **`png_get_cHRM`** (float) — 525-556. **Each of the 8 out-params independently NULL-checked** (536-551) via the `png_float` macro (`pngpriv.h:797` = `.00001 * fixed`, no branch). Rows: all 8 non-NULL; all 8 NULL → still returns `PNG_INFO_cHRM` with nothing written (contrast with `png_get_bKGD`); one at a time (8 rows); valid clear → 0\n23. **`png_get_cHRM_XYZ`** (float) — 558-593. Extra condition: **`png_XYZ_from_xy(&XYZ, &info_ptr->cHRM) == 0`** (569). Rows: valid cHRM that converts → flag + 9 values; **valid cHRM that fails to convert (e.g. `whitey < 5`) → returns 0**; each of the 9 out-params individually NULL. This is where all of §3's `png_XYZ_from_xy` failure classes become observable through the public API — highest-value row set in this area\n24. **`png_get_cHRM_XYZ_fixed`** — 597-625. Same as #23 but no `png_float`\n25. **`png_get_cHRM_fixed`** — 627-651. Same as #22 but no `png_float`\n26. **`png_get_gAMA_fixed`** — 657-672. Rows: valid → flag + value; `file_gamma == NULL` → flag, nothing written; **valid bit clear → 0** (the \"PNGv3 compatibility: only report gAMA if it is really present\" comment at 663)\n27. **`png_get_gAMA`** (float) — 676-693. Same + `png_float`\n28. **`png_get_sRGB`** — 698-713. Rows: valid → flag + intent; NULL out-param → flag; invalid → 0. Note `rendering_intent` is stored unvalidated by `png_set_sRGB`, so out-of-range intents (>3) round-trip\n29. **`png_get_iCCP`** — 717-740. **Requires `name`, `profile`, `proflen` all non-NULL** (726) — unlike most getters. `compression_type` is optional. **`*proflen = png_get_uint_32(info_ptr->iccp_profile)`** at 730 — it reads the length **out of the profile data**, not from `info_ptr->iccp_proflen`! Rows: all four non-NULL → flag; any of the three required NULL → **0**; `compression_type == NULL` → flag; assert `*proflen` equals the big-endian uint32 at profile[0..3] and **compare against `iccp_proflen`** — if `png_set_iCCP` was given a profile whose embedded length disagrees with `proflen`, the getter reports the embedded one. Genuine discrepancy row\n30. **`png_get_sPLT`** — 744-757. Returns `splt_palettes_num` (an `int`, **not** a `PNG_INFO_*` flag) and does **not** check the valid bit. Rows: `spalettes == NULL` → 0; no sPLT → `*spalettes = NULL`, returns 0; 1 palette → returns 1; 3 palettes → returns 3\n31. **`png_get_cICP`** — 761-782. **All four out-params required non-NULL** (771-772). Rows: all four → flag; any one NULL → 0; valid clear → 0\n32. **`png_get_cLLI_fixed`** — 787-802. Independent NULL checks; returns the raw ×10000 values. Rows: both → flag; each individually NULL; valid clear → 0\n33. **`png_get_cLLI`** (float) — 807-822. Multiplies by `.0001` (**not** `.00001` — the ITU scaling). Row asserting the 1e-4 scale explicitly\n34. **`png_get_mDCV_fixed`** — 828-855. **Multiplies each chromaticity by 2** (843-850) to undo the `/2` in `png_ITU_fixed_16` (`pngset.c:214`). 10 independent NULL checks. Rows: all 10; each individually; **round-trip through `png_set_mDCV_fixed` → assert the value comes back as `(input/2)*2`, i.e. odd inputs lose their low bit**. High-value lossy-round-trip row\n35. **`png_get_mDCV`** (float) — 859-887. Chromaticities × `.00002` (= 2 × 1e-5), light levels × `.0001`. Row asserting the two different scale factors\n36. **`png_get_eXIf`** — 892-899. **Stub**: emits `png_warning(\"png_get_eXIf does not work; use png_get_eXIf_1\")` and returns 0 always. One row asserting the warning + 0\n37. **`png_get_eXIf_1`** — 902-918. Rows: valid → flag + `num_exif` + pointer; out-params NULL; valid clear → 0\n38. **`png_get_hIST`** — 921-934. Rows: valid + non-NULL `hist` → flag; `hist == NULL` → 0; valid clear → 0\n39. **`png_get_IHDR`** — 937-979. **Unusual**: returns **1** (not a `PNG_INFO_*` flag) and **calls `png_check_IHDR` at 974-976**. Rows: all 7 out-params non-NULL → 1; all NULL → still **1** (nothing written); each individually; `png_ptr == NULL` → 0; `info_ptr == NULL` → 0; **info holding invalid IHDR data (e.g. `bit_depth = 3` poked directly, or set via a `png_set_IHDR` whose error was caught) → the getter raises `png_error(\"Invalid IHDR data\")`**. That last one is the standout row. Note the out-params are written **before** the validation, so a longjmp leaves them partially filled\n40. **`png_get_oFFs`** — 982-999. **All three out-params required non-NULL** (990). Rows: all three → flag; any NULL → 0; valid clear → 0; negative offsets\n41. **`png_get_pCAL`** — 1003-1026. **All seven out-params required non-NULL** (1012-1013). Rows: all seven → flag; each of the 7 individually NULL → 0 (7 rows); valid clear → 0; `nparams == 0` → `*params` is the 1-element NULL-terminated array\n42. **`png_get_sCAL_fixed`** — 1033-1054. Guarded by `PNG_FIXED_POINT && (PNG_FLOATING_ARITHMETIC || PNG_FLOATING_POINT)` (1031-1032) — compiled here. Uses **`atof` then `png_fixed`** (1047-1049), so an sCAL value out of fixed-point range → `png_fixed_error` → **`png_error`**. **No NULL checks on `unit`/`width`/`height`** — unconditional writes. Rows: valid small values → flag; valid value like `\"1e30\"` → `png_fixed` overflow → **png_error**; valid clear → 0 (nothing written). The overflow row is high-value\n43. **`png_get_sCAL`** (float) — 1058-1074. `atof` only, no `png_fixed` → **no overflow error**. Rows: `\"1e30\"` → returns `1e30` cleanly. Contrast row with #42. Also **no NULL checks** on the out-params\n44. **`png_get_sCAL_s`** — 1076-1092. Returns the raw strings. **No NULL checks**. Rows: valid → flag + pointers into `info_ptr`; valid clear → 0\n45. **`png_get_pHYs`** — 1096-1127. `retval` accumulator like #20 but **no unit conversion**. Rows: all three NULL → **0**; each individually → flag; valid clear → 0. Contrast row against `png_get_pHYs_dpi`\n46. **`png_get_P